HT_DATA.load("detail", {"fNk_zzaMoSs":{"wy":"HN's go-to recommendation for learning linear algebra intuition, repeatedly credited with making concepts click and complementing textbooks.","q":{"x":"the most welcoming and informative introduction to the topic I've ever seen","i":18679785,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":48946645,"author":"zvmaz","month":"2026-07","story":"Immersive Linear Algebra Book with Interactive Figures","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gave intuitive understanding of linear algebra after a confusing first course","quote":"3Blue1Brown[1] has opened my eyes on the intuitive understanding of linear algebra","text":"3Blue1Brown[1] has opened my eyes on the intuitive understanding of linear algebra, many, many years after taking a first course in linear algebra, which left me completely mystified. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45800005,"author":"seanhunter","month":"2025-11","story":"An Illustrated Introduction to Linear Algebra, Chapter 2: The Dot Product","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visual intuition-building series on linear algebra, far better than the blog post","quote":"It has fantastic visualisations for building intuition and in general is wildly superior to this","text":"If you actually want to learn linear algebra, don't use this blogpost. It's real weaksauce compared to the wealth of free information and resources available online. Firstly, the real illustrated guide to linear algebra is the youtube series \"The Essence of linear algebra\" by 3blue1brown[1]. It has fantastic visualisations for building intuition and in general is wildly superior to this, which seems fine but extremely superficial. If you're done with 3b1b and want to take things further, then the go-to is the excellent 18.06SC course by the late and legendary Gilbert Strang. It's amazing, it's free. [2] Still want more? OK now you're talking my language. If you are serious about linear algebra (Up to graduate level, after that you need something else) then you want the book \"Linear Algebra Done Right\" by Sheldon Axler. It's available for free from the author's website[3] and he has made a bunch of videos to supplement the book. There's also an RTD Math full lecture series[4] that follows the book and he explains each thing in a lot of detail (because Axler goes fast , so it's beneficial to unpack the concepts a bit sometimes). [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] and [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":42108671,"author":"traes","month":"2024-11","story":"Adventures in Probability","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Really intuitive graphical explanations of linear algebra concepts","quote":"The entire 3blue1brown series[0] on linear algebra is well worth watching","text":"The entire 3blue1brown series[0] on linear algebra is well worth watching, it has really intuitive graphical explanations of a bunch of concepts. Here's the one on determinants in particular[1]. TL;DW the determinant represents how much you scale the area/volume/hypervolume (depending on dimension) of a shape by applying a matrix transformation to each point. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":41417101,"author":"zvmaz","month":"2024-09","story":"Linear Algebra Done Right [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gave intuition of what linear algebra is about","quote":"It is the Youtube channel 3Blue1Brown[1] that gave me an intuition of what linear algebra was about","text":"Linear Algebra Done right did not help me try to demystify linear algebra. Quite the contrary. It is the Youtube channel 3Blue1Brown[1] that gave me an intuition of what linear algebra was about, and I am forever thankful to him. Watch it and if you are a teacher, don't be a smug \"formalist\". [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38951710,"author":"drfunk","month":"2024-01","story":"How do I relearn mathematics for machine learning?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visualizations that made things click, a perfect companion to textbooks","quote":"3blue1brown's visualizations made a lot of things click for me","text":"3blue1brown's visualizations made a lot of things click for me, years after learning algebra and calculus. In my opinion they're the perfect companion to a more formal study with textbooks. Playlist for linear algebra: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31707568,"author":"aidos","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Best beginner friendly linear algebra book?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gave intuition in hours that university years did not","quote":"Essence of linear algebra is an absolutely wonderful series.","text":"Essence of linear algebra is an absolutely wonderful series. It gave me an intuition of the subject in a matter of hours in way years of university didn’t do. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30874816,"author":"laplacesdemon48","month":"2022-04","story":"An Intuitive Guide to Linear Algebra","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A few hours saves dozens when studying a textbook","quote":"Spending a few hours on this series will easily save you dozens of hours when going through a comprehensive LA textbook.","text":"Here's another vote for 3Blue1Brown's series on linear algebra [1]. Spending a few hours on this series will easily save you dozens of hours when going through a comprehensive LA textbook. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29064413,"author":"truly","month":"2021-11","story":"SoME1 results","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Nicely made math explainers, linear algebra videos especially worth it","quote":"The videos on linear algebra in particular are worth it, especially if you do any sort of machine learning","text":"For anyone wondering what 3Blue1Brown is, it's a top Youtube channel with very nicely made explanatory videos for math. The videos on linear algebra in particular are worth it, especially if you do any sort of machine learning: [VIDEO] . The author of the channel is Grant Sanderson. He has recently given a talk at SIGGRAPH 2021: [LINK] . As part of developing his Youtube channel, he has written and open-sourced manim, a library for programatically generating animations written in Python: [LINK] ."},{"id":24863375,"author":"dr_zoidberg","month":"2020-10","story":"A Programmer’s Intuition for Matrix Multiplication","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Quick intuitions, graphics helpful early on","quote":"I strongly recommend 3blue1brown's intro to linear algebra[0] for quick intuitions","text":"I strongly recommend 3blue1brown's intro to linear algebra[0] for quick intuitions, followed by Gilbert Strangs MIT Open Course[1][2] for (much) deeper detail. Strang is a genius, but Sanderson's graphics and intuitions can be helpful early on. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":22416992,"author":"vecter","month":"2020-02","story":"An Intuitive Guide to Linear Algebra (2012)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Most intuitive linear algebra treatment, via linear transformations","quote":"nothing is as intuitive as 3B1B's series on YouTube","text":"This is ok but nothing is as intuitive as 3B1B's series on YouTube that has been posted hundreds of times on HN [0]. Linear algebra is really about linear transformations of vector spaces, which is not captured in this blog post. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19350539,"author":"daeken","month":"2019-03","story":"An Intuitive Derivation of Eigenvectors","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Perfect visual intuition for linear algebra including eigenvectors","quote":"3blue1brown's playlist on the subject is just ... perfect.","text":"If you want to get an intuitive, visual understanding of linear algebra -- including eigenvectors/eigenvalues -- 3blue1brown's playlist on the subject is just ... perfect. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19267665,"author":"aidos","month":"2019-02","story":"Immersive Linear Algebra (2016)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"The go-to series for learning linear algebra","quote":"is the go to place for learning this material","text":"3Blue1Brown's Essence of linear algebra series is the go to place for learning this material. I've included the link to the series below so people don't get lost in all his other wonderful videos along the way. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18841427,"author":"PinkMilkshake","month":"2019-01","story":"How to Start Learning Computer Graphics Programming","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear explanations applying linear algebra to graphics","quote":"His explanations are so clear that by the 2nd or 3rd video you'll already understand how it applies to computer graphics.","text":"3Blue1Brown has a great series on Linear Algebra. His explanations are so clear that by the 2nd or 3rd video you'll already understand how it applies to computer graphics. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18679785,"author":"nicebill8","month":"2018-12","story":"Introduction to Applied Linear Algebra: Vectors, Matrices, and Least Squares","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Most welcoming and informative intro to linear algebra","quote":"the most welcoming and informative introduction to the topic I've ever seen","text":"The 3B1B series on Linear Algebra is by far the most welcoming and informative introduction to the topic I've ever seen: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33176535,"author":"gwgundersen","month":"2022-10","story":"Seven sins of numerical linear algebra","stance":"rec","why":"Recommended for intuition among several resources","quote":"For intuition","text":"There are many good resources! A few, depending on your inclination: - For intuition: [VIDEO] - For rigor: [LINK] - For code: [LINK] - For numerical/algorithmic details: [LINK]"},{"id":24828594,"author":"jlos","month":"2020-10","story":"Euler's Formula: A Complete Guide","stance":"rec","why":"Intuition behind linear algebra and calculus","quote":"Those 2 video's will do a good job of giving you the 'intuition' behind Calculus and Linear Algebra.","text":"To answer your main question - \"what level of maths\" do you need is more about what topics: a couple introductory classes in Calculus, Linear Algebra, and Discrete Mathematics will make that sentence understandable. I'll give some definitions and resources, but think about how you would explain multiplication/division/algebra to a kid. At some point, you just have to work through some problems and the math just makes sense. So I'll explain the terms above, but best bet is to just take a few courses on the topics I mentioned above. Quick definitions: - Exponential: is the constant e to the power of some number. e, like pi, is a ubitiqous constant in mathematics and nature. You run into pi when doing geometry, and e when doing calculus. - A function in mathematics is similar to a function in programming, but not exactly. A course in Discrete Mathematics helps here. - F-apostrophe is notation used in calculus to show the relation of one type of function, called a derivative to another. f and f' are related. How, though, is better explained by taking calculus. - An eigenfunction exists in a system of equations. Think of it like a 'balance' point. Linear Algebra will help make sense of this term. Resources - Essence of Calculus: [LINK] - Essence of Linear Algebra: [VIDEO] Those 2 video's will do a good job of giving you the 'intuition' behind Calculus and Linear Algebra. Like programming, though, you just have to actually work some problems out by hand for the stuff to sink in. Fo"},{"id":23655193,"author":"chongli","month":"2020-06","story":"Numerical Linear Algebra for Programmers","stance":"rec","why":"Intuitive geometric interpretation of vectors, bases, eigenvalues","quote":"It will give you an intuitive, geometric interpretation for concepts such as a vector, a basis, an eigenvalue, a linear transformation","text":"Maybe you would get something out of 3blue1brown's series on linear algebra [1]. It will give you an intuitive, geometric interpretation for concepts such as a vector, a basis, an eigenvalue, a linear transformation, etc. As for why you would want to know these things, well, linear algebra is finding applications all over the place these days. Everything from computer graphics to machine learning is jam-packed with linear algebra. And I'm not just talking about basic concepts such as matrix-vector multiplication. The singular value decomposition has applications in discrete optimization, image compression, the PageRank algorithm [2], computer vision [3], and machine learning [2] [4]. Having said that, you may find it very difficult to understand something like SVD without a firm grounding in the topic of vector spaces, linear transformations, spanning, linear (in)dependence, subspaces, eigenvalues, eigenvectors, diagonalization, and determinants. This is why SVD is one of the last things you learn in a linear algebra course (indeed, it's not covered until lecture 29 of Gilbert Strang's course). Ultimately, it all depends on how relevant these things are to you. I would assume (hope) that because you clicked on this HN discussion that you're interested in learning linear algebra because you think it might be useful to you. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":22106014,"author":"edanm","month":"2020-01","story":"My story as a self-taught AI researcher","stance":"rec","why":"Builds intuition of what linear algebra is and is for","quote":"I think it's exactly that kind of \"intuition of what Linear algebra is and is for\".","text":"If you haven't seen it, it might be worth checking out 3Blue1Brown's Linear Algebra YouTube series ( [VIDEO] ). I think it's exactly that kind of \"intuition of what Linear algebra is and is for\"."},{"id":17754724,"author":"mindcrime","month":"2018-08","story":"Organized Resources for Deep Learning Researchers and Developers","stance":"rec","why":"Amazing visual Essence of Linear Algebra series","quote":"the amazing 3blue1brown \"Essence of Linear Algebra\" series","text":"If you haven't already studied Linear Algebra, and want to get a headstart on that, check out the \"Coding The Matrix\" book/videos from Brown. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Also, see the Gilbert Strang video series on Linear Algebra: [LINK] and the amazing 3blue1brown \"Essence of Linear Algebra\" series: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23152424,"author":"skywal_l","month":"2020-05","story":"A 2020 Vision of Linear Algebra","stance":"reference","why":"Series noted but said to only brush the surface","quote":"You still have 3blue1brown[1] series of video, but it just brush off the surface.","text":"Frankly, couple with this book, it does hardly get better. You still have 3blue1brown[1] series of video, but it just brush off the surface. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lY8Ol2n4o4A",1],["495nCzxM9PI",1],["V74AxCqOTvg",1],["ShEez0JkOFw",1],["q7K0vRUKXKc",1],["LNjJTgXujno",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["aB8zcAttP1E",1],["1e8VZlPBx_0",1],["rJjKP8vYjpQ",1],["id1WShzzMCQ",1],["ZTidn2dBYbY",1],["3wMKoSRbGVs",1],["kya_LXa_y1E",1],["lG2dXobAXLI",1],["NPOHf20slZg",1],["Ip3X9LOh2dk",1],["ue-1JoJQaEg",1],["DRq2NgeFcO0",1],["oKg1hTOQXoY",1]]},"NNnIGh9g6fA":{"wy":"One of the most consistently recommended lecture series on HN, praised as fascinating, funny and eye-opening about human behavior across many scales.","q":{"x":"Best course I've ever done.","i":15813123,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":35693556,"author":"culi","month":"2023-04","story":"Harvard: The Effects of Noise on Health","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sapolsky's course on human behavior biology","quote":"I also highly recommend Sapolsky's Human Behavior Biology course which he taught for Stanford but published on YouTube","text":"for anyone unaware, this is a book by Stanford professor Robert Sapolsky: [LINK] The book is about stress and the impact of glucocorticoids on the body. The chemical stress response used in \"the wild\" by animals is actually the same as the stress response we have when worrying about losing our job, not being able to complete a paper for school, dealing with poverty, etc. It's a partial explanation for why modern humans have so many chronic diseases that are rarely seen in other animals (or even archeological/anthropological evidence of non-industrial societies but he doesn't really go there much in the book) I also highly recommend Sapolsky's Human Behavior Biology course which he taught for Stanford but published on YouTube: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34604140,"author":"nomilk","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What's the best lecture series you've ever seen?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gripping, important lecture series","quote":"A rare combination of both extremely important and extremely interesting.","text":"Bob Sapolsky's Stanford Biology lecture series is... amazing. A rare combination of both extremely important and extremely interesting. If you watch just 3 or 4 minutes, you'll struggle to stop watching. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19379246,"author":"jmcqk6","month":"2019-03","story":"Online activists are silencing us, scientists say","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Stanford lectures that taught the commenter a lot","quote":"I would highly recommend Robert Sapolsky's course \"Human Behavioral Biology\" from stanford.","text":"If people are interested in exploring this topic, I would highly recommend Robert Sapolsky's course \"Human Behavioral Biology\" from stanford. The lectures are all available on YouTube and I learned so much from them: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15813123,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2017-11","story":"Ask HN: Best books you read in 2017?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Accessible multi-scale course from evolution to brains and genes","quote":"Best course I've ever done.","text":"Try Sapolsky's incredible 25-or-so-part online lectures on Biology of Human Behavior. A great lecturer on a most fascinating subject, it's accessible to a general audience. Covers the topic on all different levels, scales, approaches, from evolutionary psychology to the minutiae of brains and genes, with a lot of stories about the discoveries and researchers involved, and jokes, he's a funny guy. Best course I've ever done. Part 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11680653,"author":"Frozenlock","month":"2016-05","story":"The Real Problem with Facebook and the News","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how susceptible we are to hormones; eye-opening","quote":"I would recommend the complete playlist to anyone here. It is simply fascinating.","text":"I like that you accept to evaluate someone else's position before instantly putting them into the little \"bigot\" box. It's much better to leave traces of your thinking process, instead of simply declaring that your conclusions are the only ones that are acceptable and others are idiots. Let's take me for example. It just so happens that I'm rethinking my position on interracial marriage (yes, really). I haven't reached any solid conclusion yet, but I'm not as sure of my position as I once was. My current uncertainty is based on a few things I learned in the past few years. First, Sapolky's excellent \"Human Behavioral Biology\" did show me how incredibly susceptible we are to hormones; how a tiny little change can have huge impacts in our daily life. The part on delaying gratification was also eye opening for me. Some people are wired in a way that makes it very difficult for them to refuse a cookie now in exchange for two cookies later. So it's not necessarily that you are making a greater effort; you might be born in a way that makes it easier for you for delay gratification. I would recommend the complete playlist to anyone here. It is simply fascinating. [VIDEO] Second, the psychology professor Jordan B Peterson ( [LINK] ) teaches how an amazing predictor of success IQ is. You can see a little of what he has to say here: [LINK] Then I learned how IQ is highly correlated to genetics (from 0.75 to 0.8). By the way, for you parents, this means that you might not be as good tut"},{"id":11303394,"author":"guzmanovich","month":"2016-03","story":"Ask HN: What's the most useful online course you have watched?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fascinating, funny lectures on many topics","quote":"Fascinating, funny and gives insights into many different topics like cultural differences and nationalism.","text":"Robert Sapolsky`s lectures on human behavioral biology. Fascinating, funny and gives insights into many different topics like cultural differences and nationalism. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9754896,"author":"ploomans","month":"2015-06","story":"Robert Sapolsky talks turbulence – teens, stress, and the information age","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sapolsky's lecture series on biology of behavior","quote":"For me one of the best teachers you will find online","text":"BTW, you should watch his lecture series on the biology of behaviour: [VIDEO] For me one of the best teachers you will find online"},{"id":4027137,"author":"super_mario","month":"2012-05","story":"Iterated prisoner's dilemma contains evolutionary opponent dominating strategies","stance":"strong_rec","why":"25-hour Stanford course on behavioral biology","quote":"this 25 hour video course is also a must see for pretty much everyone","text":"That's well known if you ever took evolutionary biology course, or game theory course. Even popular books like Richard Dawkins' \"The Selfish Gene\" talk in some detail about it, and this 25 hour video course is also a must see for pretty much everyone: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41368289,"author":"philipov","month":"2024-08","story":"Intelligence is not like height","stance":"rec","why":"Explains misleading heritability statistics and twin study confounders","quote":"Anyone who wants to look at heritability numbers should make sure to watch these lectures first.","text":"Anyone who wants to look at heritability numbers should make sure to watch these lectures first. Heritability is often a misleading stat, because it has an unintuitive definition. Sapolskiy covers common failings of heritability numbers, as well as confounding factors for twin studies. [LINK] [LINK] Full Lectures: [VIDEO] An example of how non-intuitive heritability is: whether a person wears earrings or not would be a heritable trait if we're looking at mid-20th century America, because the variability is entirely explained by whether you're male or female. Heritability is unable to identify whether a trait is caused by genetics, only whether the variation correlates with genetics."},{"id":35244510,"author":"zer00eyz","month":"2023-03","story":"Psychedelic brew ayahuasca’s profound impact revealed in brain scans","stance":"rec","why":"Biology of behavior and aggression","quote":"Robert Sapolsky has a Stanford lecture series on this","text":"> - I got more confrontational which almost got me into fights, but also got me laid These systems are likely deeply tangled up in your biology. Robert Sapolsky has a Stanford lecture series on this: [VIDEO] Well worth a watch!"},{"id":27728563,"author":"eastendguy","month":"2021-07","story":"Dear Mom and Dad, please stick with me (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"High-quality lecture series on related topic","quote":"High-quality lecture series around the same topic (Human Behavioral Biology)","text":"This blog post is low quality marketing/SEO copy and paste. But the topic itself is very interesting. High-quality lecture series around the same topic (Human Behavioral Biology) [VIDEO]"},{"id":21254488,"author":"i2shar","month":"2019-10","story":"What men eat and drink may affect their babies’ health","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how environment affects epigenetics mechanistically","quote":"the initial few lectures from Robert Sapolsky's lecture series[1] were an eyeopener for me.","text":"Also, the initial few lectures from Robert Sapolsky's lecture series[1] were an eyeopener for me. The exact mechanism of how environment affects epigenetics (transcription factors, etc.) was really fascinating! [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20932045,"author":"mrdoops","month":"2019-09","story":"Ask HN: How do you keep your programming motivation up?","stance":"rec","why":"Biology perspective on how humans operate","quote":"Sapolsky's lectures are great for getting a Biology perspective to how human's operate.","text":"Trial and error as a programmer dealing with depression and anxiety accounts for a lot, but for resources I'd point you to Robert Sapolsky's lectures on Behavioral Biology (here: [VIDEO] ). Sapolsky's lectures are great for getting a Biology perspective to how human's operate. Jordan Peterson's has lectures on Personality (here: [LINK] ) which are also great and echo similar ideas from a Psychology perspective."},{"id":10106236,"author":"daodedickinson","month":"2015-08","story":"Study of Holocaust survivors finds trauma passed on to children's genes","stance":"rec","why":"Sapolsky explains environment effects on genes, including Lamarckian evolution","quote":"Dr. Sapolsky, in this lecture series, explains, for just one example, how and why Lamarckian evolution sometimes occurs","text":"There are definitely known links between psychology and genes; we know the epigenetics behind why if mothers are stressed when pregnant, their children will be more stressed for their entire lives. Far too many people still have the Jurassic Park view of DNA and believe in Central Dogma—but it has become clear that environment and all sorts of post-conception events effect genetics. Dr. Sapolsky, in this lecture series, explains, for just one example, how and why Lamarckian evolution sometimes occurs: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4147582,"author":"ehsanu1","month":"2012-06","story":"What Darwin's Theory of Evolution Reveals About Artificial Intelligence","stance":"rec","why":"Course explaining how evolution works at all levels","quote":"Here's a 25 lecture course from Stanford that explains just that","text":"This is a common fallacy about evolution, and is explained beautifully by the anthropic principle, or in other words, the innate selection bias of our existence. I'm a little confused about how the anthropic principle explains this fallacy (and it is a fallacy to be sure). I guess you're referring to how natural selection (and all the other sorts of selection that occur regularly) lead to the existence of \"appropriate\" organisms and features. But I think knowing some of the actual details in how evolution works at all the different levels (molecular bio, epigenetics, competition/cooperation, behavior, etc, etc, etc) serves as an even better explanation. Here's a 25 lecture course from Stanford that explains just that: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48237553,"author":"gobdovan","month":"2026-05","story":"The Spread of Christianity Animated","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It was recommended by Robert Sapolsky in his Introduction to Human Behavioral Biology lecture series","text":"It's even more amazing when you think about Christianity not as a clear category, but as a cloud of practices, beliefs and institutions in a broader family of religious patterns. Mircea Eliade asks how Christianity reinterpreted sacred history, myth, salvation. What does Christianity do with motifs older than itself, such as paradise, rebirth, sacrifice? In A History of Religious Ideas [0], he treats the emergence and development of Christianity, including Judaism, early Christianity, Gnosticism, late antiquity, medieval religious forms and also how it interacted with other traditions. I think it complements quite nicely the geographical spread of Christianity by also clarifying what kind of transformations of religious symbols make it recognisable as Christianity across such different contexts. There's also \"Darwin's Cathedral\" [1] that analyses religion as group-organizing system, with a focus on Calvinism. Didn't go through it, but seems relevant. It was recommended by Robert Sapolsky in his Introduction to Human Behavioral Biology lecture series [2]. [0] A History of Religious Ideas - Mircea Eliade [1] Darwin's Cathedral - David Sloan Wilson [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33852036,"author":"sitkack","month":"2022-12","story":"Zebras Hate You for No Reason: Why Amdahl's Law Is Misleading in a World of Cats","stance":"reference","why":"Opening lecture explaining course premise and avoiding categorical thinking","quote":"explains the basic premise of the course and how he aims to avoid categorical thinking.","text":"> Stanford professor Robert Sapolsky gave the opening lecture of the course entitled Human Behavioral Biology and explains the basic premise of the course and how he aims to avoid categorical thinking. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33189069,"author":"belorn","month":"2022-10","story":"“Women are wonderful” effect","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Question like those is why researchers goes to animal observations. Easier to make conclusions without introducing too much cultural assumptions. In the jungle people find a dead young female baboon. She is apparently dead from starvation, so we hastily conclude it was natural causes. The researcher however who observed the flock gives a different explanation. The young female was healthy just a few weeks ago, but after joining the flock she got continuously bitten and scratched by other females whenever she went to eat or tried to sleep. After weeks of constant stress, lack of sleep and food she died. In contrast we might find a dead young male baboon clearly beaten to death. They joined the same flock recently and after a large fight over dominance the young male acquired injuries and died. Which of the two cases displayed more capacity for violence, brutality or aggressiveness? Is a brawl better or worse than cold calculated murder? > why did we come to have a culture that fears men Very good question. One likely answer is that we have a culture that reward men who commit violence in the right context. Why do men get rewarded for violence? There are many contradicting theories and books on that subject. Are humans inherently violent or peaceful? Is society holding back the savage beast or is cooperation the human default behavior? A lot of questions, a lot of research, a lot of theories. If you want to dig down into those question there are good books and other sources on "},{"id":30140617,"author":"colechristensen","month":"2022-01","story":"Reducing inequality can reverse political polarisation: study","stance":"reference","why":"Sapolsky as a good source on stress and inequality","quote":"Good arguments are resources by Robert Sapolsky","text":"Many studies show chemical markers of these things, it's not just psychological behavioral studies (which are often quite dubious) but easily measurable outcomes. If you're thinking quite narrowly, your point seems reasonable, it's probably below significance to say your neighbor with the bigger house and nicer car is \"healthier\", but this is a lot different than them vs the family that sometimes skips meals because they can't afford to put food on the table with the knowledge that a lost job will mean homelessness. Good arguments are resources by Robert Sapolsky [LINK] or [VIDEO] or any of the books he has published (many have significantly overlapping content)"},{"id":24258061,"author":"totetsu","month":"2020-08","story":"Patrick Collison's Questions","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Robert Sapolsky - Introduction to Human Behavioral Biology","text":"on \"What's the right way to understand and model personality?\" Personality is an outdated concept, personality types triply so. There is no fundamental stability of personality, just behavior context. Christian Miller - The character gap [LINK] [LINK] Robert Sapolsky - Introduction to Human Behavioral Biology [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["040ejWnFkj0",1],["S0dSm_ClcSw",1],["wVp5koAOu9M",1],["86gyW0vUmVs",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["ZD_3_gsgsnk",1],["SIOQgY1tqrU",1],["kYYJlNbV1OM",1],["sfGtw2C95Ms",1],["bJczLlwp-d8",1],["Y0Oa4Lp5fLE",1],["9crggox5rbc",1],["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["G1r55efei5c",1],["AD4b-52jtos",1],["NP9AIUT9nos",1],["ynbgMKclWWc",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["e3msMuwqp-o",1]]},"jvPPXbo87ds":{"wy":"It is the go-to recommendation for anyone who wants to genuinely understand Bezier curves and splines, and commenters repeatedly call it one of the best programming-related educational videos.","q":{"x":"is (IMHO) one of the best educational videos on a programming topic, full stop.","i":39544412,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":44889077,"author":"viggity","month":"2025-08","story":"Bezier-rs – algorithms for Bézier segments and shapes","stance":"strong_rec","why":"73-minute incredible video on Beziers and splines.","quote":"these absolutely incredible videos from Freya Holmér","text":"Anytime béziers are mentioned on HN, I feel compelled to share these absolutely incredible videos from Freya Holmér [VIDEO] (73 minutes) [LINK] (24 minutes)"},{"id":44243767,"author":"WillAdams","month":"2025-06","story":"Ask HN: What cool skill or project interests you, but feels out of reach?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Wonderful video on Bezier curves.","quote":"Freya Holmér has a wonderful video on them","text":"For me, it's Bézier curves --- Freya Holmér has a wonderful video on them: [VIDEO] and I've been trying to read through _METAFONT: The Program_ and [LINK] and I keep wondering if I shouldn't just try scripting Inkscape.... I want to do a couple of different things, and not sure if they all fit in one project or no: - implement a single line font in my current project: [LINK] - implement a way to convert arbitrary curves into smooth arcs (for DXFs or G2/G3 arcs for G-code) - work up an interactive version of METAFONT/METAPOST which allows both programming and drawing"},{"id":42991078,"author":"viggity","month":"2025-02","story":"Jacksonpollock.org (2003)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Recommended for anyone interested in splines or beziers.","quote":"I could not recommend these videos from Freya Holmer enough.","text":"If splines/beziers are of any interest to you, I could not recommend these videos from Freya Holmer enough. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":40246977,"author":"ixwt","month":"2024-05","story":"Hobby's algorithm for aesthetic Bézier splines (2020)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains kinds of splines, equations, and importance of derivatives.","quote":"I didn't really understand Bézier splines until I watched this excellent video [0] from Freya Holmér.","text":"I didn't really understand Bézier splines until I watched this excellent video [0] from Freya Holmér. It talks about various kinds of splines, and the various equations behind them. Explains the importance of the derivatives of splines (as other comments here have pointed out). [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39544412,"author":"aaronblohowiak","month":"2024-02","story":"Look, ma, no matrices","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One of the best educational videos on a programming topic.","quote":"is (IMHO) one of the best educational videos on a programming topic, full stop.","text":"the splines yt video ( [VIDEO] ) is (IMHO) one of the best educational videos on a programming topic, full stop."},{"id":38656527,"author":"viggity","month":"2023-12","story":"Interpolation Methods","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazingly well done, deeper dive on splines; watched repeatedly.","quote":"I've watched it like 3 times over the last year.","text":"If this paper is interesting to you, you may also be interested in this amazingly well done video on the continuation of splines. It isn't exactly the same thing, and delves into some deeper concepts, but I've watched it like 3 times over the last year. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38070047,"author":"zxexz","month":"2023-10","story":"Why can't you multiply vectors? [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deep dive on splines, commenter's favorite watch of the year.","quote":"Her video \"The Continuity of Splines\"[0] is my favorite watch of the past year.","text":"Oh lovely, can't wait to give this a watch. Freya dives deep, and gives wonderful talks. Her video \"The Continuity of Splines\"[0] is my favorite watch of the past year. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34121648,"author":"AceJohnny2","month":"2022-12","story":"Apple's Unique Device Curvature","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Timestamps on reflection test, curvature combs, B-spline continuity.","quote":"Another upvote for Freya Holmér's amazing video \"The Continuity of Splines\".","text":"Another upvote for Freya Holmér's amazing video \"The Continuity of Splines\". She covers the insightful \"reflection test\" around the 28:05 mark: [VIDEO] and actual \"curvature combs\" at 32:26. Finally, she covers the continuity of curvature combs that B-Splines exhibit around the 54:00 mark: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43569995,"author":"skydhash","month":"2025-04","story":"Dijkstra On the foolishness of \"natural language programming\"","stance":"rec","why":"Very good video on splines.","quote":"here is a very good video on splines","text":"> I just need to find the perfect GUI toolkit which allows Bézier Curve representations in a way which clicks and makes sense to me, and suits my next project. Not a proper answer, but here is a very good video on splines: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43170801,"author":"WillAdams","month":"2025-02","story":"History of CAD","stance":"rec","why":"Related viewing on the mathematics behind CAD curves.","quote":"Highly recommended for anyone who wants an understanding of how modern CAD software came about.","text":"I've been reading through a PDF on my Kindle Scribe which I downloaded from the original site --- it's a great overview, and most importantly, includes citations and references to the various papers where folks shared their research/progress. These days of course, one would just get _The Essentials of CAGD_ and similar texts, or read: [LINK] or watch: [VIDEO] Highly recommended for anyone who wants an understanding of how modern CAD software came about. For my part, I have found it invaluable in working on my current project as described at: [LINK]"},{"id":42825333,"author":"weswilson","month":"2025-01","story":"Show HN: Apple-like smooth corners for Tailwind CSS","stance":"rec","why":"Relevant primer on spline continuity for smooth corners.","quote":"Obligatory video on splines","text":"Obligatory video on splines: [VIDEO] From what I've read, Apple rounded corners are using G^2 or G^3 geometric continuity to generate smoother curves. It's hard to tell which smoothing function this library uses since it looks like it's based on PNG masking."},{"id":42851753,"author":"WillAdams","month":"2025-01","story":"Desmos Animated Graphing Calculator","stance":"rec","why":"Wonderful example of animated mathematical explanation.","quote":"A wonderful example of this is: The Continuity of Splines","text":"A wonderful example of this is: The Continuity of Splines [VIDEO] and The Beauty of Bézier Curves [LINK] by Freya Holmér [LINK] While not animated, I find: _Euclid's Elements_ (Joyce's Java Version) very helpful [LINK] Since my current project has pretty much devolved to putting a UI on various trigonometric functions and allowing the user to program them, I'd be very glad of other resources in this space (currently working through Hilbert's _Geometry and the Imagination_ and the book which matches [LINK] and just finished the _Make:Geometry/Trigonometry/Calculus_ series, and have had recommended Curves and Surfaces for CAGD: A Practical Guide by Gerald Farin)"},{"id":37368115,"author":"fuzzythinker","month":"2023-09","story":"Bézier Curves and the logic behind them","stance":"rec","why":"Additional spline material from the same creator.","quote":"Also from her - The Continuity of Splines [0].","text":"Also from her - The Continuity of Splines [0]. More from her site [1]. Long interactive read from Bartosz Ciechanowski [2] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":37577367,"author":"nick-of-time","month":"2023-09","story":"Circles Do Not Exist","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed derivation of Bezier and higher-order curves.","quote":"It's a detailed discussion of how you derive Bèzier and higher-order curves to serve your purposes.","text":"I recommend anyone who finds this topic interesting watch the video The Continuity of Splines . It's a detailed discussion of how you derive Bèzier and higher-order curves to serve your purposes. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47386671,"author":"WillAdams","month":"2026-03","story":"Slicing Bezier Surfaces","stance":"reference","why":"Further background on the lerp aspect of Beziers.","quote":"For a bit more on the lerp aspect see","text":"For a bit more on the lerp aspect see: [VIDEO] I am a bit bummed since from the title I was expecting a technique for 3D surfaces w/ multiple Béziers (at least 3, one for each plane) --- if someone knows of a good text on that, I'd be glad to learn of it."},{"id":44472014,"author":"WillAdams","month":"2025-07","story":"A 37-year-old wanting to learn computer science","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a video on specific algorithms one might need.","quote":"","text":"I've been re-learning CS (programmed as a kid, then missed getting a minor in CS in college after the service by one 300-level course which wasn't being re-offered when I needed it) and then just did (La)TeX and AppleScript for my day job, but am now trying to create a tool for CNC which is quite different from those which have existed previously, and one thing which has helped a lot is MIT OCW: - the Python courses got me up-to-speed on the basics of that language: [LINK] and [LINK] - Structure and Interpretation of Computer Programs served as a disciplined review of a book which I wasn't patient enough to do the exercises of when I first read it: [LINK] - Mathematics for Computer Science helped make up for my spotty math: [LINK] I've also found the recent book: [LINK] very helpful (first reading I did one chapter at a time, re-writing my current project applying the principles of that chapter) --- interesting video overview at: [LINK] If one is fortunate, there are videos on specific subjects/algorithms which one needs, e.g., [VIDEO]"},{"id":42978547,"author":"WillAdams","month":"2025-02","story":"Visual explanations of mathematics (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Impressive custom Unity tools used for her videos.","quote":"I'm still impressed by the custom Unity tools which Freya Holmér uses for her videos","text":"There is also Joyce's Java Version of Euclid's Elements: [LINK] and I'm still impressed by the custom Unity tools which Freya Holmér uses for her videos: [VIDEO] Wish Geogebra was both more capable and widely used: [LINK] That said, these days if I need to plot out something I just use OpenSCAD: [LINK] (or, the Python-enabled version: [LINK] )"},{"id":38045426,"author":"mkaic","month":"2023-10","story":"Exploring Quaternions (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Identifies the creator, famous for this splines video.","quote":"Freya Holmer, of The Continuity of Splines [0] fame","text":"Freya Holmer, of The Continuity of Splines [0] fame, recently gave a great conference talk about how quaternions naturally arise from the desire to define the multiplication of two vectors if you just agree to one new axiom : v^2 = ||v||^2, or \"the square of a vector is equal to the square of its norm\". It was fascinating and entertaining and I highly recommend anyone who finds this stuff interesting but confusing (like I do) to check it out: [LINK] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34031993,"author":"zengid","month":"2022-12","story":"The Continuity of Splines – Video Essay by Freya Holmér","stance":"reference","why":"Section on cascading loss of control in C2 splines.","quote":"which is a great line","text":"Is there any general rule that systems that have 'continuity' also having sensitivity to initial conditions? I can't help but think of chaos theory[0] when Freya describes the C2 continuous spline in the section on \"Cascading Loss of Control\" [1], and how it \"yeets off to fucking wherever\" (which is a great line). [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34074384,"author":"scotty79","month":"2022-12","story":"Ask HN: Programs that saved you 100 hours? (2022 edition)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Short chrome browser extension I wrote some time ago that rewrites normal urls to videos like this: [VIDEO] into embedded video urls like this [VIDEO] bypassing ads and running video in full browser window. Since I watch a lot of youtube I think it might have saved me 100 hours of waiting so I can press Skip Ad button. I probably should just use AdBlock but where's the fun in that."}],"r":[["aVwxzDHniEw",2],["XehNK7UpZsc",1],["ir0FAa8P2MU",1],["fLA_d9q6ySs",1],["KrksBdWcZgQ",1],["fhOHn9TClXY",1],["dP1xVpMPn8M",1],["JcJSW7Rprio",1],["LA_DrBwkiJA",1],["DpXy041BIlA",1],["9syvZr-9xwk",1],["XA2WjJbmmoM",1],["bmSAYlu0NcY",1]]},"_ahvzDzKdB0":{"wy":"HN considers it one of the best technical talks ever and cites it in discussions of language design, small cores and extensibility.","q":{"x":"This is probably the best talk ever on the general topic","i":31210903,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31210903,"author":"kaba0","month":"2022-04","story":"Lies we tell ourselves to keep using Golang","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best talk on language growth, including native numeric types and overloading","quote":"This is probably the best talk ever on the general topic","text":"This is probably the best talk ever on the general topic: [VIDEO] [Growing a language by Guy Steele], seriously give it a look if you have the time and do put up with the seemingly strange start. But at around the end it asks this (paraphrasing): Should a language have complex numbers implemented natively? Should it have numbers module n implemented natively? What about intervals, or rational numbers? I also dislike the ridiculous overloading of cryptic symbols, but neither extreme is good. Not allowing overloading will give you BigDecimal.of(3).add(BigDecimal.ONE).divide… , while allowing it unrestricted will give you things like msg #!! something. But perhaps a sane middle ground is to allow only basic operators (+,-,*,/) to be overloadable."},{"id":22588178,"author":"pfarrell","month":"2020-03","story":"Lisp: Good News, Bad News, How to Win Big (1991)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fabulous talk that mentions Gabriel's paper title","quote":"which is one of my absolute favorites","text":"Haven’t read it but remembered this paper being mentioned in Guy Steele’s fabulous “Growing a language” talk [0]. I don’t want to give anything away about the talk (which is one of my absolute favorites), but Steele says about the title of this paper: “... the truth is that Dick Gabriel knew how to choose words with punch“ 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15090857,"author":"gnaritas","month":"2017-08","story":"Ask HN: What is your favorite CS paper?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Why languages should be growable by the programmer","quote":"Brilliant speech about how to grow languages and why it's necessary.","text":"Not a paper, but one of my favorite talks [VIDEO] Growing a Language by Guy Steele (co-inventory of Scheme). Brilliant speech about how to grow languages and why it's necessary. Languages that can be grown by the programmer, like Lisp or Smalltalk are better than languages that fixed like most others, this is why."},{"id":9741194,"author":"tikhonj","month":"2015-06","story":"The Tragedy of the Common Lisp, Or, Why Large Languages Explode","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Guy Steele on languages with a small core users can extend","quote":"It's still one of the best technical talks I've ever come across.","text":"He gave a brilliant talk about it: \"Growing a Language\"[1]. The idea is to make languages that grow —ones that provide a small, uniform core that can be extended by the user. Ideally, these extensions feel like first-class citizens: things added by users should feel on par with built-in language features. It's still one of the best technical talks I've ever come across. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46322586,"author":"NiloCK","month":"2025-12","story":"Show HN: Learning a Language Using Only Words You Know","stance":"rec","why":"Programming language design talk using only words already defined","quote":"this wonderful talk about programming language design by Guy Steele","text":"Enjoyers of this concept would probably like this wonderful talk about programming language design by Guy Steele (Sun, Java Language): Growing a Language [VIDEO]"},{"id":45100005,"author":"elric","month":"2025-09","story":"Growing the Java Language [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Steele's 1998 lecture, companion to Goetz's Java talk","quote":"It's worth a watch if you haven't watched it yet.","text":"Brian Goetz mentions Guy Steele's 1998 lecture \"Growing a language\" [1]. It's worth a watch if you haven't watched it yet. It's been discussed on HN several times [2]. Java gets a lot of flack in these parts, mostly by people who don't use Java and pull opinions out of their arse, but you'd be hard pressed watching both talks and not being impressed by the effort that's going into continuously improving the language. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":20504657,"author":"peterkelly","month":"2019-07","story":"Multi-Layer Dictionary (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Steele on growing a language with a small vocabulary","quote":"you'll definitely enjoy the talk","text":"If you like this, you'll definitely enjoy the talk \"Growing a Language\", by Guy Steele: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17990016,"author":"e12e","month":"2018-09","story":"Show HN: TechYaks – Best of 50k tech talks ranked by confidence intervals","stance":"rec","why":"Suggested as a classic missing from a best-of tech talks list","quote":"I'd expect something like Guy Steele","text":"Maybe these are mostly too new, or you have different (more practical, hands on?) definition of tech-talk - but from a quick look the only speakers I expected - and found - were Sandi Mets and Rob Pike. If it's practical, I'm surprised not to see the js \"wat\" lightning talk (which I now can't seem to find...). If it's more general \"best of\", I'd expect something like Guy Steele \"growing a language\" : [VIDEO] Douglas Engelbart \"the mother of all demos\": [LINK] Alan Kay \"doing with images makes symbols\": [LINK] Or, if that's too long, the much more condensed ted talk: \"a powerful idea about teaching ideas\": [LINK] (Maybe that's not a \"tech talk\"?) Rich Hickey \"simple made easy\" : [LINK] To name a few of the top of my head."},{"id":11441587,"author":"skystrife","month":"2016-04","story":"Cleartext: A text editor that only allows the 1,000 most common words in English","stance":"rec","why":"Talk built from monosyllabic primitives and defined terms","quote":"This idea reminds me of an amazing talk by Guy Steele","text":"This idea reminds me of an amazing talk by Guy Steele: [VIDEO] He starts the talk by assuming monosyllabic words as his primitives and builds up the words he needs to use to give the talk by providing definitions for them first."},{"id":10548656,"author":"gramasaurous","month":"2015-11","story":"A Basic Guide for Curious Minds: Review of “Thing Explainer”","stance":"rec","why":"Steele defines every word outside a given set before using it","quote":"Interesting language exercise, definitely worth a watch!","text":"Reminds me of this[0] talk by Guy Steele where he explicitly defines any word outside of a given set before he can use that term in his speech. Interesting language exercise, definitely worth a watch! [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10568382,"author":"pixelmonkey","month":"2015-11","story":"For Better or for Worse","stance":"rec","why":"Steele's take on Worse is Better and growing a language","quote":"is worth a watch despite its age","text":"I recently watched Guy Steele's \"Growing a Language\" talk. It's very much related to jmoiron's post here, and is worth a watch despite its age: [VIDEO] Steele's commentary on \"Worse is Better\" seems to me to be a better representation of the original essay than the one expressed here about Go. Steele says: \"The gist of ['Worse is Better'] is that the best way to get a language used by many persons is not to design and build 'The Right Thing,' because that will take too long. In a race, a small language with warts will beat a well designed language because users will not wait for the right thing; they will use the language that is quick and cheap, and put up with the warts. Once a small language fills a niche, it is hard to take its place.\" In other words, the lesson of 'Worse is Better' is that getting a 'good enough' thing to market, and benefiting from the network effects of its widespread use, will always beat getting the 'the really good' thing to market in the same category, because the cost of delay is the loss of (community) network effects. The illustrative example here is UNIX and C, but it can be easily cross-applied to more recent projects. From that vantage point, Go isn't a good example of 'Worse is Better'. It occupies the same niche as C, but was delivered nearly 4 decades later. It seems to be a \"second edition of C\" designed with very explicit goals of correcting specific deficiencies of C as a systems programming language, but without the scope creep that ch"},{"id":49127617,"author":"troupo","month":"2026-07","story":"Golang proposal: container/: generic collection types","stance":"reference","why":"Compares Go's evolution to Steele's growable language idea","quote":"Go is rediscovering Guy Steele's Growing a Language from first principles, at a much slower pace","text":"Go is rediscovering Guy Steele's Growing a Language from first principles, at a much slower pace: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43683192,"author":"ColinWright","month":"2025-04","story":"Growing a Language [pdf] (1998)","stance":"reference","why":"Video of Steele's 1998 OOPSLA keynote matching the PDF","quote":"It's Guy Steele[0]'s keynote at the 1998 ACM[1] OOPSLA[2] conference.","text":"This is the PDF of the talk given here: [VIDEO] It's Guy Steele[0]'s keynote at the 1998 ACM[1] OOPSLA[2] conference. [0] [LINK] . [1] Association for Computing Machinery [2] The 13th annual ACM SIGPLAN[3] Conference on Object-Oriented Programming Systems, Languages, and Applications. [3] SIGPLAN is the Association for Computing Machinery's Special Interest Group (SIG) on programming languages."},{"id":42519821,"author":"kerkeslager","month":"2024-12","story":"The Rise of Post-Literate History","stance":"reference","why":"Defining jargon in simple vernacular before using it","quote":"you simply define your jargon terms in the vernacular and then you can use the jargon terms.","text":"> Of course, I also accept that maybe the current vernacular is degrading. Sure, that's possible --but all the evidence I see is that humans can perform incredible feats of communication with the most strict limitations on language. \"Using language my audience understands\" is a strictly easier task than \"using language my audience understands and also keeping iambic pentameter\", and Shakespeare did that harder task across 39 plays. Language has evolved since Shakespeare, but it has evolved to become more capable, not less, and the current vernacular is evolved to fitness for current communication. And sure, the current vernacular is evolved for fitness to the average user's needs rather than for history specific communication, but as with any jargon, you simply define your jargon terms in the vernacular and then you can use the jargon terms. It works much like this[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30481941,"author":"Naac","month":"2022-02","story":"Minimal English (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Related talk reminded by building from limited words","quote":"","text":"Reading the paragraph about Galileo's telescope reminded me of Guy Steele's talk Growing a Language: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29824619,"author":"VHRanger","month":"2022-01","story":"“I am literally losing sleep” over Java (1996)","stance":"reference","why":"Steele's plan for Java that took 15 years to adopt","quote":"Guy Steele had in retrospect the perfect plan for Java back in the 1990s","text":"I would say Java the language is fine, but the culture around it is what's grown the distaste many have for it. The overuse of design patterns, etc. I also find it fun how Guy Steele had in retrospect the perfect plan for Java back in the 1990s, and it took them 15 years to come around to it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23452541,"author":"sahil-kang","month":"2020-06","story":"Collapse OS – Why Forth?","stance":"reference","why":"Small languages growing toward the problem domain","quote":"both are small languages and they emphasize growing the language to the problem domain","text":"I think you can draw this ‘core of an environment’ parallel between Forth and Scheme: both are small languages and they emphasize growing the language to the problem domain [1]. Common Lisp, on the other hand, is a large language: implementations provide much more than a foundational core, and a fairly comprehensive list of libraries exists. I think RPG’s Worse Is Better highlights some of the reasons why CL isn’t as popular as other languages [2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":12996730,"author":"rtpg","month":"2016-11","story":"Introducing Custom Elements","stance":"reference","why":"Steele points to encapsulation as core building block for programs","quote":"Steele's Growing A Language[0] talk specifically points to encapsulation as the core building blog for programs.","text":"The logical extreme of your argument is to make everything a `div` with classes. While possible, the main issue with that model is that it provides no encapsulation strategy. Encapsulation is one of the core tools for programmers for building complex programs, so having first class support for that in the templating language suddenly makes a lot of things easier. Without encapsulation, a lot of things become more frustrating. Event bubbling has to be micro-managed. Dev tools are less useful. It becomes even more frustrating to deal with conflicting CSS IDs. Steele's Growing A Language[0] talk specifically points to encapsulation as the core building blog for programs. Given how HTML is used nowadays (React + Angular are both \"HTML as program layouts\" models), not having encapsulation is a crutch [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8177022,"author":"bad_user","month":"2014-08","story":"Is Java the Cobol of tomorrow and Go the Java of the future?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in a discussion on language choice and simplicity","quote":"","text":"The argument on looking at Go's trend in isolation is a really weak one. Let's also look at Clojure in isolation: [LINK] - and oh look, Clojure runs on the JVM. > If I were a developer getting started today, I’d make a career bet on Go Advice on picking languages as career bets are especially weak. In my experience, careers are being built on being able to get stuff done and in the long list of things that a developer has to learn to get stuff done, the language itself is very uninteresting considering everything else combined. Personally it takes me 2-3 weeks to become functional in any new programming language, I've worked with half a dozen thus far in production and I played with another dozen in my free time. The language itself is like a drop in an ocean, compared to more fundamental knowledge that makes one productive, like math, algorithms, concurrency and parallelism, hardware, usability, domain specific knowledge, libraries, frameworks, being able to interact with a community, etc... > Go is a simple language by design. You can read the spec and other materials on the Golang website and start being productive in a single day Simplicity in the language doesn't translate to simplicity in the solutions that you're building. This is a fundamental divide, since often people are mistaking simplicity for easy, for being familiar. It's not in the original sense of the word - simplicity is the opposite of complexity, which means interwoven or entangled and in the context of s"},{"id":7371596,"author":"anaphor","month":"2014-03","story":"Wolfram Programming Cloud ","stance":"reference","why":"Guy Steele's famous talk on small growable languages","quote":"this famous talk he gave","text":"What do you say to people like Guy Steele who believe that small languages that can be grown by their users are the way forward, and do not want to pay to use a closed source programming language, ever? (fyi I'm referring to this famous talk he gave [VIDEO] )"}],"r":[["sWgDk-o-6ZE",1],["p6K4iIMlouI",1],["eX9m3g5J-XA",1],["LiWlvBro9eI",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["EKWGGDXe5MA",1],["dK3zRXhrFZY",1],["oKg1hTOQXoY",1],["Av0PQDVTP4A",1],["6wZmYMWKLkY",1],["hEx5DNLWGgA",1],["4bM3Gut1hIk",1],["Eg_ToU7m1MI",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["p2LZLYcu_JY",1],["30jNsCVLpAE",1],["lw6TaiXzHAE",1],["HB5TrK7A4pI",1],["vN4U5FqrOdQ",1],["1MGjFKs9bnU",1]]},"3LopI4YeC4I":{"wy":"Linked in threads about meritocracy, careers and startups as a reminder that luck shapes who ends up on top.","q":{"x":"if you attribute just 5% of outcomes to luck, then the people who have the best outcomes are among the luckiest people","i":31287982,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29200779,"author":"travisgriggs","month":"2021-11","story":"How imaginary numbers were invented [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Listed among favorite Veritasium videos, educational with spiritual subtext.","quote":"I love Veritasium.","text":"I love Veritasium. Derek has done a number of videos that are awesome like this. The videos are great and educational, but he also manages to subtly weave in an almost spiritual subtext to them as well. For example, in this one, you learn about imaginary numbers in some cool ways, history, how awesome e^ix is, etc, but also this yen-yang balance between embracing not only what is real but taking a chance on what is imaginary. Some of my other favorites: - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] - [VIDEO] - [LINK] I think he’s going to lose the $10,000 bet on the car though."},{"id":28123217,"author":"cgrealy","month":"2021-08","story":"The 3x+1 Problem [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"The Veritasium video HN readers most need to watch.","quote":"But this is the one I think people on HN need to watch most","text":"Absolutely. His \"gravity is not a force\" video led me through a whole range of emotions from \"this is ridiculous\" to \"actually...\" to \"holy crap, I've been wrong about this my entire life\". In terms of sheer brain-breaking... this is my favourite: [LINK] But this is the one I think people on HN need to watch most [VIDEO]"},{"id":26573096,"author":"O_H_E","month":"2021-03","story":"High IQs are associated with mental and physical disorders","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Veritasium asks whether success is luck or hard work.","quote":"I would really recommend watching \"Is Success Luck or Hard Work?\" By Veritasium","text":"Semi tangent: I would really recommend watching \"Is Success Luck or Hard Work?\" By Veritasium [VIDEO]"},{"id":33194276,"author":"jiveturkey","month":"2022-10","story":"Everything I wish I had known about raising a seed round","stance":"rec","why":"Liked video on luck accumulating and concentrating.","quote":"I like this video","text":"> number 1 reason [...] a world-class personal track record. > That’s not to take anything away from Matt. Why would that take anything away from Matt? That's tremendous. Anyway I'd disagree. The number 1 factor is luck. I like this video [VIDEO] but there are plenty like it. Luck accumulates/aggregates/concentrates. I don't know if they cover it that way in that video."},{"id":31630427,"author":"belter","month":"2022-06","story":"I cheated on my Microsoft interview (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Supports that careers pivot on lucky moments.","quote":"You are so correct (at proper timestamp)","text":"> Out of pretty much all the people I know, I can't think of any successful career not pivoting on one such fateful moment of \"luck\". One lucky question/answer, one fortunate encounter, one near miss. You are so correct (at proper timestamp): [VIDEO]"},{"id":30566598,"author":"ookdatnog","month":"2022-03","story":"Class Bullshitters","stance":"rec","why":"Commenter's preferred view on interaction of luck and success.","quote":"personally I subscribe to this view on the interaction of luck and success","text":"Discussions on the role of luck in success pop up here on HN from time to time, and one pattern that stands out to me is that, for a good chunk of people, it seems really important to their sense of identity that luck is at best a minor factor in their success. Your post is one of the more direct renderings of that point of view: you don't really argue that the parent post is wrong, merely that it's not a healthy way to frame it. Which makes me curious: why do you believe that it is bad for someone to believe that their success is largely caused by luck? As an aside, personally I subscribe to this view on the interaction of luck and success: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27685089,"author":"rgifford","month":"2021-06","story":"How to Work Hard","stance":"rec","why":"Fairly convincing segment on zip code and opportunity.","quote":"Vertasium did a fairly convincing bit on the zip code thing","text":"Stats measure our shared experience. Is there something better for this? The human spirit isn't unconquerable. Believing it is has the side effect that you write off others because, \"try harder.\" Vertasium did a fairly convincing bit on the zip code thing, which is -- I think -- why it's top of mind: [VIDEO] I encourage you to dig into economic data and not use your experience as a frame of reference for the economic reality of others."},{"id":24641265,"author":"xorfish","month":"2020-09","story":"Six Figures in 6 days","stance":"rec","why":"Veritasium on success, luck and skill.","quote":"There is also a very good Veritasium Video about that","text":"Ah, success, luck and skill. A really interesting topic. There is also a very good Veritasium Video about that: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24319563,"author":"hyuuu","month":"2020-08","story":"I thought I would have accomplished a lot more today and also before I was 35","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting view on luck, success and perception.","quote":"this is an interesting view","text":"this is an interesting view: [VIDEO] how luck plays a role to your success but also how you perceive your success which will change your behavior"},{"id":40209320,"author":"mgh2","month":"2024-04","story":"The Myth of the Second Chance","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The myth is not in second chances or our ability to adapt, this is sometimes extremely hard but doable - ex: 1st time immigrants. The myth is in our ability to make \"choices\" which shifts the narrative to \"hard work\" rather than luck, perpetuated by the elite or those \"who have made it\". [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36910595,"author":"criley2","month":"2023-07","story":"Ask HN: Is the market bad, or am I having the worst luck job hunting?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Ultimately, luck is playing a massive role here","text":"I am willing to provide a short communication some times providing information but the reality is usually a lot less interesting than people think. Reality: I want to hire 1 person, we get say 5 past all the stuff before me, we interview all 5, and then we choose the one that we think fits the best culturally, personality wise, skill wise, etc. So our reply is usually \"hey you were great, we wouldn't interview you if you weren't, we just found someone we liked a little bit more\". Which isn't very satisfying but you have to think about it from my perspective too. How do you choose between 5 great candidates? Ultimately, luck is playing a massive role here [VIDEO]"},{"id":31287982,"author":"mitchdoogle","month":"2022-05","story":"I accidentally loaned all my money to the US government","stance":"reference","why":"Shows small luck share makes top outcomes the luckiest.","quote":"if you attribute just 5% of outcomes to luck, then the people who have the best outcomes are among the luckiest people","text":"I saw a video explaining where if you attribute just 5% of outcomes to luck, then the people who have the best outcomes are among the luckiest people. It's hard to define just how much luck would matter in any given situation, but if you consider every aspect of our lives as part of luck, which I think is what people mean when they debate luck vs hard work, then I think it's a lot higher than 5% actually. That is, things like place/time of birth, parents, family, socioeconomic status at birth, schools, friends, teachers, health, timing of things, chance encounters - these things are all mostly outside of your control. Someone on hacker news might be thinking how they worked hard for years to learn software engineering, spent time networking, practiced interview questions, applied to several places, performed well in the interviews, and landed a lucrative job. And that's all part of hard work and it's often a necessary part of success. It doesn't feel like you lucked into anything. But the lucky part was mostly all the stuff I mentioned before - things that happened at birth, or things that happened because of others' actions. If you're born an affluent white American, for example, your luck meter is highly filled from the start. Then maybe at some point you had a teacher that helped you in a meaningful way, or you met a friend that influenced your decision making. And let's not forget there are people who are just as qualified, who study hard, practice, do all the same things"},{"id":31457175,"author":"woojoo666","month":"2022-05","story":"How to Be Successful (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's a trend that's well supported afaik [1]. Whether or not that trend manifests inside you as a defeatist attitude is a personal choice. But at the same time, whatever attitude you have isn't going to magically get rid of whatever disadvantages you were born into, so simply dismissing it a defeatist attitude is counterproductive. The danger is ignoring the tendency of egocentric bias in these articles[2]. Successful people assume that their values and ethics are what led them to success, when that may not be true. Though that then bears the question: who do we listen to for advice on success? Rich people? Scientific studies [3]? A thread I read on a previous hackernews post (coincidentally about Paul Graham, another founder of ycombinator) perhaps puts these ideas in better words than I [4] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK]"},{"id":31569251,"author":"ktsangop","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: Burnt-out, directionless but want to turn it around","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"We all need a bit of luck too","text":"Agreed! Been on a one-man team for many years, and have felt exactly the same. As others stated you are definitely not a loser. I know it because I'm not. :P - Almost every one of us is working on boring CRUD web apps, and that's fine - Work is not the only part of life that is supposed to make you happy (on the contrary) - Try to spend all of your free time off screen. Exercise, meditate, read some books (not coding ones ..) - XP >> LeetCode. A college graduate will definitely beat me at LeetCode, but I have been integrating online payment/banking systems in production web apps which have handled millions of $$ payments. Learn to promote those skills when job hunting, and don't worry about DS and Algos so much. - We all need a bit of luck too : [VIDEO]"},{"id":27401051,"author":"ttfkam","month":"2021-06","story":"Meritocracy Is a Myth","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that luck plays a great role in success.","quote":"Luck (and nepotism) play far too great a role in success for meritocracy to exist.","text":"Luck (and nepotism) play far too great a role in success for meritocracy to exist. [VIDEO] Then of course there's the whole \"the guy who first coined term considered it a joke.\" Too many people missed the satire and took it way too seriously. [LINK]"},{"id":27269183,"author":"CynicusRex","month":"2021-05","story":"Gen Z is going to have a hard time getting rich","stance":"reference","why":"Veritasium on whether success is luck or hard work.","quote":"Veritasium – Is Success Luck or Hard Work?","text":"1% of the human population has what, north of 80% of the world's money? If that's not some form of tyranny worthy of criticism, then I don't know what is. Most good fortune in the world comes down to luck and help of literally everyone else. Not wanting to share that wealth comes down to pure greed, ego, and ignorance. Veritasium – Is Success Luck or Hard Work? [VIDEO]"},{"id":26755213,"author":"yazaddaruvala","month":"2021-04","story":"Apple can own I/O to own the universe","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Said more succinctly, benevolent/inconsequential \"lying\" for society's or your own improvement can be a very useful modus-operandi. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":26539804,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2021-03","story":"YouTube and Patreon Still Aren’t Paying the Rent for Most Creatives","stance":"reference","why":"Derek Muller on luck and success of his channel.","quote":"Derek Muller has a couple of videos talking about the success of his Veritasium YouTube channel","text":"> The way most entertainers get known is just through sheer luck or connections. Derek Muller has a couple of videos talking about the success of his Veritasium YouTube channel (and work, luck, and success generally): [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25279256,"author":"rameerez","month":"2020-12","story":"Show HN: An interactive simulation to explore the effect of luck in success","stance":"reference","why":"Top-ranked of random people are mostly extremely lucky.","quote":"if you took 1.000 random people and ranked them by their overall success, the top ones would almost always be extremely lucky","text":"Hi guys! I was talking with a friend the other day [1] and he shared this idea, I think it comes from a Veritasium video [2], that if you took 1.000 random people and ranked them by their overall success, the top ones would almost always be extremely lucky (i.e.: you get to the top by being lucky, not by hard work alone) Although the math behind it is extremely simple (just a weighted sum), as well as the code [3], I think the idea that hard work alone doesn't guarantee success is a powerful one. Plus, I think the app is fun to play with. Hope this thought experiment helps reflect about the whole idea of randomness in life, and maybe help spark a few interesting conversations with friends! It'd be great to hear your feedback. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":25170444,"author":"falcolas","month":"2020-11","story":"Low parental income in childhood linked to increased risk of mental disorders","stance":"reference","why":"Veritasium on luck versus hard work.","quote":"<- Veritasium on luck vs hard work","text":"> I'm not trying to shame anyone. Yes, you are. “Most mental disorders are not that serious” is passive-aggressively stating that those suffering from something you consider to “not be serious” just aren’t working hard enough ; that they’re lazy, not disabled. The truth is, you can work as hard as you want, but if you’re not lucky as well, that work won’t propel you forward. [VIDEO] <- Veritasium on luck vs hard work"}],"r":[["36myc8wQhLo",1],["rhgwIhB58PA",1],["pTn6Ewhb27k",1],["OxGsU8oIWjY",1],["HeQX2HjkcNo",1],["mScpHTIi-kM",1],["Dp-mQ3HxgDE",1],["1PhArSujR_A",1],["Wp7GAKLSGnI",1],["ghtpJnrdgbo",1],["HaEPXoXVf2k",1],["QpAhX-gsHMs",1],["hMtu7vV_HmY",1],["cbSu2PXOTOc",1],["75Ju0eM5T2c",1],["48sCx-wBs34",1],["VNvzZfsC13o",1],["ovJcsL7vyrk",1],["QT0I-sdoSXU",1],["y_SXXTBypIg",1]]},"o9pEzgHorH0":{"wy":"HN returns to it repeatedly as the canonical argument for simplicity and against premature abstraction, and several commenters say it changed how they write code.","q":{"x":"Jack Diederich has a great video called Stop Writing Classes: It's about Python, but the observation can apply to several languages.","i":22563890,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22563890,"author":"michelpp","month":"2020-03","story":"I Don't Use Classes","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Applies to several languages beyond Python","quote":"Jack Diederich has a great video called Stop Writing Classes: It's about Python, but the observation can apply to several languages.","text":"I also tend to avoid writing many classes (in Python). Jack Diederich has a great video called Stop Writing Classes: [VIDEO] It's about Python, but the observation can apply to several languages. Pithy quote: \"I hate code and I want as little of it as possible in our product.\""},{"id":16839199,"author":"kyleperik","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Personal favorite PyCon talk","quote":"Stop writing classes is one of my favorites.","text":"Stop writing classes is one of my favorites. Also many pycon talks are great, or any talks by Brandon Rhodes. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8693951,"author":"Vaskivo","month":"2014-12","story":"Ask HN: What do you think about using classes in Python?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk that changed their view on class-based OOP","quote":"Then I saw the video above, and all fell into place.","text":"Stop writing classes - Jack Diederich - [VIDEO] Show them this. I'm still grateful for the moment I grew dissatisfied with OOP, or at least OOP in the Java/C# way. I was almost getting to an intermediate level in python (by myself) and had been thinking about a video game architecture problem I just couldn't solve. I was still \"indoctrinated\" in the gang-of-four patterns and inheritance. When I discovered the solution, Entity Component Architectures[1], I also discovered the \"composition over inheritance\" philosophy. My mind was blown! Coupled with some Python's characteristics (duck-typing, everything is public, first-class functions) I was having a blast. Then I saw the video above, and all fell into place. Class based OOP have specific uses, but It's a shame how heavily enforced the paradigm is. “Sometimes, the elegant implementation is just a function. Not a method. Not a class. Not a framework. Just a function.” – John Carmack Now, for scripts or not-to-big programs in Python, I mainly use NamedTuples for passing data around and (mostly) pure functions. P.S. Another funny thing: At my job I use J2EE aka \"lots of hinheritance and classes and boilerplate\". Sometimes I just have to go home and write some code in python or lua to \"cleanse my palate\". It helps me get motivated for my personal projects. [1] [LINK]"},{"id":4630473,"author":"inglesp","month":"2012-10","story":"An Object is not a Hash","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jack Diederich's PyCon talk on avoiding unnecessary classes","quote":"You can (and should!) watch it online here","text":"I think you're thinking of Jack Diederich's talk \"Stop Writing Classes\". You can (and should!) watch it online here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45456527,"author":"zahlman","month":"2025-10","story":"Why Is Python So Popular in 2025?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk from former core dev about overusing classes","quote":"I'm a fan of (former core dev) Jack Diederich's talk,","text":"Yes, the standard library has a lot of questionable design (some inspired by Java, some by C), and many programmers overuse classes. I'm a fan of (former core dev) Jack Diederich's talk, [VIDEO] ."},{"id":31475236,"author":"1penny42cents","month":"2022-05","story":"“What if it changes?”","stance":"rec","why":"Changed perspective on over-using indirection","quote":"Changed my perspective on this newfound tool I'd learned to use called \"indirection\".","text":"Reminds me of this talk, \"Stop Writing Classes\": [VIDEO] Changed my perspective on this newfound tool I'd learned to use called \"indirection\". My favorite quote is something like \"don't waste 10 minutes now to save yourself 2 minutes later\"."},{"id":28106656,"author":"jmielkeway","month":"2021-08","story":"Ask HN: What has improved You as a Developer?","stance":"rec","why":"Colleague-recommended Diederich talk","quote":"Also a colleague made me watch this from Jack Diederich","text":"I read this book: [LINK] While doing these labs: [LINK] Also a colleague made me watch this from Jack Diederich: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5514633,"author":"gingerlime","month":"2013-04","story":"Ask HN: What's the best technical talk you've heard?","stance":"rec","why":"Diederich's PyCon 2012 talk on avoiding classes","quote":"I also really liked Jack Diederich's talk \"stop writing classes\" at PyCon 2012","text":"Hickey gives great talks, but I also really liked Jack Diederich's talk \"stop writing classes\" at PyCon 2012 [VIDEO]"},{"id":42261970,"author":"pansa2","month":"2024-11","story":"Python type hints may not be not for me in practice","stance":"reference","why":"Argues against verbose object-oriented Python","quote":"see Jack Diederich's talk \"Stop Writing Classes\"","text":"Type hints encourage this sort of object-oriented design though, in my experience. The resulting code is extremely verbose compared to Pythonic \"executable pseudocode\". For example, see Jack Diederich's talk \"Stop Writing Classes\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":41612034,"author":"iveqy","month":"2024-09","story":"What 10k Hours of Coding Taught Me: Don't Ship Fast","stance":"reference","why":"Supports keeping code simple and avoiding premature abstraction","quote":"You should always keep the code as simple as possible and only add abstractions once you really need them.","text":"I strongly disagree. You should always keep the code as simple as possible and only add abstractions once you really need them. Too many times I've found huge applications that it turns out be most scaffolding and fancy abstractions without any business logic. My biggest achievement is to delete code. 1. I've successfully removed 97% of all code while adding new features. 2. I replaced 500 lines with 17 lines (suddently you could fit it on a screen and understand what it did) Also: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30043822,"author":"Attummm","month":"2022-01","story":"Pydantic","stance":"reference","why":"Reflects older Python view favoring dictionaries over classes","quote":"Before that dictionaries where seen as pythonic and classes where not.","text":"Its onlt recently that classes are embraced by python community. Before that dictionaries where seen as pythonic and classes where not. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29096851,"author":"_dain_","month":"2021-11","story":"Do-nothing scripting: the key to gradual automation (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Quote on not building for hypothetical future needs","quote":"Lots of times people think they might need something later. You don't. Or you can just do it later if it comes up.","text":"\"Lots of times people think they might need something later. You don't. Or you can just do it later if it comes up.\" [VIDEO] For glue code scripts like this you usually don't end up using encapsulation and inheritance or any other fancy OOP design patterns. It's usually grungy IO-bound procedural code, the sort of thing you'd write in bash if bash weren't terrible for long scripts. Don't burden it with premature abstractions. If you end up needing a class, just write the class later."},{"id":28090216,"author":"crdrost","month":"2021-08","story":"Semver doesn't mean major.minor.patch, it means fails.features.bugs","stance":"reference","why":"Diederich anecdote about bloated OAuth2 libraries","quote":"So I spent a week doing OAuth2 stuff.","text":"“So I spent a week doing OAuth2 stuff. It took a couple days to read through [Google’s] ten-thousand lines of code, so then I went and looked for other libraries. There's `python-oauth2`... it's the second version of python-oauth, it doesn't actually do OAuth2. But that took a while to figure out. So that one was a little bit better, 540 source lines of code, 15 classes, but I wasn’t going to put that in either because it didn’t actually do what I needed to do. So I rewrote that to just do bearer-token authentication, it’s ‘python-foauth,’ or if you prefer, ‘eff oauth.’” -- Jack Diederich, “Stop Writing Classes”, [VIDEO]"},{"id":26154350,"author":"mumblemumble","month":"2021-02","story":"Learning Ruby: Things I Like, Things I Miss from Python","stance":"reference","why":"Diederich's point at 5:40 about not building for later","quote":"Lots of times people think they might need something later. You don't. Or, you can just do it later.","text":"> But my instinct tells me a node will eventually have more attributes and methods. I couldn't agree more with Jack Diederich's thoughts on that sentiment, at 5:40 in the video linked above: [VIDEO] > Lots of times people think they might need something later. You don't. Or, you can just do it later."},{"id":23842806,"author":"el_oni","month":"2020-07","story":"You've only added two lines – why did that take two days?","stance":"reference","why":"PyCon talk message that we ship features, not code","quote":"which, while i dont agree with the whole thing, has the message \"we ship features, not code","text":"It reminds me of a pycon talk [0] which, while i dont agree with the whole thing, has the message \"we ship features, not code\". That also reminds me of \"when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure\". LOC is a decent measure, but features are our targets [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23334076,"author":"makapuf","month":"2020-05","story":"The use of `class` for things that should be simple free functions","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the two-method class heuristic","quote":"if your class has only two methods, one of which is init, it's a function","text":"Also known in python as \"if your class has only two methods, one of which is init, it's a function\" in the \"stop writing classes\" [VIDEO] EDIT: typo, changed link"},{"id":22217334,"author":"Psyladine","month":"2020-02","story":"Old CSS, New CSS","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">That assumes that all of this complexity was done for complexity’s sake. Or they just don't know any better. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19301103,"author":"youwishboye","month":"2019-03","story":"Ask HN: What made you change your mind about a programming language/paradigm?","stance":"reference","why":"Bonus video on avoiding Java-style class bloat in Python","quote":"Bonus video","text":"Growing a Python codebase like you would a Java/C# one (i.e. with tons of classes, interfaces, factories etc.) is a sure way to pull your hair out. However, Python provides a bit of metaprogramming that could drastically reduce the number of LOC needed, should you write it in a Pythonic way. That is, experienced Python programmers could write concise and performant Python code in less or at worst equal time as experienced Java/C# programmers. But if you hire Java/C# devs to write Python code, I can see how you could arrive at your conclusion. Bonus video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7513700,"author":"simon_weber","month":"2014-04","story":"Why Ruby Class Methods Resist Refactoring","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the two-methods heuristic for when not to use a class","quote":"The signature of 'this shouldn't be a class' is that it has two methods, one of which is __init__.\" [0]","text":"\"The signature of 'this shouldn't be a class' is that it has two methods, one of which is __init__.\" [0] Why not write a function? This addresses all of the objections. Then refactor by pulling out concerns into other functions. Need to share logic around the functions? Use a decorator. Passing too many attributes? Create a class to contain them. I think classes are best avoided until it's obvious that encapsulating state provides a benefit, which I don't see in this case. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5116418,"author":"krosaen","month":"2013-01","story":"The IDE as a value","stance":"reference","why":"PyCon talk on classes versus plain data and functions","quote":"Stop writing classes\" from PyCon","text":"I like this trend towards values instead of objects, Rich's talk is really worth watching too [LINK] . Using this approach in UIs is stretching the idea even further into a realm where I previously thought OO had a sweet spot. I still like to convince myself, though, as to why this approach makes sense over the traditional OO / encapsulation approach. My current line of reasoning is that while having well defined services and interfaces can help organize a software system, the actual arguments and return values of these end points are really better thought of as values - and the expected structure of these values can certainly be part of the API. You just don't force people to construct and destruct objects for everything you are passing around. A couple of related resources on this topic: \"Stop writing classes\" from PyCon [VIDEO] Rob Pike's reflection on using data vs OO: [LINK]"}],"r":[["CaCcOwJPytQ",1],["xnez6tloNSQ",1],["04ERSb06dOg",1],["jHP942Livy0",1],["j4H9LL7A-nQ",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["jSo5v5t4OQM",1],["CGRtyxEpoGg",1],["2Ex8EEv-WPs",1]]},"MFEHFsO-XSI":{"wy":"HN's default link for anyone asking whether heat pumps work and why they're efficient, thanks to the presenter's clear and enthusiastic delivery.","q":{"x":"If you want an (entertaining) deep dive into heat pumps, I can recommend Technology Connection's","i":34166583,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34852636,"author":"sushiburps","month":"2023-02","story":"200-year old heat pump technology is back","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Recommended channel videos on heat pumps","quote":"Highly recommend the YouTube channel 'Technology Connections' videos on heat pumps","text":"Highly recommend the YouTube channel 'Technology Connections' videos on heat pumps: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":34166583,"author":"the-alchemist","month":"2022-12","story":"US companies are producing heat pumps that work below -20F(-28C)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining deep dive into heat pumps","quote":"If you want an (entertaining) deep dive into heat pumps, I can recommend Technology Connection's","text":"If you want an (entertaining) deep dive into heat pumps, I can recommend Technology Connection's [LINK] and its followup, [VIDEO]"},{"id":31796444,"author":"mtlmtlmtlmtl","month":"2022-06","story":"DOE announces breakthrough in residential cold climate heat pump technology","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Series on how heat pumps work, environmental value and cost savings","quote":"Technology Connections on Youtube has a great video series on how heat pumps work","text":"Technology Connections on Youtube has a great video series on how heat pumps work, why they're important environmentally and what kind of power cost savings they can bring. The second video is a longer guide for people who want to get a heat pump. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":35756018,"author":"feedsmgmt","month":"2023-04","story":"New York to Ban Natural Gas, Including Stoves, in New Buildings","stance":"rec","why":"Channel covering heat pumps extensively","quote":"This YouTube channel covers the topic extensively","text":"In almost all of the continental USA the number of days that cold are more than offset by the savings during the rest of the days of the year. This YouTube channel covers the topic extensively [VIDEO]"},{"id":35139694,"author":"shagie","month":"2023-03","story":"Launch HN: Electric Air (YC W23) – Heat pump sold directly to homeowners","stance":"rec","why":"Midwest channel making the case for heat pumps in cold areas","quote":"From an (opinionated) Youtube channel out of the midwest (Chicago area which isn't known for being warm)","text":"They're viable in Maine too. Heat pumps are defying Maine’s winters and oil industry pushback [LINK] From an (opinionated) Youtube channel out of the midwest (Chicago area which isn't known for being warm) - Why Heat Pumps are Immensely Important Right Now [VIDEO]"},{"id":34739754,"author":"yreg","month":"2023-02","story":"How a Heat Pump Works","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on heat pumps","quote":"Technology Connections has a good video on heat pumps.","text":"Technology Connections has a good video on heat pumps. (Actually several of them, I link the newest one.) [VIDEO]"},{"id":34358056,"author":"nayuki","month":"2023-01","story":"Do heat pumps work in cold climates?","stance":"rec","why":"One of several excellent videos explaining residential heat pumps","quote":"He made several excellent videos explaining residential heat pumps","text":"He made several excellent videos explaining residential heat pumps: [2021-02-28] [LINK] \"Heat Pumps: the Future of Home Heating\" (35m14s) [2021-04-01] [LINK] \"Ground Source / Geothermal Heat Pumps and Other Info\" (28m03s) [2022-03-26] [VIDEO] \"Why Heat Pumps are Immensely Important Right Now\" (21m02s) [2022-04-13] [LINK] \"Heat Pumps are Not Hard: Here's what it will take to start pumping\" (46m32s)"},{"id":33884213,"author":"shagie","month":"2022-12","story":"TSMC ups Arizona investment from $12B to $40B with second semi fab","stance":"rec","why":"Covers heat pump efficiency and temperature tolerances","quote":"gets into the energy efficiency of heat pumps and their temperature tolerances.","text":"Heat pumps have come a long way. [VIDEO] gets into the energy efficiency of heat pumps and their temperature tolerances."},{"id":32750665,"author":"pfdietz","month":"2022-09","story":"Sierra Club, environmental groups petition EPA to ban natural gas for heating","stance":"rec","why":"Enthusiastic presenter on heat pump efficiency advances","quote":"This guy gets really excited about heat pumps","text":"Yes, apparently there have been huge advances in the efficiency of heat pumps since then. And this is why ground source heat pumps are less of a thing these days: you don't need the help of that underground temperature as much. This guy gets really excited about heat pumps: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":31613935,"author":"HalcyonCowboy","month":"2022-06","story":"What a gas stove ban means for restaurants","stance":"rec","why":"Clear explanation of heat pump efficiency","quote":"Here's a video that explains it much better than I could, although it's a bit long, this YouTuber is great.","text":"This is not true since the vast majority of electric whole home heating units sold today are heat pumps, which are up to 300% efficient [1]. If the power generation and distribution is 33% or higher efficiency, then a heat pump will outperform a natural gas furnace operating at 100% efficiency. EDIT - Here's a video that explains it much better than I could, although it's a bit long, this YouTuber is great. [2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31180664,"author":"shagie","month":"2022-04","story":"No, you can’t save £30 per year by switching off your “standby” devices","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why heat pumps matter","quote":"Why Heat Pumps are Immensely Important Right Now -","text":"Another one of my \"came across this in YouTube\" things (started with the design of a hurricane lantern) Why Heat Pumps are Immensely Important Right Now - [VIDEO] The key is the Coefficient of performance [LINK]"},{"id":32712810,"author":"KVFinn","month":"2022-09","story":"Interest in heat pumps has increased dramatically in recent years","stance":"reference","why":"Heat pumps can save natural gas versus burning it","quote":"Apparently you can actually save natural gas with electric heat and a heat pump.","text":">Burning something for heat is always more effective, cheaper, and produces fewer emissions compared to an electric heater... >If you’re getting electric heat from coal fired steam that’s always worse than burning the coal locally to heat something, and this is true for all emission-producing power sources. Apparently you can actually save natural gas with electric heat and a heat pump. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32537507,"author":"8bitsrule","month":"2022-08","story":"No Sun, No Wind, Now What? Renewable Energy Storage","stance":"reference","why":"Heat pumps working well in cold outdoor temperatures","quote":"Today's heat pumps can work well for many down to outdoor temperatures of about 5 degrees F (-15C).","text":"Article failed to mention that wind/solar storage can be usefully teamed-up with the another (renewable) energy source, the heat in the ground ... which is nearby many of our houses. Today's heat pumps can work well for many down to outdoor temperatures of about 5 degrees F (-15C). They'll bring down the cost of using the other sources. [ [VIDEO] ]"},{"id":31895538,"author":"Avamander","month":"2022-06","story":"Energy crunch concerns lead Japan’s government to call for reduced AC use","stance":"reference","why":"Video covering off-peak pre-cooling","quote":"Technology Connections made a nice YouTube video ( ) about it.","text":"So off-peak pre-cooling, did I read your comment right? That has been talked about quite a bit actually. Technology Connections made a nice YouTube video ( [VIDEO] ) about it. I'm a bit surprised though that off-peak pre-cooling isn't more common or even widely incentivised, especially with the rise of smart home and IoT stuff. All I've seen is reduction (so less cooling) during peak, if I'm not remembering wrong, people were quite annoyed with that."},{"id":30970644,"author":"runarberg","month":"2022-04","story":"Nuclear power helped prevent ~2M deaths in the last 50 years","stance":"reference","why":"Heat pump efficiency supporting argument about reduced energy needs","quote":"I just watched the latest Technology Connections video about heat pumps[1]","text":"I’m backtracking the debate here so feel free to ignore this. I just watched the latest Technology Connections video about heat pumps[1] where he talks about the total energy savings in replacing gas stoves with heat pumps to provide residential heating, with total energy savings up to 5 times. This goes to show my previous point about how “[w]e don’t need to replace carbon power 1:1 if we patch up the inefficiencies of our current energy use.” So to answer your concern that: > Electrifying railways and other transportation is going to increase electricity usage, not decrease it. All else being equal, yes. However we are not going to replace the energy consumption 1:1. Electrical railways have the potential to be far more efficient in the energy use. Especially if you consider the potential increase in traction allowing better track usage which will pull super inefficient cars and lorries from the road and onto the tracks. Also a minor correction: > So assuming that electricity consumption is going to decrease is a very dubious prospect. No, I don’t assume that. However I am hopeful that we can easily decrease our total energy consumption as we electrify. Meaning that if we are still pumping gas to put into our newly electrified infrastructure and consumer systems, there is a potential that we need to pump less of it for the same benefits because of how much more efficient these new electrified systems are over the old fossil fuel powered ones. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31037437,"author":"Tomte","month":"2022-04","story":"Why Germany won’t keep its nuclear plants open","stance":"reference","why":"Shows heat pump efficiency in Chicago winters","quote":"Around Chicago you can expect to have only a few days in winter where the COP is bad","text":"Sure they exist. But it's more than just \"swap one unit with another\" which was the claim above. Heat pumps are actually pretty efficient, down to low temperatures. Have a look at [LINK] where Alec discusses that. Around Chicago you can expect to have only a few days in winter where the COP is bad: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["43XKfuptnik",5],["7J52mDjZzto",2],["7zrx-b2sLUs",2],["JcJSW7Rprio",1],["cZYNADOHhVY",1],["1Jwo5qc78QU",1],["dnHdqPBrtH8",1],["r1ZLGqL1FMo",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["P7GKK3liv8M",1],["9W74aeuqsiU",1],["kdz6FeQLuHQ",1],["d4EgbgTm0Bg",1],["dqQw05Mr63E",1],["Y99Wj-NStok",1],["-opBifFfsMY",1],["AcS3NOQnsQM",1],["f7MzFfuNOtY",1],["by1xpy8ob8E",1],["Sp9H0MO-qS8",1]]},"dK3zRXhrFZY":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a brilliant talk on Julia's design and how it is or isn't a Lisp.","q":{"x":"the recent brilliant talk about design of Julia","i":11862372,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11862372,"author":"e12e","month":"2016-06","story":"Nim Programming Language 0.14.0 released","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Brilliant talk on the design of Julia","quote":"the recent brilliant talk about design of Julia","text":"This reminded me of the recent brilliant talk about design of Julia: \"Julia: to lisp or not to lisp?\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":11805306,"author":"Flow","month":"2016-05","story":"European Lisp Symposium 2016","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great Julia talk showing generated machine code","quote":"the Julia talk ( ) was just great.","text":"Ah, the Julia talk ( [VIDEO] ) was just great. Showing the generated machine code is so hackerish yet so rare these days. :-/"},{"id":32745853,"author":"travisd","month":"2022-09","story":"From Common Lisp to Julia","stance":"rec","why":"Karpinski argues Julia defines arithmetic in its own stdlib, more lispy than Lisp","quote":"`Int64` is actually defined in a Julia file.","text":"Stefan Karpinski (one of the core Julia contributors) gave a talk (in 2016, so rather dated as far as the Julia syntax and whatnot go) about how Julia is secretly more lisp-y than even lisp & Scheme. The core of this argument is that most language specifications dedicate many pages to defining how arithmetic works, whereas Julia is actually just able to define that in Julia code as part of the stdlib (the implementation just calls out to LLVM intrinsics, but nonetheless, super neat!). `Int64` is actually defined in a Julia file. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18407964,"author":"e12e","month":"2018-11","story":"Chicken Scheme 5.0","stance":"rec","why":"Nice talk on how Julia is or isn't a lisp","quote":"This nice talk mentions a bit on how Julia is (not) a lisp","text":"I'm a great admirer of Julia, but I doubt it would appeal to those seeking a \"true\" lisp/scheme. Even if it's has a sane macro system, and much of the same power. You can indeed see s-expressions (search for \"s-expression\") : [LINK] This nice talk mentions a bit on how Julia is (not) a lisp (if I recall correctly): \"Julia: to Lisp or not to Lisp?\": [VIDEO] This might also be of interest: [LINK] As well as (maybe) clasp: [LINK]"},{"id":17037493,"author":"e12e","month":"2018-05","story":"Common Lisp homepage","stance":"rec","why":"Julia sits atop a scheme with an interesting pragmatic type system","quote":"it has a rather interesting, pragmatic type system","text":"You might also want to have a look at Julia. It's not really a lisp, but it sits on top of a scheme - and it has a rather interesting, pragmatic type system: \"Julia: to lisp or not to lisp?\": [VIDEO] [LINK] Maybe compare and contrast with Maxima?: [LINK]"},{"id":13872012,"author":"e12e","month":"2017-03","story":"Some fun with π in Julia","stance":"rec","why":"Quick overview of Julia's design choices","quote":"that gives a quick overview of some of the design choices","text":"I'm not sure if \"special language\" is a particularly apt description of what Julia is. SQL is a \"special\" language. At any rate - a lisp-like language with llvm backend, package manager, and a (small, but not trivial) group of users writing real software - what's not to like? I (still) recommend the talk: \"Julia - to lisp or not to lisp?\", that gives a quick overview of some of the design choices: [VIDEO] For me, I like that it has a real story trying to balance \"actual integer math\" (not this silly machine constrained twos-compliment hack\" and \"you could conceivably write a ray tracer that wasn't unusably slow\" :)"},{"id":12903862,"author":"e12e","month":"2016-11","story":"A Look at How Traders and Economists Are Using the Julia Programming Language","stance":"rec","why":"Illuminating talk on Julia, lisps and programming languages","quote":"I found it was rather illuminating on Julia in particular and lisps and programming languages in general.","text":"Speaking of talks on Julia, I'll again recommend: [VIDEO] \"Julia: to Lisp or not to Lisp?\" from the European Lisp Symposium in May 2016. I found it was rather illuminating on Julia in particular and lisps and programming languages in general."},{"id":12025803,"author":"e12e","month":"2016-07","story":"Why is Python slow","stance":"rec","why":"Numerical arithmetic is hard to get right and fast","quote":"Reminded me about the excellent talk about Julia","text":"From the linked [lwn] article: \"Another example he gave demonstrates the slowness of the C runtime: import itertools sum(itertools.repeat(1.0, 100000000)) That will calculate the sum of 100 million 1.0s. But it is six times slower than the equivalent JavaScript loop. Float addition is fast, as is sum(), but the result is not. Larry Hastings asked what it was that was slowing everything down. Modzelewski replied that it is the boxing of the numbers, which requires allocations for creating objects. Though an audience member did point out with a chuckle that you can increase the number of iterations and Python will still give the right answer, while JavaScript will not.\" Reminded me about the excellent talk about Julia: \"Julia: to Lisp or not to Lisp?\" [VIDEO] One things he points out early is that both the C99 and the R6RS Scheme spec is 20% numerical. Because correct and (reasonably) fast numbers and arithmetic is actually pretty hard to get right on a computer - if you want to abstract away hardware \"short-cuts\" and allow for precise arithmetic by default. It will be interesting to see how much type hints (eliminating some of the boxing/unboxing) will help python. And if it turns out to really be a good fit for the language -- everyone wants \"free\" performance, but transitioning to a (even partially) typed language is certainly not \"free\". Another point with the above loop, is that ideally, even if it can't be optimized/memoized down to a constant - it really shouldn't have t"},{"id":11801448,"author":"krzychukula","month":"2016-05","story":"European Lisp Symposium 2016","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among best European Lisp Symposium talks","quote":"Best talks in my opinion","text":"Best talks in my opinion: * [LINK] * [VIDEO] Notable mentions: * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] I liked OpenGL lightning talk as well: * [LINK]"},{"id":29356222,"author":"fault1","month":"2021-11","story":"PyTorch: Where we are headed and why it looks a lot like Julia (but not exactly)","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that Julia really is a lisp","quote":"but in the end, julia really is a lisp","text":"Personally, I think when you write Julia in shorthand form, it looks nothing like ruby or basic. for example, check out parts of the stdlib: [LINK] but in the end, julia really is a lisp: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15472898,"author":"stevelosh","month":"2017-10","story":"Some Insights from a Julia Developer","stance":"reference","why":"Julia is surprisingly Lispy, with an s-expression REPL","quote":"Julia is surprisingly Lispy for a language with Algol-family syntax","text":"> Actually, in general Julia is surprisingly Lispy for a language with Algol-family syntax. It's less surprising if you know there's still a version of the Julia REPL that works with sexpressions: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15474098,"author":"e12e","month":"2017-10","story":"A Lisp that runs Python (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Julia as alternative to lisp with less brackets","quote":"","text":"> ... a relatively fast functional language that replaces most of the brackets with indentation. You might like sweet expressions coupled with a common lisp, a scheme or racket: [LINK] [LINK] ... Or perhaps have a look at Julia: [LINK] \"Julia - to Lisp or not to Lisp\": [VIDEO] [ed: and as mentioned below, there's also wisp: [LINK] ]"},{"id":14802061,"author":"e12e","month":"2017-07","story":"Back to the Future: Lisp as a Base for a Statistical Computing System [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"My initial thought was that this was about a project building atop of clasp, like CANDO: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] But this does seem a bit more like Julia? \"Julia: to Lisp or not to Lisp?\" [VIDEO] Then there's also the Axiom system, which I guess is primarily for symbolic computation, but I don't know if it might make sense to use it as a building block for statistical software? [LINK]"}],"r":[["sWgDk-o-6ZE",1],["p6K4iIMlouI",1],["eX9m3g5J-XA",1],["LiWlvBro9eI",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["EKWGGDXe5MA",1],["_ahvzDzKdB0",1],["oKg1hTOQXoY",1],["Av0PQDVTP4A",1],["6wZmYMWKLkY",1],["hEx5DNLWGgA",1],["4bM3Gut1hIk",1],["Eg_ToU7m1MI",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["p2LZLYcu_JY",1],["30jNsCVLpAE",1],["5bQhGS8V6dQ",1],["iw17c70uJes",1],["gsffg5xxFQI",1]]},"lKXe3HUG2l4":{"wy":"HN returns to it constantly as a witty, still-accurate diagnosis of why software is bloated and messy, and as a go-to recommendation in any thread on modules, package managers or software quality.","q":{"x":"one of those talks which were incredibly funny and informative at the same time","i":19707649,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19924078,"author":"leonroy","month":"2019-05","story":"John Carmack on QuakeWorld latency and business model (1996)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Famous Joe Armstrong talk, a fantastic watch","quote":"Fantastic watch, thanks for recommending.","text":"> \"The mess we're in\" famous talk by joe amstrong For anyone else: [VIDEO] Fantastic watch, thanks for recommending. Sad to hear that Joe Armstrong passed away a few weeks ago."},{"id":19706607,"author":"dpeck","month":"2019-04","story":"Joe Armstrong has died","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Armstrong talks about how software can get better, with snark","quote":"Highly recommend his thesis (2003) and a few of his great interviews/presentations","text":"That is so sad, he was in his late 60s and it seemed like he had a lot of life left in him, talking about how software can get better and having a great (and sometimes snarky) outlook on the profession. Highly recommend his thesis (2003) and a few of his great interviews/presentations for anyone who isn’t familiar with Joe, it captures a lot of what he thought about and pushed for in his professional life. [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] I hope his family and friends can find some comfort in how much he was appreciated and admired in the development community."},{"id":19707649,"author":"zaph0d_","month":"2019-04","story":"Joe Armstrong has died","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Funny and informative talk, including a story about a single Erlang code comment","quote":"one of those talks which were incredibly funny and informative at the same time","text":"This is absolutely devastating! His talk \"The Mess We're In\" was one of those talks which were incredibly funny and informative at the same time. I absolutely lost it, when he told the story of the single comment his coworker put into the Erlang code.[1] Rest in peace! [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40194251,"author":"mif","month":"2024-04","story":"Did we lose our way in making efficient software?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk about physical limits of computers and computation","quote":"It’s a great talk about the physical limits of computers and computation.","text":"Reminds me of some of the remarks Joe Armstrong made a while ago [1], and which I came across via another submission a couple of weeks ago (which escapes me). It’s a great talk about the physical limits of computers and computation. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30160727,"author":"mhitza","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: How do you explain the sloppiness of modern software?","stance":"rec","why":"Related talk on why software is sloppy","quote":"Joe Armstrong's \"The Mess We're In\" talk could be interesting as well","text":"For those that enjoyed the video you linked, Joe Armstrong's \"The Mess We're In\" talk could be interesting as well [VIDEO]"},{"id":29514934,"author":"badsectoracula","month":"2021-12","story":"C64 OS: make a Commodore 64 feel fast and useful","stance":"rec","why":"Funny talk on broken, bloated software and nobody understanding systems","quote":"here is a nice (and funny) talk from Joe Armstrong on the topic","text":"Luddite is someone who is opposed to new technology, which doesn't apply here as the sentiment isn't about being opposed to the new tech but about the new tech not being taken advantage of. The software we build today is \"vastly\" more powerful in the sense that, strictly speaking, you can do more stuff, but it is way less powerful - and often broken - than what it could be, considering what you can see being possible on older hardware and what seems to be done in modern hardware. Also it isn't really something that happens today, here is a nice (and funny) talk from Joe Armstrong on the topic from almost 8 years ago[0]. Though this sort of sentiment goes way back, e.g. Wirth's classic \"Plea for lean software\" article from 1995[1]. (Joe's talk is more about the broken part and IMO he misidentifies the issue being about trying to make things fast when the real issue -that he also mentions- is that pretty much nobody knows what is really going on with the system itself) [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":20789464,"author":"camgunz","month":"2019-08","story":"Software Architecture Guide","stance":"rec","why":"Suggests Armstrong as a broader perspective beyond Fowler","quote":"Might I suggest Joe Armstrong","text":"Agree, but people tend to stop at Fowler and live it as gospel. There is a profound richness in the different ways to think and talk about computers, engineering, and information science, far more wonderous than any one person can catalog. Read SICP, Knuth, Code Complete, Codd. Hell, just YouTube around. If your vocabulary is prescribed by literally one guy in a fedora, you gotta get out there. Might I suggest Joe Armstrong [1] :) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19706613,"author":"tosh","month":"2019-04","story":"Joe Armstrong has died","stance":"rec","why":"Armstrong's talk on software mess remains spot on","quote":"Still so spot on.","text":"Just re-watched his Strangeloop talk: “The mess we’re in”. Still so spot on. He will be dearly missed. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16003624,"author":"hepek","month":"2017-12","story":"Physical Limits of Computation (2009)","stance":"rec","why":"Armstrong on the article's ideas","quote":"Joe Armstrong gives a very good talk about ideas in the article","text":"Joe Armstrong gives a very good talk about ideas in the article in \"The Mess We're In\" (2014): [VIDEO]"},{"id":12641781,"author":"0xmohit","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"The Mess We're In\" by Joe Armstrong [3]","text":"How To Design A Good API and Why it Matters [0] The Principles of Clean Architecture [1] The State of the Art in Microservices by Adrian Cockcroft [2] \"The Mess We're In\" by Joe Armstrong [3] [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45889161,"author":"leoc","month":"2025-11","story":"How I fell in love with Erlang","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Armstrong's public appearances and opinions","quote":"made plenty of blog posts and public appearances and expressed a number of strong opinions","text":"It isn't entirely true though. The original Erlang guys made Erlang: The Movie [LINK] and the sadly missed Joe Armstong made plenty of blog posts [LINK] and public appearances and expressed a number of strong opinions (though genially) [VIDEO] . Robert Virding's public profile is not as high, but he's by no means in hiding either. [LINK] — or indeed [LINK] )"},{"id":44203908,"author":"gavmor","month":"2025-06","story":"SaaS is just vendor lock-in with better branding","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It's part of The Mess We're In[0].","text":"The discovery tax is actually something like O(n log n), because you have to also search and also compare. It's part of The Mess We're In[0]. 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29121358,"author":"j-pb","month":"2021-11","story":"What memory model should Rust use?","stance":"reference","why":"Armstrong jokes you should throw away your first version","quote":"Joe Armstrong has a joke in one of his keynotes [1], that after you've written the first version of something you should throw it away completely","text":"Thanks for the Paper! I'm looking forward to spend my weekend with it. Normally I'm a huge fan of a philosophy of backwards compatibility. Clojure has shown that it is possible with enough dedication. But I feel like it's a liability with the lineage you describe for Rust, the problem space was just too unexplored and vast, and the language has grown too large. Joe Armstrong has a joke in one of his keynotes [1], that after you've written the first version of something you should throw it away completely, because you didn't understand the problem at all when you wrote your solution, and now you understand it a bit better. A bit like how the web would be a much less ghastly ecosystem if we hadn't abandoned versioning for living documents which force every behaviour ever to interact, and god forbid be compatible, with one another, in a combinatorial hairball. Modern browsers would be less unwieldy if they just shipped 30 different rendering engines, one for each major revision, than ship a blob that purportedly is able to still render everyones Dogs website from the 90s (which is not true of course, it's just that nobody from the 90s is here to complain). It's a bit ironic that Rust fell into the same trap that caused the complexity that inspired its inception in the first place. Maybe once the major \"features\" for safe system programming were identified a new language (revision) should have started with only those features at its core. I fear that if such a language were to cr"},{"id":26555421,"author":"svieira","month":"2021-03","story":"Tz: A Time Zone Helper","stance":"reference","why":"Armstrong describes pains getting his own slides ready","quote":"Joe Armstrong's talk _The Mess We're In_ where he talks about the pains he had getting his slides for the talk ready","text":"Your travails reminds me of Joe Armstrong's talk _The Mess We're In_ where he talks about the pains he had getting his slides for the talk ready: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17662708,"author":"randomsearch","month":"2018-08","story":"The problem with programming and how to fix it","stance":"reference","why":"Perspective on the mess of modern software development","quote":"A little off-topic but good for perspective","text":"I'd say your comment is not constructive: you're asking me to name all the solutions, otherwise my observation has no merit? I'll expand a little. Let's take a single example: developing a single page web application. To know how to do that, I need to learn HTML, CSS, JS Frameworks, web pack or whatever. Linters, preprocessors, package management .... Contrast that to building flash app where a designer could use a GUI to produce a beautiful interactive app in no time at all. Yep, we all hated Flash, but in terms of accessibility to creators, it was beautiful compared to the mess we've made. A little off-topic but good for perspective: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17269713,"author":"gandreani","month":"2018-06","story":"VFS shim that allows a SQLite database to be appended to another file","stance":"reference","why":"Joe Armstrong presents a similar idea in his Strange Loop talk","quote":"really similar to an idea that Joe Armstrong presented in Strange Loop one time","text":"Madness! Or actually really similar to an idea that Joe Armstrong presented in Strange Loop one time (just the idea no demo or anything) [VIDEO]"},{"id":9915405,"author":"kylebrown","month":"2015-07","story":"Do we need browsers?","stance":"reference","why":"Armstrong on why package managers suck: entropy","quote":"See Joe Armstrong, The Mess We're In","text":"Javascript package managers like npm tend to work much more seamlessly than the old unix ones. Wouldnt mind scrapping them all for [LINK] Also, the reason package managers suck is because.. entropy. See Joe Armstrong, The Mess We're In [VIDEO]"},{"id":8572920,"author":"doty","month":"2014-11","story":"Why do we need modules at all? (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Armstrong on naming documents by content hash","quote":"where he opines that the web would be great if, instead of URLs, every published document were just named with a hash of its content.","text":"I think Mr. Armstrong would approve, given his comments near the end of [VIDEO] , where he opines that the web would be great if, instead of URLs, every published document were just named with a hash of its content."},{"id":8574542,"author":"Verdex","month":"2014-11","story":"Why do we need modules at all? (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I saw Joe's strange loop talk [1] a while ago","text":"I saw Joe's strange loop talk [1] a while ago and I get the same vibe reading his post as I did when watching the video. It sounds very cool, but I can't shake the feeling that it only works for 85% of the code. That is to say if you program in exactly the right way, you will be able to do everything you want and it will work with this system, but there are ways of programming that won't work with this system. More specifically I feel like there are two problems. 1) It feels suspiciously like there's a combination of halting problem and diagonalisation that shows there are an uncountably infinite number of functions that we want to write that can't be named (although I would want to have a better idea of how this is supposed to work before I try to hammer out a proof). 2) I don't understand how it's possible for any hashing scheme to encode necessary properties of a function such that the function with necessary properties has a different hash than an otherwise identical function without these properties. For example can we hash these functions such that stable sort looks different than unstable sort? Wouldn't we need dependent typing to encode all required properties? And if that's the case couldn't I pull a Gödel and show that there's always one more property not encodable in your system? [1] - [VIDEO] [2] [2] - [LINK] (thanks for the link)"},{"id":8641804,"author":"ChaoticGood","month":"2014-11","story":"JavaScript's Future seems very bright","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Lets not forget that the usage of javascript correlates with need to target the omnipresence of browser and not for merits of javascripts idioms or design pattern paradigms. Javascript is a great hacker language. All the improvements as stated above will extend the capacity of js to quickly hack up mvp apps. With that said I think the argument can be made that javascript was never designed from the ground up to support this new omnipresent realtime persistent application platform. For me the question remains to be seen answered if a server that was designed the ground up to be a server such as netty offers a more stable solution then node.js I think some might say that ES6 is like putting lip stick on a pig therefore patching up JS to keep evolving as more expressive language. Every time I run NPM install I wonder what kinds of incidental complexities I am I getting myself into. Then dred if I have learn yet another build system. Every time I run NPM I think of an old Jim Breuer joke [1] -- NPM is the \"tequila\" in the JS party of complexity. ASM.js is amazing, but are we getting a little too drunk on our technologies. ASM.js just might be amazing koolaid when it comes to the sobering decision to build something from \"the feet up\" to serve on purpose and serve it well. JS can be the jack of all trades and master of none and that can be a bad thing. One thing is for sure JS is really fun, but I would not go making a stock market exchange out a javascript codebase. Speed is not "}],"r":[["MdmQUlD7P40",1],["fhOHn9TClXY",1],["hyry8mgXiTk",1],["rmueBVrLKcY",1],["c12cYAUTXXs",1],["rI8tNMsozo0",1],["udlMSe5-zP8",1],["SrKj4hYic5A",1],["cPNkH-7PRTk",1],["9Q6sLbz37gk",1],["7jiPeIFXb6U",1],["CxVXvFOPIyQ",1],["o_TH-Y78tt4",1],["aAb7hSCtvGw",1],["LIb3L4vKZ7U",1]]},"1TKSfAkWWN0":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a short, clear explanation of why moving charges create magnetic fields.","q":{"x":"if you are curious why a moving charge creates a magnetic field watch this Veritasium video","i":28502795,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":41388699,"author":"amelius","month":"2024-08","story":"NASA Discovers a Long-Sought Global Electric Field on Earth","stance":"rec","why":"Simple explanation of magnetism as emergent from electric field and relativity.","quote":"Here is a simple explanation","text":"I think what GP meant is that (from one viewpoint) magnetism is an emergent property of the electric field and relativity. Here is a simple explanation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40772059,"author":"lugoues","month":"2024-06","story":"Why does current flow the opposite way from the electrons?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why magnets work due to relativity.","quote":"And why magnets work due to relativity","text":"Sort of, electricity and magnetism are two sides of the same coin, electromagnetism, so it's waves in the electromagnetic field with the wire acting as a wave guide. (as far as I understand) Veritasium has a video that goes over this. It was pretty controversial at release but it gives a good overview. [LINK] And why magnets work due to relativity [VIDEO]"},{"id":28502795,"author":"programmer_dude","month":"2021-09","story":"Why can’t I go faster than the speed of light?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why a moving charge creates a magnetic field.","quote":"if you are curious why a moving charge creates a magnetic field watch this Veritasium video","text":"Wow, this is the first time I have understood why nothing can go faster than the speed of light and why time dilation is a thing. A very easy to read article, give it a try. Also if you are curious why a moving charge creates a magnetic field watch this Veritasium video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23892328,"author":"HPsquared","month":"2020-07","story":"Bohr–van Leeuwen theorem – magnetism in solids is a quantum mechanical effect","stance":"rec","why":"Much better explanation of magnetism as a relativity side effect.","quote":"Veritasium on YouTube made a much better explanation","text":"Magnetism is a side effect of special relativity: moving charges have length contraction (depending on frame of reference) which makes them appear more/less dense and therefore an attractive/repulsive force. Veritasium on YouTube made a much better explanation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22827853,"author":"rovolo","month":"2020-04","story":"Why is Maxwell's theory so hard to understand? (2007) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Video explainer on magnetic fields across reference frames.","quote":"Here's a video explainer","text":"Here's a video explainer: [VIDEO] Magnetism affects moving charges, but not stationary charges. A magnetic field in your reference frame is really just an electric field in the moving charge's reference frame."},{"id":22323862,"author":"snarfy","month":"2020-02","story":"Ultimate Electronics","stance":"rec","why":"Explains magnetism as a relativistic effect of electrostatics.","quote":"Here is a pretty good video explaining magnetism as a relativistic effect of electrostatics","text":"That's right. Here is a pretty good video explaining magnetism as a relativistic effect of electrostatics - [VIDEO]"},{"id":8129665,"author":"kull","month":"2014-08","story":"How Magnets Produce Electricity (1954) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Modern explanation of magnetism via special relativity.","quote":"I love the way they explain it","text":"Here some modern explanation of the phenomena: (I love the way they explain it) \"How Special Relativity Makes Magnets Work\" [VIDEO] ."},{"id":8576835,"author":"snarfy","month":"2014-11","story":"Is Aluminum Magnetic? (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"Video also explains magnetism as a relativistic effect.","quote":"This video also talks about it","text":"Another fun fact: Magnetic fields are a consequence of relativity and length contraction of electric fields. [LINK] This video also talks about it: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["oI_X2cMHNe0",1],["zz4YVJ4aRfg",1],["Q8ccXzM3x8A",1],["yg-UqIIvang",1]]},"7FeqF1-Z1g0":{"wy":"Called a classic CCC talk, and the Xerox bug is cited as a cautionary tale about trusting scans and digitized archives.","q":{"x":"If you speak german and haven’t seen this yet, watch it, this is a classic.","i":29207008,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":34048813,"author":"masswerk","month":"2022-12","story":"Enhance Speech from Adobe – Free AI filter for cleaning up spoken audio","stance":"strong_rec","why":"German talk about not trusting scans.","quote":"Epic talk (in German, David Kriesel,","text":"Epic talk (in German, David Kriesel, \"Traue keinem Scan, den du nicht selbst gefälscht hast\"): [VIDEO]"},{"id":29207008,"author":"solarkraft","month":"2021-11","story":"Confused Xerox copiers rewrite scanned documents, expert finds (2013)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Legendary CCC presentation on the Xerox scanner number-changing bug.","quote":"If you speak german and haven’t seen this yet, watch it, this is a classic.","text":"(2013). The whole ordeal lead to a quite legendary presentation by David at CCC (german, no captions, live translation available but pretty bad): [VIDEO] If you speak german and haven’t seen this yet, watch it, this is a classic. His talks are all entertaining and interesting."},{"id":25591774,"author":"snicksnak","month":"2020-12","story":"RC3 talk: How to survive in spacecraft [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"German talk on scanners silently altering documents.","quote":"I would also highly recommend","text":"I would also highly recommend \"Traue keinem Scan, den du nicht selbst gefälscht hast\" [0] but I just noticed that there are no english subtitles available. [0] [VIDEO] (2015)"},{"id":28159912,"author":"sdoering","month":"2021-08","story":"Contract by Email","stance":"rec","why":"German talk on scanners replacing digits through pattern matching.","quote":"It was a really great talk.","text":"This reminded me of a great talk (in German) by David Kriesel about scanners not scanning correctly but replacing letters and numbers because of pattern matching (if I remember correctly). It was a really great talk. Not sure if there is an English version somewhere: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19395375,"author":"masswerk","month":"2019-03","story":"DARPA Is Building a $10M, Open-Source, Secure Voting System","stance":"rec","why":"CCC talk on Xerox scanner compression altering scanned content (German).","quote":"compare this great CCC-talk by David Kriesel","text":"Thanks for the complementary links! Regarding Xerox scanner compression issues, compare this great CCC-talk by David Kriesel, \"Traue keinem Scan, den du nicht selbst gefälscht hast\" [0] – Sorry, German only. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] (Didn't MS's PDF-viewer have similar issues?)"},{"id":44733836,"author":"matrss","month":"2025-07","story":"iPhone 16 cameras vs. traditional digital cameras","stance":"reference","why":"Xerox scanner compression replaced digits like 6 with 8.","quote":"sometimes replaced e.g. 6 with 8, and similar things.","text":"Worse, Xerox scanners specifically meant for digitizing documents have changed text for a long time. The compression algorithm they used (I think even in the default settings) sometimes replaced e.g. 6 with 8, and similar things. See: [VIDEO] (german, but there should be news articles from back then in english as well, somewhere)"},{"id":38706040,"author":"Log_out_","month":"2023-12","story":"UK plan to digitise wills and destroy paper originals \"insane\" say experts","stance":"reference","why":"Xerox scan bug as cautionary tale for digitizing archives.","quote":"We will never know what numbers might have been scrambled.","text":"Germany scanned its national archives in with xerox stations. We will never know what numbers might have been scrambled. [VIDEO] So similar projects existed and were so disastrous that they were quietly buried. To this day if a document was xerox scanned before the Bugfix it might be nil and void in court."},{"id":33908560,"author":"winReInstall","month":"2022-12","story":"Archiving official documents as an act of radical journalism","stance":"reference","why":"Xerox scanners corrupted numbers in scanned archives.","quote":"guy discovered that xerox workstations (like the ones to scan in government archives) corrupted numbers.","text":"Related: [VIDEO] guy discovered that xerox workstations (like the ones to scan in government archives) corrupted numbers. Thus falsifying documents on a massive scale. Obamas \"birth certificate\" went through this document shredder. So distributing, non digital copies. Thats were the lasting value is."},{"id":28162937,"author":"a1369209993","month":"2021-08","story":"Contract by Email","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Which has german/english dual audio, but defaults to german","text":"> Not sure if there is an English version somewhere: [VIDEO] Seems to be: [LINK] Direct: [LINK] Which has german/english dual audio, but defaults to german (so presumably google ruined the english track on upload). If your video player doesn't support picking audio, you can probably fiddle with ffmpeg to strip out the german track."}],"r":[["qJEWOTZnFeg",1],["y7HrM-fk_Rc",1],["pL3Yzjk5R4M",1],["AGCmPLWZKd8",1],["ezVk1ahRF78",1]]},"DkGMY63FF3Q":{"wy":"HN uses it as shorthand for vague, detail-free visionary pitches and as a satirical jab at TED-style techno-utopian talks.","q":{"x":"neatly summarizes so much of the breathless excitement of vague, detail-free scientific and entrepreneurial claims","i":30654731,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30654731,"author":"karaterobot","month":"2022-03","story":"Lasers could cut lifespan of nuclear waste from a million years to 30 minutes","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Summarizes breathless, detail-free scientific and entrepreneurial claims.","quote":"neatly summarizes so much of the breathless excitement of vague, detail-free scientific and entrepreneurial claims","text":"> If he gets pulses 10,000 times faster, he says he can modify waste on an atomic level. The great thing about articles like this is that it reminds me to go rewatch the the video \"Compost-Fueled Cars: Wouldn't That Be Great?\"[1], which neatly summarizes so much of the breathless excitement of vague, detail-free scientific and entrepreneurial claims. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6921035,"author":"simonsarris","month":"2013-12","story":"We Need to Talk About TED","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Satirical criticism of TED talks.","quote":"want a good satirical criticism of TED, I highly recommend them","text":"If you haven't seen The Onion's[1] Onion Talks and want a good satirical criticism of TED, I highly recommend them: [VIDEO] (There are more if you want to find them, I didn't want to pollute commentspace with too many links) [1] The Onion is a satire newspaper, one of the first newspapers to heavily adopt an online format. They just killed their print edition for good last year."},{"id":5336349,"author":"rpm4321","month":"2013-03","story":"TEDx talks discredited: Sheldrake speaks, argues that speed of light is dropping","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Satirical takedown of the startup idea guy.","quote":"They may be the funniest thing The Onion has ever done.","text":"If you haven't seen them, make sure to check out The Onion's TED parodies - Onion Talks: [LINK] They may be the funniest thing The Onion has ever done. =================================== Edit: of particular interest to the HN crowd would be their takedown of the \"Idea Guy\": [VIDEO] and the \"Social Media Consultant\": [LINK]"},{"id":20499839,"author":"fullshark","month":"2019-07","story":"Microsoft is investing $1B in OpenAI","stance":"rec","why":"Satire of techno-utopian TED talks.","quote":"the Onion did a great send up of techno-utopian TED talks here","text":"Not exactly what you are asking for, but the Onion did a great send up of techno-utopian TED talks here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7513165,"author":"javaun","month":"2014-04","story":"Ask HN: Should I sign this agreement?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"tl;dr. Do not sign the document. Beyond this case, it could also affect your future employability. VCs fund good teams, not ideas, which are cheap. If your partner doesn't realize that, she also has a hard lesson in front of her. Another great HN post: [LINK] +++++++ IANAL.* This is mostly based on my personal experience with NDAs, employment docs, and patent applications. NDA may also go by the name of a \"confidentiality agreement\" or a \"proprietary information\" document. You should also refuse to a non-compete agreement that says you can't pursue a related business or anything barring you from recruiting someone from this business. She may also ask you to sign a \"work for hire\" agreement that assigns your work product to her, which would also give away your ownership claim. Obviously, this work-for-hire contract is typically signed when you're getting paid as an employee or contractor. More to beware: You may also be presented with documents assigning away all \"inventions\". Be especially wary of this, as you may be signing away your idea and your right to pursue something even vaguely related later. You may also be giving her what she needs to file a patent. As others have suggested, company uses of NDAs are all over the place. Many companies do require them. Many don't. Strictness is all over the place, as is validity by state. For example, non-competes are almost unenforceable in California. Some companies who ask you to sign such an agreement will offer to compensate for"},{"id":6374384,"author":"rpm4321","month":"2013-09","story":"Show HN: Kickstart Your Own X-Prizes with FuturePrize","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Hi HN, FuturePrize is a crowdfunding site for incentivized prize competitions – kind of like if you mixed Kickstarter with the X-Prize. This isn’t a minimum viable product (we had a couple of alternate takes on the site that we ended up scrapping), but it’s definitely a more basic version of what we have in mind. It’s still a little rough around the edges, but we would greatly appreciate any feedback HN might have. We’ve gone ahead and seeded the site with half a dozen prizes of varying degrees of crazy, that we hope illustrate the range of projects you might see on FuturePrize – from absurdly ambitious, literal moonshots like the Icy Moon FuturePrize, to something pretty basic like “A Software Fix for Glowing Pet Eyes in Photos” that just represents an unmet consumer need. We realize this site could be a bit controversial on HN, where the “Idea vs Execution” debate happens frequently, and usually leans pretty heavily towards “Execution” being everything. That’s mostly true, but it’s our view that ideas can definitely be very important. People seem to know this instinctively when they hang on to them like Gollum from Lord of the Rings, even if deep down they know that they may never get the chance to execute on them. It’s our belief that providing a platform that allows the free exchange and funding of ambitious or novel ideas - where people can get a bit of recognition for their idea, and maybe even choose to keep a very small portion of the funds raised - could be a very be"},{"id":48892354,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2026-07","story":"Zig Creator Calls Spade a Spade, Anthropic Blows Smoke","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":">Ideas have always been cheap. Now, proof of concepts have become as cheap. I don’t care about your Show HN unless you have spent a month on it. I dunno, this idea guy seems to have some really great ideas, what's wrong with you skeptical programmers who aren't on board implementing his ideas for equity? [VIDEO]"},{"id":39694158,"author":"whynotmaybe","month":"2024-03","story":"Mass timber is great, but it will not solve the housing shortage","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the idea is there, it just needs implementation","text":"Has someone on onion talks said it before \"the idea is there, it just needs implementation\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":33214227,"author":"aniken","month":"2022-10","story":"Even after $100B, self-driving cars are going nowhere","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The “only” thing missing, is to get the best engineers, and pay them double what they normally make. Source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15135281,"author":"jessriedel","month":"2017-08","story":"A camera that snaps a GIF and ejects a cartridge that displays it","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Step 1: Have the idea. Step 2: Implementation. We're already half way there!","text":"> If you can make that cartridge dirt cheap, you will have invented an exciting new technology. Step 1: Have the idea. Step 2: Implementation. We're already half way there! [VIDEO]"},{"id":10017743,"author":"johngalt","month":"2015-08","story":"Ask HN: Do you work in a company that will fire you for average performance?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the management version of the compost fueled cars guy","text":"Have you ever met someone who was a hobbyist or amateur engineer at best, but always has grand ideas about how things should work? But has no idea of the type of investment or execution it would take to pull off. That is what I think of whenever I hear management strategies like these. It is like the management version of the compost fueled cars guy: [VIDEO] Deciding that you only want the best people is easy. Is there really a business out there who decides they want terrible staff? Determining who those people are is extremely difficult. Even if you are successful in identifying the best people, can you keep them? Whenever I find exceptional people, it takes more than 'not firing' them to keep them. More realistically. Policies like these aren't designed with any idea of identifying or retaining talent. Instead they are usually a means to affect work culture. Like grading on a curve. It makes everyone work harder."},{"id":9441019,"author":"thinkpad20","month":"2015-04","story":"Scientists skeptical about Theranos blood test","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of","text":"> From further reading, her initial idea was just that, an idea. She thought it would be possible to make a handheld device that could do blood tests. Reminds me of: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7584687,"author":"nostromo","month":"2014-04","story":"The Spectacular Failure Of Better Place","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I can't help but wonder if this Onion Talk was modeled after Better Place","text":"Why did they spend so much time and money addressing the limited range of electric cars... in Israel. A country smaller than New Jersey. It seems like his initial market had little need for his one differentiating feature. I can't help but wonder if this Onion Talk was modeled after Better Place after reading the article: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["T0CpOYZZZW4",1],["Ak4on5uTaTg",1],["CK62I-4cuSY",1],["v3pYRn5j7oI",1],["5eBT6OSr1TI",1]]},"gd5uJ7Nlvvo":{"wy":"HN people recommend it as an entertaining, surprisingly deep talk that comes up whenever encodings, mojibake or Unicode are discussed.","q":{"x":"you owe it to yourself to check out this super entertaining talk","i":36868375,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36868375,"author":"spudlyo","month":"2023-07","story":"Unicode is harder than you think","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining talk that leads to more of the speaker's talks","quote":"you owe it to yourself to check out this super entertaining talk","text":"If you found this essay interesting, you owe it to yourself to check out this super entertaining talk \"Plain Text\"[0] from NDC 2022 by Dylan Beattie. Rabbit hole warning: This video caused me to lose an entire Sunday watching Dylan's talks on YouTube, which are uniformly awesome. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45638377,"author":"tpmoney","month":"2025-10","story":"The macOS LC_COLLATE hunt: Or why does sort order differently on macOS and Linux (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Talks on text pitfalls, including sort order of Danish cities","quote":"Dylan Beattie has done a handful of interesting talks[1]","text":"Not Tom Scott, but Dylan Beattie has done a handful of interesting talks[1] effectively on \"there's no such thing as plain text\" which in part covers this sort of thing. In fact, I think your Danish cities list is actually one of his examples. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40396977,"author":"ReleaseCandidat","month":"2024-05","story":"Write Plain Text Files (2022)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the messy reality of encodings behind plain text","quote":"Somebody needs to watch","text":"> Every device, including ones long gone, and ones not invented yet, can read and edit plain text. [...] But plain text? Always. Everywhere. Somebody needs to watch \"Plain Text\" (Dylan Beattie, NDC Copenhagen 2022) [VIDEO] For example, have fun with ISO 8859-2 vs. Windows 1250. And add Windows 1252 for giggles. And for the ASCII-only persons: there still is EBCDIC."},{"id":34174925,"author":"mikece","month":"2022-12","story":"Ask HN: What is the best conference talk you've seen in 2022?","stance":"rec","why":"One of the most interesting talks of the year","quote":"one of the most interesting talks I've seen this year is one on Plain Text","text":"\"Best\" is subjective but one of the most interesting talks I've seen this year is one on Plain Text: [VIDEO] And although it's almost ten years old, Gary Bernhardt's \"Birth and Death of Javascript\" is one I re-watch at least twice a year and frequently recommend (and it's unreal how prescient he is in this talk -- I just hope the references to the Bay Area exclusion zone and us all running for our lives isn't as accurate as his prediction of the raise of WASM): [LINK]"},{"id":33094763,"author":"0xFEE1DEAD","month":"2022-10","story":"Mojibake","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting talk on text encoding stories like mojibake","quote":"The whole talk was an interesting watch tho.","text":"Just yesterday I watched a talk from NDC Copenhagen by Dylan Beattie about this exact topic. The story which stood out the most was this [VIDEO] The whole talk was an interesting watch tho."},{"id":41443627,"author":"yochem","month":"2024-09","story":"Llms.txt","stance":"reference","why":"Backs the claim that there is no such thing as plain text","quote":"there is no such thing as plain text","text":"Yes, and .py is \"plain\" text too. The extension however helps with signaling the intend of the file. Also, there is something to say for the argument \"there is no such thing as plain text\" [0] [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37452685,"author":"DanHulton","month":"2023-09","story":"NSO Group iPhone Zero-Click, Zero-Day Exploit Captured in the Wild","stance":"reference","why":"Argues plain text does not truly exist","quote":"On plain text -- in short, it doesn't exist","text":"On plain text -- in short, it doesn't exist: [VIDEO] And there have definitely been UTF-8 parsing bugs before and likely will again."}],"r":[["7BGtVJ3lBdE",1],["OJv8rFap0Nw",1],["iSmkqocn0oQ",1],["JWD1Fpdd4Pc",1],["0g9BcZvQbXU",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1]]},"uL2D3qzHtqY":{"wy":"A repeated recommendation for anyone interested in VMs, with Click's talks regarded as unusually informative on JVMs, JITs and the user/kernel boundary.","q":{"x":"It's well worth watching if you are interested in vms at all.","i":12639145,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12639145,"author":"x0x0","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk by a former Sun and Azul JVM architect","quote":"It's well worth watching if you are interested in vms at all.","text":"Cliff Click was the jvm architect at sun then spent a decade at azul systems as their jvm architect. The talk is \"A JVM Does That?\" It's well worth watching if you are interested in vms at all. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13743468,"author":"qznc","month":"2017-02","story":"The “high-level CPU” challenge (2008)","stance":"rec","why":"Cliff Click's opinions on JVM versus OS responsibilities","quote":"There is a fascinating talk by Cliff Click","text":"There is a fascinating talk by Cliff Click \"A JVM Does That?\" At the end he shares some opinion about what should be done by JVM or OS and what should change. video: [VIDEO] slides: [LINK] I remember a talk, where he was also talking about hardware. It does not seem to be this one. For example, a time register would be useful. Syscalls like clock_gettime are too slow. CPU info like cycle counts fail with dynamic frequency scaling."},{"id":9543058,"author":"btrask","month":"2015-05","story":"Linux futex_wait() bug – update to latest patches now","stance":"rec","why":"Informative talks on performance and the user/kernel boundary","quote":"I recently watched some talks by Cliff Click[1] that were extremely informative","text":"I'm not part of the Java world at all, but I'm starting to think that Azul Systems is one of the few groups of people who know what they're doing with regard to Linux performance and the user/kernel boundary. I recently watched some talks by Cliff Click[1] that were extremely informative, and from what I understand about their proposed kernel patches, they seem like important improvements. This report by Gil Tene (their CTO according to Wikipedia) lends more support to that theory. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9187106,"author":"Alupis","month":"2015-03","story":"Peter Lawrey Describes Petabyte JVMs","stance":"rec","why":"Cliff Click talk on JVM development and big-system scaling","quote":"Cliff Click from Azul has done some great Google Tech Talks about JVM development","text":"There are some interesting challenging when scaling this large with any system. Peter referenced some of Azul System's stuff and their custom GC. Cliff Click from Azul has done some great Google Tech Talks about JVM development and scaling JVM's out to really big systems.[1][2] [1] A JVM Does That? - [VIDEO] [2] Java on a 1000 Cores - [LINK]"},{"id":5519772,"author":"qznc","month":"2013-04","story":"Ask HN: What's the best technical talk you've heard?","stance":"rec","why":"Cliff Click on JVMs, compilers and CPUs","quote":"Anything from Cliff Click is quite good.","text":"Probably this one: [LINK] Anything from Cliff Click is quite good. This guy has a very deep understanding of compilers, virtual-machines, CPUs, and their interactions. A JVM does that? [VIDEO] Java on a 1000 Cores [LINK]"},{"id":10725950,"author":"xxs","month":"2015-12","story":"Chris Lattner on Swift and dynamic dispatch","stance":"reference","why":"Source for how JITs optimize virtual calls","quote":"","text":"JITs do know the call target. This is a quote from \"A JVM does that?\"[0]: C++ avoids virtual calls – because they are slow ● Java embraces them – and makes them fast ● Well, mostly fast – JIT's do Class Hierarchy Analysis ● CHA turns most virtual calls into static calls ● JVM detects new classes loaded, adjusts CHA – May need to re-JIT ● When CHA fails to make the call static, inline caches ● When IC's fail, virtual calls are back to being slow ---- There are several misconception about Java in the write-up. The real reason there won't be a bootloader is the need for the Java runtime and the need to GC. Unoptimized virtual calls won't affect any bootloader for that matter (and you can/should use static and/or private methods for the inner loops) [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9402648,"author":"pron","month":"2015-04","story":"JVM Internals (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in a broad discussion of JVM implementations","quote":"","text":"Well, LLVM is something else altogether, as it doesn't try to abstract away the OS, while the JVM does[1]. As to the JVM, well, first of all, there is no \"the JVM\". There are many JVMs by many vendors, and many types of JVMs (from smart-cards, through various embedded devices, and all the way to desktops, servers and mainframes). Some of the most interesting -- and most important -- ones are made by companies you've probably never heard of, like Aicas and Atego that make hard realtime JVMs for use in avionics, medical devices and other safety-critical systems. The best-known JVMs are HotSpot, OpenJDK's JVM (which serves as the basis for Oracle and Azul's JVMs) and J9, IBM's JVM. When many people say \"the JVM\" they mean the SE (i.e. \"standard edition\") of HotSpot. While HotSpot and the CLR are more similar -- at least in their goals -- than different, and each is ahead of the other in some respects and behind in others, I'd say that the most interesting features of HotSpot are its JIT and GCs (it comes with a few) that are years ahead of anything else used in any other environment in the industry (well, except Azul that sells a modified HotSpot with a \"pauseless\" GC that never pauses the application for more than a few microseconds). In my opinion, the most exciting development regarding HotSpot is the work being done on HotSpot's next generation optimizing JIT called Graal, that comes with an a complementary language toolkit called Truffle[2]. It pushes the envelopes further "},{"id":2475446,"author":"skorgu","month":"2011-04","story":"Linus Torvalds on Garbage Collection (2002)","stance":"reference","why":"Cliff Click on GC limits of real-time collectors","quote":"","text":"IBM's Metronome[1] advertises maximum pause times in the hundreds of micro seconds range. Cliff Click[2] cites a 10MB max heap for it so definitely not a cure-all but definitely something I'd like to play around with. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5uljtqyBLxI",2],["TgmA48fILq8",1],["Uooh0Y9fC_M",1],["HJ-719EGIts",1],["Z20XNp-luNA",1]]},"1jHUY3qoBu8":{"wy":"It is the most-recommended beginner Blender tutorial on HN, with several people saying it made them comfortable in a couple of hours.","q":{"x":"until this tut by Imphenzia, which made me comfortable in under 2 hours","i":28867473,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":35361335,"author":"jolexxa","month":"2023-03","story":"Blender 3.5","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Teaches tools so you can build what you imagine.","quote":"The video that finally helped me get going was this one from Imphenzia on YT","text":"hey me too!! The video that finally helped me get going was this one from Imphenzia on YT: [VIDEO] all his other stuff is great, like building and rigging a low-poly character. Instead of having to follow tutorials and make stuff I didn’t want to, he shows you how to use the tools to make what’s in your imagination!"},{"id":28867473,"author":"artificialLimbs","month":"2021-10","story":"Apple Joins Blender Development Fund","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Made the commenter comfortable with Blender in under two hours.","quote":"until this tut by Imphenzia, which made me comfortable in under 2 hours","text":"I uncomfortably poked at blender a few times over the years until this tut by Imphenzia, which made me comfortable in under 2 hours: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44933251,"author":"Etherlord87","month":"2025-08","story":"LL3M: Large Language 3D Modelers","stance":"rec","why":"Good if old tutorial for basic modeling workflow.","quote":"Here's a good, albeit old tutorial","text":"Wrong tutorials. A lot of these models consist of just taking a primitive like a sphere, scaling it, and then creating another primitive, scaling it, moving it, so you have overlapping hulls (\"bad\" topology). Then in shading you just create a default material and set its color. There are models in the examples that require e.g. extrusion (which is literally: select faces, press E, drag mouse). Some shapes are smoothed/subdivided with Catmul-Clark Subdivision Surface modifier, which you can add simply by pressing CTRL+2 in \"Object Mode\" (the digit is the number of subdivisions, basically use 1 to 3, you may set more for renders). Here's a good, albeit old tutorial: [VIDEO] Yes I made some assumptions when estimating it takes about a day to learn to make models like this: you have a free day to spend it in its entirety to learn, and as a hackernews user your IQ is over average and you're technically savvy. And last assumption: you learn skills required evenly, rather than going deep into the rabbit hole of e.g. correct topology; if you go through something like Andrew Pierce's doughnut tutorial, it may take more than a day, especially if you play around with the various functions of Blender rather than strictly following the videos - but you will end up making significantly better models than the examples presented, e.g. you will know to inset cylinder's ngons to avoid the Catmul-Clark subdiv artifacts you can see on the 2nd column of hats. > this tech is insanely useful. No, i"},{"id":25532303,"author":"juanuys","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: Best Tutorials for Blender?","stance":"rec","why":"Blender tutorial worth following, along with the creator's other videos.","quote":"For Blender, I've really liked this video, and others by the same chap","text":"For Blender, I've really liked this video, and others by the same chap: [VIDEO] For game dev, I'd start with something with a UI and general-purpose like Godot or Unity, or if you're not a coder, maybe GameMaker and its ilk."}],"r":[["xUezfuy8Qpc",1]]},"HPuvDm8IC-4":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an accessible way to remove the magic from Docker and understand how containers work.","q":{"x":"have given talks on how to make containers from scratch that are very illuminating","i":20060765,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20060765,"author":"hardwaresofton","month":"2019-05","story":"Billions wasted on Hadoop startups, the same will eventually be true of Docker","stance":"rec","why":"Illuminating talk showing containers are just a few flags and kernel features.","quote":"A few people, notably Liz Rice and Jessie Frazelle have given talks on how to make containers from scratch that are very illuminating","text":"This is precisely why I said the fact that someone would gloss over this is a red flag. The point is super critical . VMs are literally so hard to do correctly and in a performant fashion that parts of CPU instruction sets[0], and kernel subsystems (KVM[1]) were created to make them easier to run. Containers, in contrast are literally a few flags and a bunch of in-kernel antics. A few people, notably Liz Rice and Jessie Frazelle have given talks on how to make containers from scratch that are very illuminating for those that are interested: [VIDEO] [LINK] [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":16735257,"author":"hardwaresofton","month":"2018-04","story":"A Workshop on Linux Containers: Rebuild Docker from Scratch","stance":"rec","why":"Writing a container from scratch in Go; same talk as other version.","quote":"First two are basically the same talk, but it doesn't hurt to hear the same ideas more than once.","text":"In the same vein: Building a container from scratch in Go (Liz Rice) @ Container Camp 2016 -> [LINK] What's a container really let's write one in go from sctach (Liz Rice) @ Golang UK Conference -> [VIDEO] Cgroups, Namespaces and beyond: What are containers made from (Jerome Petazzoni) @ DockerCon 2015 - [LINK] Building Containers in Pure Bash and C (Jessica Frazelle) @ ContainerSummit 2016: [LINK] First two are basically the same talk, but it doesn't hurt to hear the same ideas more than once."},{"id":15830204,"author":"hardwaresofton","month":"2017-12","story":"Ask HN: Good resources on learning how docker works under the hood?","stance":"rec","why":"Similar talk on how containers work, alongside Frazelle's.","quote":"There's also this one, which is similar","text":"Super late to this thread but the best video I've ever seen on how docker/containers work is this one: Jessica Frazelle's (docker core maintainer) talk on how to build containers in pure bash and C: [LINK] There's also this one, which is similar: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13235426,"author":"archevel","month":"2016-12","story":"Linux Container Internals","stance":"rec","why":"A starting point for learning how containers work.","quote":"I started by watching and reading","text":"I started by watching [VIDEO] and reading [LINK] Also \"man namespaces\" on a linux machine will give some deeper documentation."},{"id":12732370,"author":"e1g","month":"2016-10","story":"Dropping Linux capabilities to improve container security","stance":"rec","why":"Accessible presentation on building a poor-man's Docker by hand.","quote":"I recommend this accessible presentation on how to write a poor-mans Docker by hand[1] to remove some of the magic behind containers.","text":"The core philosophy of \"one app per container\" has not changed. Strictly speaking, this will never change because containers - whether Docker, ACI, or OCI - are processes and not VMs . I recommend this accessible presentation on how to write a poor-mans Docker by hand[1] to remove some of the magic behind containers. As with any philosophy, there are many smart people who hold an opposite view. Docker itself is perhaps deviating from the architectural simplicity of container==process, while the underdog rkt is enforcing this approach even at the cost of user-land usability (e.g. rkt cannot run containers as daemons by itself). In practice, most containers must have an OS wrapping the application (unless you're distributing binaries), and that OS often ends up being a nearly fully baked version of Ubuntu/Debian/Fedora or whatever the person is most comfortable with outside of Docker. edit PS: One common exception to this philosophy is a short-lived container for admin/management/debugging. Having a full OS with devel/admin tools is useful for investigation/maintenance purposes, then you kill it and nothing is changed in prod. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["B5NULPSiOGw",1],["cYsVvV1aVss",1],["o2HBHckrdQc",1],["AdNJ3fydeao",1],["iukBMY4apvI",1],["sK5i-N34im8",1]]},"3GJUM6pCpew":{"wy":"Go-to explanation whenever the strange 29.97 frame rate comes up, praised for its clarity and presenter.","q":{"x":"definitely go and watch Matt Parker's legendary explanation of why NTSC runs at 29.97fps","i":24842304,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24842304,"author":"mikepurvis","month":"2020-10","story":"My Eight-Year Quest to Digitize 45 Videotapes","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Matt Parker's explanation of why NTSC runs at 29.97fps.","quote":"definitely go and watch Matt Parker's legendary explanation of why NTSC runs at 29.97fps","text":"Vaguely related, anyone who hasn't seen it, definitely go and watch Matt Parker's legendary explanation of why NTSC runs at 29.97fps: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46354971,"author":"walkerbrown","month":"2025-12","story":"The biggest CRT ever made: Sony's PVM-4300","stance":"rec","why":"TV spectrum allocation, NTSC, PAL and origin of 29.97 fps.","quote":"check out this video (great presenter!) explaining TV spectrum allocation, NTSC, PAL, and the origin of 29.97 fps.","text":"For anyone this deep on the thread, check out this video (great presenter!) explaining TV spectrum allocation, NTSC, PAL, and the origin of 29.97 fps. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16201670,"author":"santix","month":"2018-01","story":"Color Television: NTSC Tutorials","stance":"rec","why":"Explains NTSC's 29.97 frame rate.","quote":"Cool video. I found another interesting one","text":"Cool video. I found another interesting one: Why is TV 29.97 frames per second? [0], which refers to NTSC's frame rate. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12786720,"author":"mxfh","month":"2016-10","story":"The “432 Hz vs. 440 Hz” conspiracy theory","stance":"rec","why":"Matt Parker's comprehensive explanation of the NTSC frame-rate ratio.","quote":"Matt Parker (standupmaths) did a comprehensive video, with some original write-up on this recently","text":"If anyone wonders where the odd NTSC frame-rate ratio originated from, Matt Parker (standupmaths) did a comprehensive video, with some original write-up on this recently: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16193628,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2018-01","story":"Learn FFmpeg the hard way","stance":"reference","why":"Explains NTSC color backward compatibility and the odd frame rate.","quote":"That comes from the old NTSC standard and the history of adding colour in a backwards compatible way","text":"That comes from the old NTSC standard and the history of adding colour in a backwards compatible way: [VIDEO] In the case that the above posters are mentioning, this probably has less to do with NTSC compatibility and more to do with bad timing hardware or cameras that attempt to do too much in PIO mode instead of using real encoding hardware."}],"r":[["s4QOHe4bOZc",1],["kU3BceoMuaA",1],["lwhMThePdIo",1],["BdHK_r9RXTc",1],["LKnqECcg6Gw",1],["VkIJbpdTujE",1]]},"uBh2LxTW0s0":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a dramatic, moving demonstration of what deep brain stimulation can do for tremors.","q":{"x":"You should see this video to really understand how impressive dbs technology is.","i":7643357,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7753535,"author":"darkmighty","month":"2014-05","story":"Tremor-cancelling spoon for Parkinson's tremors","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Life-changing effect of DBS","quote":"This video left a deep impression on me","text":"This video left a deep impression on me: [VIDEO] I didn't realize how this sort of tech can be so life changing (or life giving)"},{"id":7643357,"author":"bjourne","month":"2014-04","story":"The Science Behind Deep Brain Stimulation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how impressive DBS technology is","quote":"You should see this video to really understand how impressive dbs technology is.","text":"You should see this video to really understand how impressive dbs technology is. Incredible doesn't even begin to describe it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6608927,"author":"npalli","month":"2013-10","story":"Darpa Initiative Will Focus on Advancing Deep Brain Stimulation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Dramatic on/off effect of a neurostimulator on tremors","quote":"Anyone who wants to see the dramatic impact of deep brain stimulation needs to see this video","text":"Anyone who wants to see the dramatic impact of deep brain stimulation needs to see this video[1]. Just by turning off and on his neurostimulator (see starting 2:00-ish) see the dramatic change in his body. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7752936,"author":"hansy","month":"2014-05","story":"Tremor-cancelling spoon for Parkinson's tremors","stance":"rec","why":"Before and after effects of DBS treatment","quote":"this video is pretty powerful","text":"For anyone interested in seeing the before/after effects of DBS treatment, this video is pretty powerful: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7752989,"author":"fossuser","month":"2014-05","story":"Tremor-cancelling spoon for Parkinson's tremors","stance":"rec","why":"Good example of tremors nearly cured by DBS","quote":"This is a pretty good example","text":"Obviously something like brain surgery shouldn't be taken lightly, but I've had a family friend with tremors go through the process and the result is pretty amazing. Went from pretty severe tremors to appearing basically completely cured. This is a pretty good example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12978887,"author":"Balgair","month":"2016-11","story":"The mathematics of science’s broken reward system","stance":"reference","why":"Example of science helping people","quote":"(a good showing of why science is right and helps folks)","text":"Science has a lot going wrong with it. Here are some I can list: P-values, the h-index (article discusses), the funding crunches and (US specific) the cyclic nature of NIH funding, the massively skewed incentives of publish or perish, the entire idea of trying to assign value to discoveries (article discusses), the 2-body problem of family life and science (at least the Germans expect you to have no life and are clear on that), the work-life non-balance, the relatively very poor pay, the hyper-competition and sabotage (in some fields, labs, or universities), the total ignorance of doctors/politicians that take your work and apply it in appalling ways, the entire racket of scientific publishing, etc. As always, we should point out to newcomers to this discussion of one of the most read scientific paper EVER by John P. Ioannidis \"Why Most Published Research Findings Are False\" [0]. If you don't have access and don't want to use Sci-Hub or ICanHazPDF, here is a youtube of John discussing his work [1]. You can use the google to really jump into a rabbit hole here. Essentially, flip a coin, heads the paper is right, tails the paper is wrong, it really is that bad these days. Ok, so lets just scrap the whole thing then, right? Here is why not: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] (a good showing of why science is right and helps folks) [LINK] So, yeah, science is a mess right now. But if we scientists don't step up and make it not a mess, those scummy money-grubbing scammer dirtbags"}],"r":[["nqzpAbK9qFk",1],["7R4xhwy-1oI",1],["wUwZZaI5u0c",1],["xsLJZyih3Ac",1],["Ucp0TTmvqOE",1],["PUv66718DII",1],["qEV9qoup2mQ",1],["g6bOwQdCJrc",1],["hx7BXih7zx8",1],["folTvNDL08A",1],["7xTGNNLPyMI",1]]},"vJjKwSF9gT8":{"wy":"HN uses it as the go-to explanation of why hydrogen internal combustion engines do not make sense.","q":{"x":"Here's a very factual explanation of why hydrogen internal combustion engines won't work","i":34489720,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37837690,"author":"drewg123","month":"2023-10","story":"Oil sector is lobbying for inefficient hydrogen cars to delay electrification","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Why hydrogen is not a practical fuel for combustion engines.","quote":"has an excellent video regarding why hydrogen is not a practical replacement for gasoline/petrol/diesel","text":"The YouTube channel Engineering Explained has an excellent video regarding why hydrogen is not a practical replacement for gasoline/petrol/diesel in a traditional internal combustion engine: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39210118,"author":"drewg123","month":"2024-01","story":"Crash tests indicate guardrail system can't handle heavy electric vehicles","stance":"rec","why":"Reasons pure hydrogen cars are not practical.","quote":"i watched a fascinating YT video by Engineering Explained","text":"Except containing hydrogen requires bulky tanks. I was curious, and googled.. It turns out the only hydrogen car I'm aware of that's on sale in the US (Toyota Mirai) weighs several hundred pounds more than the most popular EV in the US (Tesla Model 3).. ~4300lbs vs ~3900lbs. Except the Mirai isn't a pure hydrogen car.. its more of an EV with a hydrogen powered generator. I'm not aware of any purely hydrogen fueled cars on sale. i watched a fascinating YT video by Engineering Explained that went into the reasons why a pure hydrogen car is not practical ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":34489720,"author":"gwbas1c","month":"2023-01","story":"'A complete failure' Japan's 'fantasy' hydrogen strategy does nothing","stance":"rec","why":"Factual explanation of why hydrogen combustion engines won't work.","quote":"Here's a very factual explanation of why hydrogen internal combustion engines won't work","text":"> collect facts The physical properties and limitations of hydrogen are well-known. Many people repeat them in other posts in this thread. Here's a very factual explanation of why hydrogen internal combustion engines won't work: [VIDEO] (In summary, you can't compress hydrogen enough to fit in a reasonably-sized tank. The energy that it takes to compress hydrogen will be expensive.)"},{"id":33507762,"author":"taylodl","month":"2022-11","story":"The New Hydrogen Car That Travels 2k Km with a Single Tank","stance":"rec","why":"Explains problems with hydrogen engines, including distribution and storage.","quote":"explained a lot of problems you can expect with hydrogen engines - including distribution and storage issues.","text":"YouTube's Engineering Explained channel took a close look at the hydrogen engine Toyota recently unveiled and in doing so explained a lot of problems you can expect with hydrogen engines - including distribution and storage issues. Watch here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33790736,"author":"gwbas1c","month":"2022-11","story":"Ask HN: How innovative is the hydrogen industry?","stance":"rec","why":"Why hydrogen in an internal combustion engine doesn't make sense.","quote":"This video explains why using hydrogen to power an internal combustion engine doesn't make sense","text":"WRT hydrogen engines: This video explains why using hydrogen to power an internal combustion engine doesn't make sense: [VIDEO] Lots of more informative responses in this thread than I can explain; just remember that a lot of people are promoting \"hydrogen\" in situations where it is either economically inferior to traditional batteries, or where the laws of physics make it impractical. (IE, the hydrogen V8 requires tanks so large that the vehicle is impractical.) In whatever hydrogen space you're getting into, make sure you run the numbers like this video does. (IE, efficiency, volume of compressed hydrogen, energy lost to compressing / refrigerating, ect,) and make sure they make sense. For example, hydrogen might not make sense for day-to-day storage compared to a battery, but it may make perfect sense for a replacement for a standby generator."},{"id":34986805,"author":"LeifCarrotson","month":"2023-03","story":"Germany refuses to agree to EU ban on new fossil fuel cars from 2035","stance":"reference","why":"Analysis of why hydrogen combustion is impractical.","quote":"Check out this analysis","text":"Hydrogen fuel cells can be made to work, they're currently somewhat worse than lithium-ion batteries in terms of energy storage but it's not completely hopeless. On the other hand, hydrogen combustion is an inefficient process that uses an astonishingly bulky, expensive fuel that needs to be compressed to absurd pressures and has no realistic use case - except to give petrolheads hope that things don't have to change and to kick the can down the road while continuing to burn fossil fuels. Check out this analysis: [VIDEO] or the numerous sources cited. This is not about German car companies like BMW, Volkswagen, and Mercedes being cut off from productive lines of inquiry. Those lines were only undertaken for show to allow them to continue business as usual. This is about them successfully bribing the German government to allow them to keep destroying the planet."},{"id":34637226,"author":"freetime2","month":"2023-02","story":"Seawater split to produce green hydrogen with nearly 100% efficiency","stance":"reference","why":"Critical look at Toyota and BMW hydrogen combustion engines.","quote":"neither of them sound very appealing to me.","text":"Toyota (in collaboration with Yamaha) is developing a V8 hydrogen combustion engine [1]. BMW is also developing a V12 hydrogen combustion engine [2]. \"Engineering Explained\" has put out videos on both, and neither of them sound very appealing to me. [3][4] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":34484643,"author":"TheRealPomax","month":"2023-01","story":"'A complete failure' Japan's 'fantasy' hydrogen strategy does nothing","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why hydrogen engines won't work.","quote":"a better person to ask to explain why this is just not going to work","text":"Maybe, but [LINK] is probably a better person to ask to explain why this is just not going to work, through his \"The Unfortunate Truth About Toyota's Hydrogen V8 Engine\" [1] and \"BMW's Hydrogen V12 Engine Is A Hilarious Engineering Stunt\" [2] videos. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":33396785,"author":"inhumantsar","month":"2022-10","story":"Volkswagen develops hydrogen car that can travel 2k kilometers on one tank","stance":"reference","why":"Hydrogen combustion engines face storage and efficiency problems.","quote":"Combustion generators in the car have storage issues and efficiency issues.","text":"Fuel cells in the vehicle would be much more efficient. Combustion generators in the car have storage issues and efficiency issues. liquid hydrogen needs cryogenic storage, compressed hydrogen isn't space efficient relative to the output. Combustion generators at home would suffer from the same storage problems as in cars. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["-k-V3ESHcfA",1],["xi1Lq79mLeE",1],["sfrYOWlKJ_g",1]]},"SMEOKDVXlUo":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a corrective to enterprise blockchain marketing and as an explanation of what makes Bitcoin distinctive.","q":{"x":"Just watch Blockchain vs. Bullshit","i":14800741,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15868025,"author":"tlrobinson","month":"2017-12","story":"It's Not Just a Bitcoin Bubble – It's Far Worse","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains blockchain is just one piece of what makes Bitcoin interesting.","quote":"Andreas Antonopoulous has a great talk on this","text":"Andreas Antonopoulous has a great talk on this: [VIDEO] Spoiler: nothing, “blockchain” is one piece of what makes Bitcoin interesting."},{"id":14800741,"author":"dpc_pw","month":"2017-07","story":"Master Card, Cisco, and Scotiabank Join the Enterprise Ethereum Alliance","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Debunks corporate blockchain marketing.","quote":"Just watch Blockchain vs. Bullshit","text":"How pointless. The whole point of Bitcoin was to have uncensorable, decentralized asset that can be used to exchange value without any trust etc. That is the only reason we endure this utterly shitty and inefficient blockchain thing, and we exchange money for this otherwise pointless online points. I have no problem with ETH as cryptocurrency / smart contract platform. But this whole Enterprise Ethereum Alliance is just one big BS. Etherum community is trying to pump ETH value by associating with brand names, and corporations are trying to pump their stock value by presenting themselves as innovative. BS - empty words and marketing gimmicks. Just read through that page. Just watch Blockchain vs. Bullshit: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15017130,"author":"Ninn","month":"2017-08","story":"Blockchain could fix a key problem in China’s food industry","stance":"rec","why":"Corrects misunderstanding of the core technology.","quote":"I can recommend watching","text":"This sound like just another case of misunderstanding the core technology. I can recommend watching: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["mD4L7xDNCmA",1]]},"VeAdryYZ7ak":{"wy":"HN remembers it as the memorable explanation of restarts and multiple dispatch, referenced whenever those features come up.","q":{"x":"I will forever think of this talk by Peter Siebel","i":44149321,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44149321,"author":"ethagnawl","month":"2025-06","story":"Ovld – Efficient and featureful multiple dispatch for Python","stance":"rec","why":"Memorable talk associated with multiple dispatch.","quote":"I will forever think of this talk by Peter Siebel","text":"I will forever think of this talk by Peter Siebel (author of Practical Common Lisp) whenever I hear about multiple dispatch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11528251,"author":"jlg23","month":"2016-04","story":"A polyglot's guide to multiple dispatch","stance":"rec","why":"Compares multiple dispatch with the visitor pattern.","quote":"covers that from a different angle, also comparing multiple dispatch with the visitor pattern","text":"Peter Seibel's talk \"Practical Common Lisp\"[1] covers that from a different angle, also comparing multiple dispatch with the visitor pattern. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9976940,"author":"jlg23","month":"2015-07","story":"Use Cases for Change-Class in Common Lisp","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Common Lisp's generic-method object system versus C++/Java.","quote":"explains that pretty well","text":"The author provides a good example of legitimate use of change-class with his compiler example. About violating \"some earlier architectural plan\" in the case of libraries: Being able to do this allows CL programmers to freely mix libraries with different \"architectural plans\" without resorting to some half-baked fusion architecture that has to account for the different styles used by the libraries. Last but not least, change-class is not a hack - it's behavior is well defined and documented. And when you consider that CL's \"object orientation\" is more generic that c++'s or java's, based on generic methods alone, inferring from c++- or java-paradigms to good CL style just does not work. Peter Seibel's talk \"Practical Common Lisp\" at google explains that pretty well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2518715,"author":"tdrgabi","month":"2011-05","story":"The German School of Lisp (\"Fluchtpunkt Lisps\")","stance":"rec","why":"Introduces Lisp restarts.","quote":"Restarts are really nice, I first heard about them in","text":"I agree that my macros explanation was stretched. I simplified it too much. Restarts are really nice, I first heard about them in \"Practical Common Lisp\" google talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21613216,"author":"bjoli","month":"2019-11","story":"Overview of Rust error handling libraries","stance":"reference","why":"Seibel discusses Lisp error handling in his Google talk.","quote":"Peter Seibel talks about it in Practical Common Lisp","text":"Peter Seibel talks about it in Practical Common Lisp and in the talk he had at Google about the book: [VIDEO] The book is available online I think."}],"r":[["YX40hbAHx3s",1]]},"AZX6awZq5Z0":{"wy":"Recommended for its account of competence without comprehension, relevant to evolution and to debates about AI.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend this Royal Institute talk","i":14390732,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14390732,"author":"badosu","month":"2017-05","story":"The Energy Expansions of Evolution","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how dumb evolution designed intelligence via small steps, bottom-up.","quote":"I highly recommend this Royal Institute talk","text":"I highly recommend this Royal Institute talk: Information, Evolution, and intelligent Design - With Daniel Dennett [0]. The presenter provides a series of small steps in the way of understanding how the combined leaps of 'dumb' evolution was able to design intelligence. He contrasts the 'bottom-up' approach to the 'top-down' design in a very intelligent manner. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39650764,"author":"rthrfrd","month":"2024-03","story":"Claude 3 catches researchers testing it","stance":"rec","why":"Dennett on competence without comprehension, overlapping earlier talk.","quote":"I find Daniel Dennett's framing of competency vs comprehension a good place to start.","text":"I find Daniel Dennett's framing of competency vs comprehension a good place to start. He did a nice talk at the RI on it some time ago[1]. A previous one overlaps with similar ground[2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13791555,"author":"neom","month":"2017-03","story":"Is Consciousness an Illusion?","stance":"rec","why":"Dennett talk on information, evolution and design.","quote":"World Science Festival has a slew of really awesome talks etc on this subject posted to youtube","text":"World Science Festival has a slew of really awesome talks etc on this subject posted to youtube: [LINK] And The Neuroscience of Consciousness – with Anil Seth is really great: [LINK] Information, Evolution, and intelligent Design - With Daniel Dennett: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38040995,"author":"passion__desire","month":"2023-10","story":"The Slow Death of Authenticity in an Attention Economy","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There was a question asked on Quora by a child to her mother, \"Why haven't we used all the short words and gave them meaning before using all the long words?\" The simple answer, apart from historical origins of words is \"error correction\". It is far easier to error correct words which are far apart in spelling and audio space. relevant : [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["9XkHBmE-N34",1],["2JAOTJxYqh8",1],["RlXdsyctD50",1],["YycAzdtUIko",1],["1YFrISfN7jo",1],["qbIk7-JPB2c",1],["mht-1c4wc0Q",1],["xRel1JKOEbI",1],["umatbZ0n4mE",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["G0Q4UAiKacw",1],["tafGL02EUOA",1],["KePNhUJ2reI",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1],["TTFoJQSd48c",1],["AyORieDhpkg",1],["PbmJkMhmrVI",1]]},"EZxP0i9ah8E":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it for its memorable lines on advice, limited experience and early user focus, and rewatch it as a reminder not to be prescriptive.","q":{"x":"I watch the video every once in a while to remind myself not to be similarly prescriptive","i":7199958,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7199958,"author":"alabut","month":"2014-02","story":"How I learned to stop giving advice","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Why advice is limited experience plus overgeneralization, and how to process feedback","quote":"I watch the video every once in a while to remind myself not to be similarly prescriptive","text":"Paul Buchheit said something similar at startup school years ago and I watch the video every once in a while to remind myself not to be similarly prescriptive when I meet new people. His key takeaway slide was a simple equation: Limited Life Experience + Overgeneralization = Advice The full video has more info on how to get past the advice you hear and actually process feedback: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2088731,"author":"revorad","month":"2011-01","story":"Ask HN: Tell me about your failed startup.","stance":"rec","why":"Buchheit on interpreting 'that's impossible'","quote":"An oldie but goodie","text":"If someone says \"that's impossible\", you can interpret it as: \"According to my limited experience and very narrow understanding of reality, it's very unlikely.\" An oldie but goodie - [VIDEO]"},{"id":1263600,"author":"grinich","month":"2010-04","story":"Ben Horowitz: Things Some VCs Do That I Don’t Like","stance":"rec","why":"Buchheit on reframing 'that's impossible' as limited experience","quote":"If someone says: \"That's impossible.\" You should understand it as: \"According to my very limited experience and narrow understanding of reality, that's very unlikely.","text":"Paul Buchheit had a good quote at the 08 startup school. If someone says: \"That's impossible.\" You should understand it as: \"According to my very limited experience and narrow understanding of reality, that's very unlikely.\" Video: [VIDEO] Slides: [LINK]"},{"id":10748009,"author":"nostrademons","month":"2015-12","story":"Evidence that “disfluencies” in speech enhance comprehension","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a substantive talk full of filler words","quote":"I thought it was great","text":"I'd love to see the study broken out by people who are listening for confidence vs. content . I recall showing my mom Paul Buchheit's Startup School 08 talk: [VIDEO] I thought it was great, and I remember listening very intently to parse out exactly what he was saying and figure out what he was saying. I showed it to my mom, and her first reaction was \"Why are you listening to him? He seems kinda nebbishy.\" Notice how many pauses, umms, y'knows, and other filler words are in that speech. I ignored them because I already knew what he had accomplished; my assumption was that he knew what he was talking about, and so I should pay close attention. My mom, who knows nothing about tech and didn't start using GMail until 2012, came away with the impression that he was unconfident, rambling, and didn't really know what he was talking about. The advice to eliminate filler words may be a consequence of a world where people make snap judgments about the credibility of a person in seconds, and usually are not qualified to judge the content of the speech at all. Notice how the article says that this advice first caught on in the 20th century, right as mass communication and soundbite messages caught on. The studies they cite, however, all featured listeners from the same field as the speaker who already cared about the content."},{"id":3527629,"author":"alabut","month":"2012-01","story":"Interview with Facebook's Andrei Alexandrescu","stance":"reference","why":"Buchheit's talk on learning and advice","quote":"Reminds me of Paul B's video from startup school","text":"Love his answer to #7 on what advice he'd give to a new developer: \"7. What advice would you give to a beginner server-side developer and what to an open-source author / contributor? Meh, advice-shmadvice. Who am I to give advice? What’s experience good for in our field? I used to know how to write a keyboard driver and floppy disc formatters with non-standard densities. I’d be all but obsolete today if I hadn’t learned continuously. Oh, so maybe this would be one piece of advice: learn how to learn, and stick with principles; mastering individual technologies will follow. Technology is a great servant but a terrible master. The best people in our métier are those who know how to quickly become experts in some particular field.\" Reminds me of Paul B's video from startup school: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1856023,"author":"revorad","month":"2010-11","story":"How to Take Down Facebook -- Hint: It Ain't Twitter. ","stance":"reference","why":"Buchheit's point about virality and limitation","quote":"Viral and limitation don't go hand in hand.","text":"Viral and limitation don't go hand in hand. Paul Buchheit made this point realy well in his startup school talk couple of years ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1588836,"author":"DanielRibeiro","month":"2010-08","story":"If you can't find 10 people who say they'll buy it...","stance":"reference","why":"Buchheit advises aiming for 100 happy users","quote":"You should aim for 100 happy users","text":"Paul Bucheit said something similar on Startup School 2008 ( [VIDEO] ): \"You should aim for 100 happy users\". And he mentions that being happy is quite hard to accomplish, as happy users will not put up with any unevenness they see. I find this more useful for startups that are still looking for a business model (mostly consumer oriented startups)."}],"r":[["pPJf8KrvJXU",1],["7XUWpze_A_s",1]]},"Iyp_X3mwE1w":{"wy":"Recommended as an entertaining, in-depth beginner's guide that persuades skeptics that a wall outlet often suffices.","q":{"x":"I was particularly swayed by the technology connections video on this topic I watched before buying my first EV","i":46627276,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":33679507,"author":"gcanyon","month":"2022-11","story":"Hyundai’s Ioniq 6 EV makes its US debut","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Extremely in-depth look at EV costs, trade-offs and home charging.","quote":"this entertaining video goes into extreme depth","text":"For anyone wondering about the practicalities of an EV: cost trade-offs, home charging, and more, this entertaining video goes into extreme depth: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46627276,"author":"Dibes","month":"2026-01","story":"Ford F-150 Lightning outsold the Cybertruck and was then canceled for poor sales","stance":"rec","why":"Convinced commenter that a wall outlet suffices for daily EV charging.","quote":"I was particularly swayed by the technology connections video on this topic I watched before buying my first EV","text":"The thing is, for most people a standard wall outlet is plenty. The math works out that a simple 15/20 amp circuit can charge over 40 miles overnight, and the vast majority of people aren't driving more than that for their daily commute plus errands. Level 1 charging is genuinely sufficient most of the time. I was particularly swayed by the technology connections video on this topic I watched before buying my first EV [VIDEO] The real pain point, in my opinion, is whether you have any place to plug in nightly. If you don't, then as you pointed out, it becomes a nightmare to own. Range anxiety is completely justified when public charging infrastructure is still as unreliable as it is, years after the initial build outs. Your points about charging pain are all too common. If you have a garage with an outlet, you are generally fine. I lived off a level 1 charger for over a year before I decided I wanted the convenience of a level 2 charger."},{"id":33183139,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2022-10","story":"GM created a new energy business to sell batteries and solar panels","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why you need far less home charging power than assumed.","quote":"Technology Connections (of dishwasher fame) goes over why you probably need much less","text":"> Most people with an EV will be fine with a simple 19kW AC charger in their garage. Technology Connections (of dishwasher fame) goes over why you probably need much less: * [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5TkIe60y2GI",1],["bglWCuCMSWc",1],["S7TUe5w6RHo",1],["v3y8AIEX_dU",1],["Ds-v2-qyCc8",1],["sI-1YNAmWlk",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["VQKMoT-6XSg",1],["S0qjK3TWZE8",1],["M8F5hXqS-Ac",1],["CUjt36SD3h8",1],["pLPpl2ISKTg",1],["oqu5DjzOBF8",1],["zT58pzY41wA",1],["j4H9LL7A-nQ",1],["KPUlgSRn6e0",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["MejbOFk7H6c",1],["qWKz3psejb0",1],["cj02_UeUnGQ",1]]},"MiUHjLxm3V0":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as the accessible deep-dive on EUV lithography and why chip manufacturing is so hard.","q":{"x":"The whole process was deep magic to me before I watched that video.","i":47866125,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48008307,"author":"btown","month":"2026-05","story":"ASML's Best Selling Product Isn't What You Think It Is","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows engineering behind EUV lithography at scale for 3nm chips","quote":"I highly recommend this recent (Dec 2025) Veritasium video about them","text":"For those interested in ASML, and how much engineering goes into being able to make EUV lithography work reliably at scale for 3nm nodes (by hitting individual droplets of tin with highly accurate lasers and turning them into plasma!), I highly recommend this recent (Dec 2025) Veritasium video about them: [VIDEO] For more: [LINK] (There's something incredible and downright \"alchemical\" about the fact that the reason our sand can \"think\" is because we use light to vaporize tin which then carves intricate glyphs into the sand!)"},{"id":47866125,"author":"terlisimo","month":"2026-04","story":"Making RAM at Home [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how EUV chip manufacturing works","quote":"The whole process was deep magic to me before I watched that video.","text":"The whole process was deep magic to me before I watched that video. It didn't seem less magical after I've watched it. More so, if anything. Asianometry[0] has a number of videos on EUV lithography that cover some of the mind-blowing advances in the years since. Veritasium[1] recently also made a video on the subject. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47937820,"author":"random3","month":"2026-04","story":"The World's Most Complex Machine","stance":"rec","why":"Story of ASML and its long-term vision","quote":"Veritasium has a nice video about it","text":"Veritasium has a nice video about it [VIDEO] Obviously, there are a lot of reasons why. But it boils down to having the vision, the belief and the strength to follow through over many years. It's important to not confound vision with random Kool-Aid. Instead it's grounded in research. That research is itself grounded in a strong vision and belief — it got laughed by the entire physics community at the time: > 'people seemed unwilling to believe bending x-rays, and they tended to that we had actually made an image by regard the whole thing as a big fish story Now contrast this with the current academic reality — \"publish or perish\" and the reality of venture financing and corporate culture that \"depends\" (arguably in self-inflicted manner, that's not 100% the case) on quarterly repots. ASML is just a recent story, but if you look back, you'll see that most revolutions have a similar pattern of people crazy enough to deviate from the herd. The rest— the immense financial risk, the 5000 suppliers, etc. came as a result of having the ability to see through all the noise and the grit to to follow through when everyone calls you an idiot for not doing something \"useful\""},{"id":46559577,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2026-01","story":"AI Coding Assistants Are Getting Worse","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on ASML machine design","quote":"Veritasium recently made a good video on the ASML machine design","text":"ASML's secret sauce is not that secret or uncopyable. The Chinese are already working on their clone of the Twinscan tools. Veritasium recently made a good video on the ASML machine design: [VIDEO] The outcome may seem like magic, but the input is \"simply\" hard work and a big budget: billions of dollars and years of investment into tuning the parameters like droplet size, frequency, etc... The interviews make it clear that the real reason ASML's machines are (currently) unique is that few people had the vision, patience, and money to fund what seemed at the time impossible. The real magic was that ASML managed to hang on by a fingernail and get a successful result before the money ran out. Now that tin droplet EUV lasers have not only been demonstrated to be possible, but have become the essential component of a hugely profitable AI chip manufacturing industry, obtaining funding to develop a clone will be much easier."},{"id":46646018,"author":"joncrocks","month":"2026-01","story":"Apple Is Fighting for TSMC Capacity as Nvidia Takes Center Stage","stance":"rec","why":"Great video on the company and cutting-edge lithography","quote":"There was a great video recently on the company + techniques used for cutting-edge lithography.","text":"Do you mean AMSL? There was a great video recently on the company + techniques used for cutting-edge lithography. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47937745,"author":"rufo","month":"2026-04","story":"The World's Most Complex Machine","stance":"reference","why":"Covers ASML EUV machines as most complex commercial machine","quote":"I just watched the Vertasium video[1] on ASML's EUV lithgoraphy machines over the weekend","text":"I just watched the Vertasium video[1] on ASML's EUV lithgoraphy machines over the weekend, and I think the qualifier they used was \"most complex machine _you can purchase commercially_\". I can't remember if it was an ASML representative that said that, or if it was an overlaid asterisk that popped up on the screen at some point - but I definitely remember thinking about the space shuttle and Saturn V/Apollo and those sorts of things before I saw the qualifier. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47387313,"author":"naasking","month":"2026-03","story":"Qatar helium shutdown puts chip supply chain on a two-week clock","stance":"reference","why":"In-depth review of ASML's EUV lithography machines","quote":"Here is the Veritasium video where they review ASML's EUV lithography in depth","text":"Here is the Veritasium video where they review ASML's EUV lithography in depth: [VIDEO] High thermal conductivity, inertness, and low absorption of the EUV wavelength used are the primary characteristics needed, and purity is needed to eliminate contamination of numerous ultrasensitive stages. AI summarized it as follows: EUV tools require ultra‑high‑purity helium, typically grade 9N (99.9999999%) or better, with strict ppb‑level limits on: Nitrogen, Oxygen, Moisture, Hydrocarbons, Refractory compounds, Particulates. How helium is used: - Cooling the EUV plasma source - Purging and protecting optics - Maintaining inert, ultra‑clean environments - Leak detection"},{"id":46945635,"author":"lloeki","month":"2026-02","story":"Nobody knows how the whole system works","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates civilization-scale complexity in making microprocessors","quote":"I recently watched this","text":"> This is an important distinction because there are things like microprocessors, which no single person knows how to make. But also: no single person could alone build something that has anywhere near the same capability I recently watched this: [VIDEO] The levels of advanced _whatever_ that we've reached is absurdly bonkers. It seems to me that at some point in the last 50 or so years the world went from \"given a lot of time I can make a _crude_ but reasonably functional version of whatever XYZ in my garage\" to \"it requires the structural backbone of a whole civilization to achieve XYZ\". Of course it's sort of a delusion. Maybe it's more about the ramp appearing more exponential than ever."},{"id":46968121,"author":"measurablefunc","month":"2026-02","story":"The Singularity will occur on a Tuesday","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for how supercomputer-class chips are made","quote":"","text":"Good point. OP was saying \"no one knows\" when in fact plenty of people do know but people also often conflate knowing & understanding w/o realizing that's what they're doing. People who have studied programming, electrical engineering, ultraviolet lithography, quantum mechanics, & so on know what is going on inside the computer but that's different from saying they understand billions of transistors b/c no one really understands billions of transistors even though a single transistor is understood well enough to be manufactured in large enough quantities that almost anyone who wants to can have the equivalent of a supercomputer in their pocket for less than $1k: [VIDEO] . Somewhere along the way from one transistor to a few billion human understanding stops but we still know how it was all assembled together to perform boolean arithmetic operations."},{"id":47130809,"author":"m4rtink","month":"2026-02","story":"ASML unveils EUV light source advance that could yield 50% more chips by 2030","stance":"reference","why":"Hydrogen gas flow carries away tin debris from mirrors","quote":"there is actually some hydrogen gas flowing at quite a high speed though the laser chamber to carry away the tin debris","text":"IIRC from the Veritasium video[0] there is actually some hydrogen gas flowing at quite a high speed though the laser chamber to carry away the tin debris so that it does not accumulate on the mirrors. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47136740,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2026-02","story":"ASML unveils EUV light source advance that could yield 50% more chips by 2030","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the si (along with pp ) parameter is used for tracking purposes","text":"> [VIDEO] PSA: the si (along with pp ) parameter is used for tracking purposes: ?si=kEPSicC2WXYhcQ6L consider cutting whenever possible."}],"r":[["kIoZDUd5DKw",1],["GC-0tCy4P1U",1],["O133ppiVnWY",1],["kPR8h4-qZdk",1],["SFv8Wm2HdNM",1],["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["SDtAh9IwG-I",1],["LIb3L4vKZ7U",1],["_Cp-BGQfpHQ",1],["ig_qpNfXHIU",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["koTf7u0v41o",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["GuigptwlVHo",1],["0rR9IaXH1M0",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["99dKzubvpKE",1],["6wBrOV2FJM8",1],["5IUj1EZwpJY",1]]},"vJG698U2Mvo":{"wy":"HN uses it constantly as the classic demonstration that attention is selective, in threads on driving, motorcycles, AI and perception.","q":{"x":"a longer but more relevant \"motorcycle invisibility training\" video","i":21157588,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21157588,"author":"chrisweekly","month":"2019-10","story":"Lethal memory fail: Why drivers see, and then forget motorcyclists","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short demonstration that is eye-opening for first-time viewers","quote":"for many first-time viewers, it's an eye-opening experience.","text":"As a motorcyclist -- and lifelong learner w/ a degree in cognitive psychology -- I really appreciate demonstrations like this one^1 from Daniel Simons. It's a short video (< 90s), but for many first-time viewers, it's an eye-opening experience. ^1. [VIDEO] Edit: just saw @elosarv's link to a longer but more relevant \"motorcycle invisibility training\" video ( [LINK]"},{"id":41223298,"author":"meowster","month":"2024-08","story":"Massachusetts 'Reviewing' Kei Car Ban After Uproar over Canceled Registrations","stance":"rec","why":"Short test showing people see what they expect","quote":"Watch/take this Selective Attention test (1 min 22 sec)","text":"It's called Selective Attention which explained in another comment: > Notably, people tend to see what they expect to see. > When they are looking for cars, they tend not to see motorcycles, even when they are in plain view. Watch/take this Selective Attention test (1 min 22 sec): [VIDEO]"},{"id":17908507,"author":"crispyambulance","month":"2018-09","story":"A Fighter Pilot’s Guide to Surviving on the Roads","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates selective attention relevant to drivers missing cyclists","quote":"Human attention is very much selective. Check out this shocking video","text":"This is sound advice. The only time I've made contact with a moving car as a cyclist was when I was going past an off-ramp. The weird thing was that I thought the driver \"saw me\". It was late morning, no problem with visibility. From my POV, she was looking right at me. In fact, she was looking \"through\" me. She simply was expecting other cars and a person on a bike was not what she was looking for. Human attention is very much selective. Check out this shocking video: [VIDEO] After that, I ride with a bright flashing headlight at all times, even daylight. It gives some measure of protection, I think."},{"id":44627052,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2025-07","story":"How Tesla is proving doubters right on why its robotaxi service cannot scale","stance":"reference","why":"Shows human limits tracking multiple objects","quote":"","text":"It is true most humans can not track more than 5 objects concurrently. However, lidar data sets tend to produce sparse unambiguous point cloud data that is a lot more feasible to process. For example, a picture/reflection of a road on a bus is not a viable option with 3D data. Cheers =3 [VIDEO]"},{"id":42986529,"author":"rsanek","month":"2025-02","story":"Are LLMs able to notice the “gorilla in the data”?","stance":"reference","why":"Classic human parallel to LLMs missing gorilla in data","quote":"Humans seem to me to be just as likely to make these kinds of errors in the general case.","text":"Humans seem to me to be just as likely to make these kinds of errors in the general case. See the classic [VIDEO] , which has an interesting parallel with this paper."},{"id":42699694,"author":"RandomBacon","month":"2025-01","story":"Making an intersection unsafe for pedestrians to save seconds for drivers","stance":"reference","why":"Used to argue people miss things in plain view","quote":"Have you ever taken the selective attention test?","text":"> maybe you shouldn't dress like that if you don't want the attention. Have you ever taken the selective attention test? [VIDEO] (short 1:22 video)"},{"id":42704198,"author":"nyrikki","month":"2025-01","story":"Allstate used GasBuddy and other apps to track driving behavior: lawsuit","stance":"reference","why":"Classic basketball example of selective attention","quote":"The classic basketball game example of selective attention if you don't buy it.","text":"Never made that claim, but dawn and dusk are the most risky. Mine happened on a lunch break, can't see through rock either. But you only see a fraction of the wildlife that is there. If your sole stratagy is to see them you may be surprised how many deer hit the sides of cars. Complacency and selective attention are very real human problems. The classic basketball game example of selective attention if you don't buy it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41744390,"author":"jetrink","month":"2024-10","story":"Meta Movie Gen","stance":"reference","why":"Famous example of missing details when not looking","quote":"it's amazing what details we can miss when we aren't looking for them.","text":"A friend who lives in North Carolina sent me a video of the raging floodwaters in his state- at least that's what the superimposed text claimed it was. When I looked closer, it was clearly an Indian city filled with Indian people and Indian cars. He hadn't noticed anything except the flood water. It reminded me of that famous selective attention test video[1]. I won't ruin it for those who haven't seen it, but it's amazing what details we can miss when we aren't looking for them. I suspect this is made even worse when we're casually viewing videos in a disjointed way as on social media and we're not even giving one part of the video our full attention. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39824625,"author":"Retr0id","month":"2024-03","story":"Chess puzzle, but you are what you capture","stance":"reference","why":"Analogy for missing obstacles in a puzzle","quote":"in a way that reminded me of the Selective Attention Test[0]","text":"Yup, you can't move to them. Weirdly I didn't notice them at all until several attempts in (and was getting confused as to why certain moves weren't allowed), in a way that reminded me of the Selective Attention Test[0]. Once I'd clocked them I was able to solve it pretty quickly. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38587808,"author":"BoppreH","month":"2023-12","story":"Three Things That LLMs Have Made Us Rethink","stance":"reference","why":"Example of humans ignoring prominent stimuli","quote":"accidentally ignore surprisingly prominent stimuli[2]","text":"> it doesn't understand its own limitations, ie it has no self awareness. And how much self awareness do humans have, exactly? We walk around with heads filled with false memories[1], accidentally ignore surprisingly prominent stimuli[2], and struggle to understand things as simple as material color[3]. And people are mostly unaware of these limitations, or those examples would not be considered surprising! I think we plan around the limitations we observe, not our real limitations. If that's the case, then LLMs simply lack training data to notice those limitations. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] (old selective attention test) [3] [LINK]"},{"id":36392748,"author":"sankyo","month":"2023-06","story":"Invisibility Training for Motorcyclists [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Classic psychology video relevant to motorcycle invisibility","quote":"This old psychology video comes to mind.","text":"This old psychology video comes to mind. [VIDEO] Also, in San Francisco a lot of the \"paint\" in the crosswalks is little plastic pellets melted. I have had my rear wheel slide out going over the thick white crosswalk bars. Best to keep off the throttle and roll over them. Same for the giant metal plates they use to cover up ongoing construction projects. They can be very slippery."},{"id":32018149,"author":"digitalengineer","month":"2022-07","story":"Why do people not notice our enormous, prominent, clear and contrasting banner?","stance":"reference","why":"Example that what we see depends on task","quote":"Case in point: dancing gorilla awareness test,","text":"Yes. Eyetracking is rather useless as what we see depends on the task we have to perform. We filter out what we don't need and don't even register it consciously. As such, general eye tracking studies can only tell you so much... Case in point: dancing gorilla awareness test, [VIDEO] ."},{"id":26408218,"author":"aaron695","month":"2021-03","story":"Drivers new to level 2 driving assistance had lower awareness of on-road events","stance":"reference","why":"Inattentional blindness study related to driver awareness","quote":"Gorillas in our midst: sustained inattentional blindness for dynamic events","text":"The moonwalking bear/gorilla comes to mind for this. Gorillas in our midst: sustained inattentional blindness for dynamic events - [VIDEO] / [LINK] But I'm not sure what that means, is it good or bad to see the bear. You are driving not looking at pretty things. You want to reduce dimensions. It's a 1 tonne vehicle, color shouldn't matter."},{"id":22230378,"author":"tmh88j","month":"2020-02","story":"Tesla's self driving algorithm's overlay [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Selective attention can catch viewers off guard","quote":"I recall it catching me off guard the first time I saw it.","text":"> You see a handful of lines and text across the screen and it's sufficient to make you go \":o\"? Enough to conclude it must be better than a brain? I think he's referring to selective attention. Can't comment on the validity of this \"study\", but I recall it catching me off guard the first time I saw it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22232144,"author":"jfim","month":"2020-02","story":"Tesla's self driving algorithm's overlay [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows humans perceive differently from computers","quote":"The selective attention test [0] really shows how humans can perceive certain things in a way that's different from how computers perceive them.","text":"Oh definitely. The selective attention test [0] really shows how humans can perceive certain things in a way that's different from how computers perceive them. The research on GANs also shows how computers can be fooled by things that wouldn't confuse humans. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14406868,"author":"JadeNB","month":"2017-05","story":"At The Dawn Of Recorded Sound, No One Cared","stance":"reference","why":"Analogy: once you know the effect you cannot unsee it","quote":"","text":"> The notion of not knowing why recording sound could be useful to me is hard to grasp as someone who has always been around it. This seems like (from their point of view) the Blub paradox ( [LINK] ), and (from our point of view) something of its converse. From their point of view, they couldn't see the importance of sound recording because they didn't have any existing mechanisms to make use of it. From our point of view, we can't not see the importance of sound recording, because we have so many ways of making use of it. (Kind of like how, once you know the effect being demonstrated in [VIDEO] , you not only no longer experience it, but find it hard to believe that you ever did.)"},{"id":10901824,"author":"cooper12","month":"2016-01","story":"A Canvas Made of Pixels","stance":"reference","why":"Selective attention test related to discussion","quote":"Your video reminded me of this selective attention test","text":"Your video reminded me of this selective attention test: [VIDEO] . This Wikipedia article also seems to be related to the phenomenon and talks about the video too: [LINK] ."},{"id":7483689,"author":"Someone","month":"2014-03","story":"Gunshot victims to be suspended between life and death","stance":"reference","why":"Shows vision is not as continuous as it feels","quote":"","text":"I am not sure we even know that normal consciousness is continuous. We experience it that way, but that says nothing. We think our vision is continuous, too, but it isn't ( [VIDEO] . More info at [LINK] (had to use a shortener because the URL gives information away for those who don't know this experiment))"},{"id":2290534,"author":"brisance","month":"2011-03","story":"Multitasking","stance":"reference","why":"Selective attention experiment supporting focus over multitasking","quote":"Refer to Daniel Simons's experiment (the invisible gorilla) on selective attention.","text":"However, the argument that multitasking on computers is bad because humans can’t multitask is flawed. That was right in the article itself. I believe the author's point is that focus/attention is more important than being able to \"multi-task\". Refer to Daniel Simons's experiment (the invisible gorilla) on selective attention. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39459244,"author":"JieJie","month":"2024-02","story":"The killer app of Gemini Pro 1.5 is using video as an input","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Sure, but does it pass the Selective Attention Test?","text":"Sure, but does it pass the Selective Attention Test? [VIDEO] (I don't know, I don't have access.)"}],"r":[["HXAW2GseICs",1],["-6rWqJhDv7M",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["x94PGgYKHQ0",1]]},"4YYvBLAF4T8":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an interesting talk on how weak real-world door security is, and cites it for the stories of bad doors.","q":{"x":"Heard it recently and thought it was really interesting.","i":18217891,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18741370,"author":"lathiat","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: Best talks of 2018?","stance":"rec","why":"One of Deviant Ollam's talks","quote":"And the search for the perfect door","text":"Deviant Ollam - The Hotel Room Gourmet [LINK] Also not 2018 but his elevator with Howard Payne talk: [LINK] (1h version) [LINK] (2h version) And the search for the perfect door: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18217891,"author":"winrid","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting talk heard recently","quote":"Heard it recently and thought it was really interesting.","text":"I hope this isn't too much of a stretch. Heard it recently and thought it was really interesting. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15198605,"author":"lathiat","month":"2017-09","story":"Evan Doorbell's Classic Phreaking Tapes","stance":"rec","why":"Ways into buildings without picking locks","quote":"This is a good related talk - goes outside the box on all the ways you can often get into commercial buildings","text":"I realise the thread is phone phreaking related and that was kind of the point of your post; but in the spirit of the talk below to think more outside the box... There's a pretty good chance in many cases you can just open the external box with some easy to get (and relatively standard) key. And hit a button. Related: [LINK] This is a good related talk - goes outside the box on all the ways you can often get into commercial buildings without picking (or even in many cases, interacting with) the lock \"The Search for the Perfect Door\" by Deviant Ollam [VIDEO] Also good: [LINK] [Howard Payne & Deviant Ollam - This Key is Your Key, This Key is My Key] [LINK] [DEF CON 22 - Deviant Ollam & Howard Payne - Elevator Hacking - From the Pit to the Penthouse]"},{"id":37963509,"author":"OmarAssadi","month":"2023-10","story":"How to mitigate the Hetzner/Linode XMPP.ru MitM interception incident","stance":"reference","why":"Deviant Ollam's stories on bad doors","quote":"bad doors [2]","text":"Assuming this was done at the government's request, I assume Hetzner is more than willing to comply with a court order mandating they allow them to monitor and physically size a machine. And outside of nation-state requests, even ignoring the fact that someone could probably pay-off an employee, I think ease would depend a lot on the datacenter and target; judging by the awesome and hilarious story behind the Fremont Cabal accidentally becoming an internet exchange by essentially having some dude barely secretly slipping unauthorized cables into the raceways [1], I figure there are a lot of places where if your target is simply renting a couple rack units or single rack rather than an entire locked cage, you can probably get physical access by doing the same. Also, a lot of Deviant Ollam's stories about industrial security and the dozens of ways he's broken into utility companies, server rooms, etc — mostly just by being confident, looking the part, bad doors [2], and badge cloning [3] — don't give me a ton of confidence that someone with skill couldn't feasibly either get direct access to servers they shouldn't, or at the very least, access to an important part of the supply chain for their target. And speaking of supply chain, my processor died recently, so I ordered a brand-new in box replacement Ryzen, and when it arrived last night, out of curiosity, I wanted to see if I could get the CPU out of the box without breaking the tamper-evident authenticity seal... ... and abo"},{"id":15517946,"author":"goialoq","month":"2017-10","story":"Socially Engineering Myself into High Security Facilities","stance":"reference","why":"Door lock security talk","quote":"locks are bad, and doors are awful","text":"Someone leaves their computer unlocked when they walk away -> network access. door locks: locks are bad, and doors are awful [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["yT9bit2-1pg",1],["oHf1vD5_b5I",1],["QMiubC6LdTA",1],["NSuaUok-wTY",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["noQcWra6sbU",1],["OpEB6hCpIGM",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["uYbdx4I7STg",1],["ra_9zU-mnl8",1],["a9b9IYqsb_U",1],["rNu8XDBSn10",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["2KZb2_vcNTg",1],["NmGaXEmfTIo",1],["ZUvGfuLlZus",1],["DOWDNBu9DkU",1]]},"6p8zAbFKpW0":{"wy":"HN points to it as an informative explainer on why many carbon offset schemes are not genuine.","q":{"x":"John Oliver with a quite informative video about why many carbon offsets/carbon credits schemes are most likely not genuine","i":36305749,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37663232,"author":"joshvm","month":"2023-09","story":"Investigation: 78% of carbon offset projects globally are “likely junk“","stance":"rec","why":"Good segment on complexities of carbon credits.","quote":"It's complicated, but John Oliver did a good segment on it.","text":"It's complicated, but John Oliver did a good segment on it. [VIDEO] Planting trees is not an easy solution. [LINK] A major problem is that monitoring and verification is patchy. A carbon credit is in theory something like X ton-years, but there is no guarantee that what you pay for will be around in X years and policing us extremely difficult. X is traditionally 100 years. If I sell you a carbon credit and then cut all my trees down, what happens? Another is fraudulent sites - people selling credits for sites that already exist, we're already planned for protection, etc."},{"id":36305749,"author":"d4nyll","month":"2023-06","story":"","stance":"rec","why":"Informative on why many offset schemes are not genuine.","quote":"John Oliver with a quite informative video about why many carbon offsets/carbon credits schemes are most likely not genuine","text":"John Oliver with a quite informative video about why many carbon offsets/carbon credits schemes are most likely not genuine in their attempts to offset carbon emissions. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32783910,"author":"russdill","month":"2022-09","story":"Companies are buying large numbers of carbon offsets that don’t cut emissions","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why carbon offsets are ineffective given perverse incentives.","quote":"A John Oliver explainer","text":"In theory, it's great. In practice perverse incentives and little to no regulation make it worthless. A John Oliver explainer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37661629,"author":"moffkalast","month":"2023-09","story":"Investigation: 78% of carbon offset projects globally are “likely junk“","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier coverage of the same carbon offset problem.","quote":"John Oliver had a piece on that [0] over a year ago.","text":"John Oliver had a piece on that [0] over a year ago. Companies want the most CO2 offset per dollar to present themselves as neutral as per their greenwashing campaign, so naturally they buy those from the lowest bidder. And those are the ones least likely to be legit, so it's mostly only scams that get the business lmao. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34297176,"author":"cableshaft","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Plans for Weathering the Tech Recession?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for issues with carbon offsets.","quote":"there's various issues with carbon offsets that John Oliver goes into here[2]","text":"Supposedly Cleantech/ClimateTech is hiring a lot now[1], according to some articles I saw pop up recently. I did try to investigate Cleantech jobs last cycle, back in mid 2021, and didn't have a great experience, personally. Most of the jobs posted seemed to fall into one of two buckets: 1 - Startups that \"helped companies go carbon neutral\", i.e. they accept money from the business, skim some off the top (for their business), and funnel it towards various clean initiatives, then send some certificate to the company showing they've offset X amount of carbon. It just doesn't seem much different than a grift to me, and there's various issues with carbon offsets that John Oliver goes into here[2]. Also regardless of whether it's a grift or not, that sounds like totally uninteresting tech to work on. 2 - Startups that bill themselves as helping companies get more efficient, i.e. help improve their logistics or make their electric grids \"smart\" to save energy, etc. Problem with that is that already seems like something a company wants and tries to do anyway, and while yes in the short term it does seem like that results in wasting less energy and saves resources, in the long term, it just allows the company to ramp up further and use even more energy and resources. Like two companies with the most efficient logistics in the world are Amazon and Walmart, and that just let them become even more massive. So wading thruogh all that to find more interesting Cleantech companies I found "}],"r":[["fWqBmmPQP40",1],["fzBRYsiyxjI",1]]},"CaasbfdJdJg":{"wy":"Recommended as a clear, visual explanation of continued fractions and why phi is hardest to approximate by fractions.","q":{"x":"Pretty good Mathologer video about continued fractions","i":37134537,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37134537,"author":"uoaei","month":"2023-08","story":"Decimal to Fraction","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation of continued fractions and most irrational number.","quote":"Pretty good Mathologer video about continued fractions","text":"Pretty good Mathologer video about continued fractions: [VIDEO] One neat trivium I like to quiz the mathematically-minded on is: what number is the most irrational , i.e., toward which number do rational approximations converge the slowest ? The answer can be determined from continued fractions and is discussed in the video near the end."},{"id":33277188,"author":"jerf","month":"2022-10","story":"The Mystery of 355/113 (2010)","stance":"rec","why":"Justifies calling phi most irrational via its continued fraction.","quote":"The video also goes over the details of the pi expansion.","text":"\"same with golden ratio (repeating 1s)\" Mathologer has a good video in which he uses this to justify calling the golden ratio the \"most irrational\" number because of this expansion: [VIDEO] The video also goes over the details of the pi expansion."},{"id":29015820,"author":"rrauenza","month":"2021-10","story":"Where transcendental numbers hide in everyday math","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting Mathologer video on the most irrational number.","quote":"But its still a really interesting video!","text":"I was going to post this Mathologer video ( [VIDEO] ) based on e being \"the most irrational number\" -- but I misremembered. It's the golden ratio, phi. But its still a really interesting video!"},{"id":46792656,"author":"AceJohnny2","month":"2026-01","story":"Golden Ratio using an equilateral triangle inscribed in a circle","stance":"reference","why":"Found the video explaining golden ratio as continued fraction limit.","quote":"Edit: years of searches and minutes after I post this I found","text":"Edit: years of searches and minutes after I post this I found [VIDEO] thanks to using \"continued fraction\" in my search instead of \"infinite series\" X( Original: Tangentially, for a few years I've been looking for a Youtube video, I think by Mathologer [1], that explained (geometrically?) how the Golden Ratio was the limit of the continued fraction 1+1/(1+1/(1+1/(...))). Anyone know what I'm talking about? I know Mathologer had a conflict with his editor at one point that may have sown chaos on his channel. [1] [LINK]"},{"id":20849177,"author":"std_throwaway","month":"2019-08","story":"Golden Ratio","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why the golden ratio is most irrational.","quote":"The golden ratio is the most irrational number","text":"The golden ratio is the most irrational number: [VIDEO] This is why it creates some distinctive patterns when applied to circular/spiral shapes."}],"r":[["vs41JrnGaxc",1],["S2xHZPH5Sng",1],["t-O-Qdh7VvQ",1],["oI_X2cMHNe0",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["bvim4rsNHkQ",1],["yY9GAyJtuJ0",1],["bHIhgxav9LY",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["pTn6Ewhb27k",1],["r6Rp-uo6HmI",1],["RIctzAQOe44",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["bL6hp8BKB24",1],["yL_-1d9OSdk",1],["kPRA0W1kECg",1],["cefnmjtAolY",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["GFMyMxMYDNk",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1]]},"JxI3Eu5DPwE":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a level-headed counterpoint to ECS zealotry and a good intro to game architecture beyond ECS.","q":{"x":"Bob Nystrom has an excellent talk on roguelike architecture [0]","i":28862788,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":48128243,"author":"marcingas","month":"2026-05","story":"Xs of Y – roguelike that names itself every run. Written in 4kLoC","stance":"rec","why":"Good general talk on roguelike software design; channel full of inspiration.","quote":"This is a good talk about roguelike software design in general","text":"I'd say don't immediately go into making an ECS and don't sweat about perf too much. You can totally do this in any programming language. This is a good talk about roguelike software design in general: [VIDEO] Tbh the whole channel is a gold mine of inspiration. Also check: [LINK]"},{"id":28862788,"author":"halvnykterist","month":"2021-10","story":"Hands-On Rust: Effective Learning Through 2D Game Development and Play","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent talk on roguelike architecture beyond ECS.","quote":"Bob Nystrom has an excellent talk on roguelike architecture [0]","text":"I can't help but be heavily skeptical of approaches to a (traditional) roguelike that use ECS. The idea is very entrenched in the rust gamedev community, but for a turn based tile based game there's extremely little benefit and a lot of added complexity. Bob Nystrom has an excellent talk on roguelike architecture [0] and rust as a language itself doesn't prevent any of these approaches. If anything, the existence of sum types as enums make many of them all the more powerful. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24507622,"author":"hu3","month":"2020-09","story":"An Introduction to Data Oriented Design with Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Good starting point on how game development handles data-oriented design","quote":"Here's a good start","text":"This is known and heavily used by the game industry for a long time now. Ultimately it comes down to data locality and there's no silver bullet. Performance varies with your data access pattern and code must be adapted to keep caches as warm as possible. There are also maintainability trade-offs. In general I'd advise this kind of optimization only for performance-critical code like games. For more information on how game development approaches this, read about Entity Component Systems. Here's a good start: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46099598,"author":"sunrunner","month":"2025-11","story":"Langjam Gamejam: Build a programming language then make a game with it","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as one of Nystrom's works alongside his books.","quote":"","text":"The crossover of language design/implementation and game development made me assume this was either a Jonathon Blow (Jai, Braid, The Witness) or Bob Nystrom project (Game Programming Patterns, Crafting Interpreters, 'Is There More to Game Architecture than ECS?' [1]). Either way, given the time constraints and requirements I'm expecting a lot of text adventures and rogue-likes. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38584283,"author":"Xeamek","month":"2023-12","story":"Murder is a pixel art ECS game engine in C#","stance":"reference","why":"Related talk on game architecture alternatives to ECS.","quote":"","text":"Since engine is targetting pixel art, isn't ECS a bit of an overkill...? And yes, technically artstyle has nothing to do with game architecture. But in practice, most games of this style are pretty small, and thus don't really have any real performance requirements, so ecs'ing everything is overengineering compared to oop-style, coupled architecture, no? Also related: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35437563,"author":"illiarian","month":"2023-04","story":"Flecs – A fast entity component system for C and C++","stance":"reference","why":"Talk questioning zealous ECS adherence, by the Game Programming Patterns author.","quote":"Is There More to Game Architecture Than ECS","text":"I've also seen a few game devs saying ECS are useless in quite a few situations (or, at least, a very zealous adherence to ECS). I guess it's one of those things that you don't know until you try (and hit the limits of using or not-using one). I'm also reminded of Robert Nystrom's \"Is There More to Game Architecture Than ECS\": [VIDEO] (Yes, Robert Nystrom of Game Programming Patterns and Crafting Interpreters )"},{"id":28449020,"author":"Rd6n6","month":"2021-09","story":"Unity patents ECS","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as an older talk mentioning ECS, as prior art.","quote":"Bob Nystrom (the game programming patterns guy) that mentions ecs from years ago","text":"Isn’t there a ton of prior art? I’ve seen tech talks about ecs implementations for years. From the top of my head, here’s a great tech talk about overwatch. [LINK] Here’s a tech talk by Bob Nystrom (the game programming patterns guy) that mentions ecs from years ago [VIDEO] Edit: letsInvalid in another thread above linked to an article from 2013: [LINK]"}],"r":[["HxaD_trXwRE",1],["stxVBJem3Rs",1]]},"KNWh6Kw3ejQ":{"wy":"HN cites it as a quick, entertaining explainer to correct the myth of the frivolous coffee lawsuit.","m":[{"id":37704358,"author":"ttfkam","month":"2023-09","story":"Woman sues McDonald's after she's burned by hot coffee, echoing 1994 case","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining overview of the real facts of the McDonald's coffee lawsuit.","quote":"","text":"> I read it far enough Clearly not since the crux of both lawsuits has nothing to do with the build quality of cups or lids. Entertaining overview: [VIDEO] Lawyer's examination: [LINK] Whichever you prefer. Did you know the woman who sued so many years ago just wanted her burn injuries paid for, not a multi-million dollar payout? The coffee is brewed at such a high temperature, it could not be consumed when delivered to the customer. It was a known issue for many years with repeated (broken) promises to fix it. Rather than immediate consumption, the high temperature was targeted for folks to buy it, put it in a cup holder, drive to work, and then drink it upon arrival, by which time it would have presumably cooled to a drinkable temperature. For this use case it worked. However at the point of sale it is hot enough to cause third-degree burns through clothing within two seconds. If you spill it in a car, your mobility to deal with such a burning hazard is also predictably quite limited. Your clothing keeps the hot liquid next to your skin, prevents the heat from radiating away as quickly, and the seat belt restricts movement even further even if the car is not in motion. Liquid hot enough to cause third-degree burns in two seconds in a confined space is absolutely considered a foreseeable hazard for the vendor under laws regarding liability, especially after they were given multiple warnings prior to injury. \"But I want hotter coffee, so it's still hot when I get to work,\" yo"},{"id":16581153,"author":"SlowBro","month":"2018-03","story":"Decoding the Design History of Your Coffee Cup Lid","stance":"rec","why":"Clip explaining the truth about the McDonald's coffee lawsuit.","quote":"","text":"They mentioned the coffee lawsuit. I heard about that one many times while working at Dunkin Donuts some 22 years ago. The customers were always (jokingly) threatening to burn themselves and sue the company for millions. I thought that lady was greedy until learning the truth much later. An Adam Ruins Everything clip explains: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15700585,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2017-11","story":"Tesla Is a 'Hotbed for Racist Behavior,' Worker Claims in Suit","stance":"rec","why":"Good explainer on the McDonald's coffee lawsuit and its media framing.","quote":"","text":"The McDonald's case was most likely intentional though. They're a big company and most likely pushed the media that direction as damage control. There's a good Adam Ruins Everything video on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16228305,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2018-01","story":"Burger King Has an Opinion on Net Neutrality","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of a corporate PR campaign against a legitimate lawsuit.","quote":"","text":"Special interest groups already dictate policy. There have been actual studies on that, and I did a video on one of them a while back: [LINK] I'm currently working on a post called \"You can't vote with your purchases\" which is something I feel a lot of libertarians and capitalists spew out. Don't like Wal-Mart? Don't shop there. The trouble is you really can't. Edward Bernays and Anna Freud really changed the game when they started teaching companies how to market to people emotionally to draw them in. I know tons of people, myself included, who don't shop at Wal-Mart, but it doesn't make a dent. Wal-Mart can still manipulate millions of people with their extensive ad campaigns, and most people simply don't care. Tobacco companies got women to smoke more by disusing it as being part of women's rights (look up Torches of Freedom). McDonalds led a PR campaign against \"frivolous lawsuits\" from the women who got 3rd degree, life threatening burns from their coffee, that people still believe to this day ( [VIDEO] ). The Baptist church didn't put a dent in Disney by boycotting them. Even MLKings Montgomery bus boycotts didn't succeed due to financial pressure. They succeeded because the news and media attention got so huge that the Supreme Court had to step in. Every company does something unethical. Your processors are manufactured in Malaysia by people who get paid barely enough to live on, your shoes are made in Africa by companies driving people off their land and into the citi"},{"id":14576935,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2017-06","story":"Instacart Tries to Hang on to Whole Foods as Amazon Swoops In","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the real facts behind the McDonald's coffee lawsuit.","quote":"","text":"I too stopped shopping at Amazon a little over a year ago after returning to the US. I stopped shopping at Wal-Mart in 2009 and simply see Amazon as the new Wal-Mart. But you and I and everyone else who decides not the shop there; we don't make a dent. You cannot vote with your dollars. There are still way too many people who don't care or who follow the advertising. It's the reason why Uber will be around for decades. Public Relations/Propaganda/Marketing is amazing. McDonalds convinced millions of Americans the girl who sued for spilling coffee on herself had fought and won a frivolous lawsuit when the reality was far from the truth: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14045665,"author":"tajen","month":"2017-04","story":"Uber finds one allegedly stolen Waymo file on an employee’s personal device","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the McDonald's coffee lawsuit example.","quote":"","text":"I'm happy some people still think like you. The respect for trials was lost at one point in recent history (and I don't know whether the million-dollar coffee and counter-argument explains it [1]), but the theory of justice is that the judge determines what is fair. I'm especially disgusted that so many men lose their jobs upon women's accusations: Without a judge, I can't verify that said men were guilty or just that a woman leveraged the system. And this happens with many other types of suspicion. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["uI1eRy0uBI8",1],["fwjwePe-HmA",1],["pnv5iKB2hl4",1],["LrObZ_HZZUc",1],["BzB5xtGGsTc",1],["-HFwok9SlQQ",1],["niKblgZupOc",1],["35Idb_lCU4o",1],["dBnniua6-oM",1],["Eco2s3-0zsQ",1],["uDqjIdI4bF4",1]]},"NAh9oLs67Cw":{"wy":"HN cites it as the prime example of going absurdly deep on a tiny subject, and as a favorite among long YouTube videos.","q":{"x":"I love this deep absurd analysis of a single Garfield comic.","i":36121487,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36121487,"author":"adzm","month":"2023-05","story":"The Odd Appeal Of Absurdly Long YouTube Videos","stance":"rec","why":"Hour-long absurd analysis of one Garfield comic.","quote":"I love this deep absurd analysis of a single Garfield comic.","text":"It's only an hour, but I love this deep absurd analysis of a single Garfield comic. An hour long, set to the music of Kundun by Philip Glass. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35413912,"author":"adzm","month":"2023-04","story":"If Philip Glass Were a Film","stance":"rec","why":"Lasagna Cat episode using the Glass score.","quote":"which is also a great watch.","text":"I never realized this was also the score used in the 07/27/1978 episode of Lasagna Cat [0] which is also a great watch. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25064750,"author":"bdowling","month":"2020-11","story":"Why's (Poignant) Guide to Ruby (2004)","stance":"rec","why":"Hour-long monologue on finding meaning in a Garfield strip.","quote":"My favorite may be this one, a one-hour single-take monologue about finding the meaning of life in a Garfield strip.","text":"I prefer `lasagnacat` for my existential/surreal Garfield riffs. [LINK] My favorite may be this one, a one-hour single-take monologue about finding the meaning of life in a Garfield strip. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27121813,"author":"kickscondor","month":"2021-05","story":"●●|●●●●●|●●|●","stance":"reference","why":"Another example of going deep on a small subject.","quote":"","text":"These 'going deep' videos also bring to mind 'The Lasangacat Monologue': [VIDEO] (Originally ran across this in HN comments btw)"},{"id":25355241,"author":"yellowapple","month":"2020-12","story":"Philip Glass: My problem is people don’t believe I write symphonies (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Kundun score mentioned in connection with Glass.","quote":"","text":"I'd listened to Philip Glass quite a bit through studying music in high school (including some exposure from the drum and bugle corps scene), but hilariously enough it's his composition for Kundun that stuck with me: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48674911,"author":"Michelangelo11","month":"2026-06","story":"SoftBank 2026 AGM [pdf]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I hope lasagnacat can come back, do a special on this","text":"I love how in the slide with \"It was the goose that created value\", the goose has its wings raised. The goose is ... triumphant. Ascendant. Its arc is complete: after being valued at $0, the goose is recognized for the inexhaustible source of value it is. The goose is vindicated . And finally, in the denouement, we see the goose in serene repose. \"It is the Goose itself,\" we are told, in bold, confident lettering. This is what matters. Not the eggs. I hope lasagnacat can come back, do a special on this, and hire John Barrymore for the narration, as a spiritual sequel to their Pipe Strip video ( [VIDEO] )."}]},"OBPpRqxY8Uw":{"wy":"HN people point to it as the more detailed follow-up when articles touch on entanglement, holography and black holes.","q":{"x":"I found these lectures by Leonard Susskind very insightful","i":18938220,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":18938220,"author":"ithkuil","month":"2019-01","story":"Strangeness of Black Holes","stance":"rec","why":"Insightful Susskind lecture on what is behind black hole horizons.","quote":"I found these lectures by Leonard Susskind very insightful","text":"I found these lectures by Leonard Susskind very insightful: - Leonard Susskind on The World As Hologram ( [LINK] ) - Leonard Susskind | \"ER = EPR\" or \"What's Behind the Horizons of Black Holes?\" ( [VIDEO] ) - Entanglement and Complexity: Gravity and Quantum Mechanics ( [LINK] )"},{"id":12091439,"author":"Neeek","month":"2016-07","story":"In Search of Time’s Origin (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"More math and explanation on horizons and holography.","quote":"There's a little more math and explanation in those videos","text":"This kind of sounds like a variation/extension on Leonard Susskind's hologram reality, he mentions in these presentations and lectures[1] that a lot of the ideas came from Maldacena and the soup can (I hope that's the right video, it's been a while since I watched them, the related videos should offer more if I got it wrong). There's a little more math and explanation in those videos, just look for the ones regarding horizons and the like, though I'm afraid I can't help you with your search for papers. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10047982,"author":"vladimirralev","month":"2015-08","story":"How Quantum Randomness Saves Relativity","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed Susskind lecture on ER=EPR.","quote":"There is also more detailed lecture available here on this particular topic","text":"This is probably inspired by the recent hype about messenger lectures by Susskind [LINK] There is also more detailed lecture available here on this particular topic [VIDEO]"},{"id":36672630,"author":"hughesjj","month":"2023-07","story":"The Physicist Who’s Challenging the Quantum Orthodoxy","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I've been incessantly watching lectures in this space","text":"I'm definitely into the \"it's an emergent phenomena\" camp. I think it's inherently relational, as that's a more efficient way to encode geometry rather than space itself being a quantized \"thing\". Afaik this theory is the \"leading\"/imho most promising theory of \"quantum gravity\", that being the \"gravity = entanglement\" conjecture and the related ideas of \"Complexity= action/volume/whatever\" that susskind and many others have been developing for the past 20 so years. All that said I'm nowhere near a physicist and am probably just spewing a total misunderstanding of the situation from my armchair. That said, I've been incessantly watching lectures in this space to try to beat an understanding into my dumb dumb brain because it's super, super fucking cool. - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] - [VIDEO] - [LINK] I'm also fascinated by the idea of phase transitions, which seems to be how the laws of physics have \"evolved\" so far. It's crazy how much quantum computation is coming into play with this stuff, ex last year's Nobel prize with the bell inequality. That said I'm sure being a programmer I'm biased to think the universe is inherently computation/math."},{"id":24191078,"author":"bmh100","month":"2020-08","story":"A Twist on Schrödinger's Cat Paradox","stance":"reference","why":"Susskind on entanglement being equivalent to wormholes.","quote":"Leonard Susskind said that quantum entanglement between particles equivalent to a wormhole between black holes","text":"Leonard Susskind said that quantum entanglement between particles equivalent to a wormhole between black holes [1]. The collapse still propagates at the speed of light across the bridge. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18244565,"author":"philipov","month":"2018-10","story":"Are Black Holes Actually Dark Energy Stars?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm not an expert either, but according to the treatments I've seen on GR, the flipping of time and space inside of black holes is just an artifact of the choice of coordinates, and can be fixed by changing your coordinate system. However, there are some ideas based on holography for solving the black hole information paradox that posit that it doesn't even make sense to talk about the inside and outside of a black hole at the same time at all (black hole complementarity), that the inside of the black hole is actually encoded on the surface of the horizon. This of course just leads to other paradoxes, and the only thing that seems certain is that GR is approximate in some way, and all these questions are the result of stretching the theory to regimes in which it is no longer valid. This is why many people are searching for fundamentally new ways of constructing theories that yield the same answers for experiments we've tested, but begin from different assumptions. [0] However, as for black holes containing the same information, our universe is expanding uniformly at an accelerating rate, and this results in cosmological horizons beyond which objects are moving faster than the speed of light (with respect to the frame of reference). Therefore, black holes in different galaxies shouldn't be able to have access to the same light cones without compromising the principle of locality. Although, if you believe that entangled black holes in different galaxies would lead to the same s"},{"id":17404833,"author":"philipov","month":"2018-06","story":"Real-Life Schrödinger’s Cats Probe the Boundary of the Quantum World","stance":"reference","why":"Public lecture on gravity and entanglement.","quote":"[0] and [1] are a public lecture","text":"It seems that it is more sensible to say that entanglement is locality. The notion of two points being close to each other appears to be a course-grained approximation of high amounts of entanglement in the vacuum. If you divide the space along a boundary and break the entanglement between regions on both sides, the effect is to increase the distance between them. So perhaps it is better to think of gravity, locality and dynamical spacetime, as the \"hydrodynamics of entanglement\" [0] and [1] are a public lecture, [2] is colloquium [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":10675259,"author":"danbruc","month":"2015-12","story":"Holometer rules out first theory of space-time correlations","stance":"reference","why":"Susskind's idea that entanglement holds space together.","quote":"Susskind explores the idea that entanglement is what holds space together under the name \"ER = EPR","text":"Probably biased by the physicist I mostly listen to because they have a lot of lectures and talks online, Leonard Susskind and Nima Arkani-Hamed. The idea that space is quantized is pretty unlikely to be true because of relativity, i.e. because of length contraction the size of those space quanta depends on the motion of the observer. So if you find those space quanta have a specific size in your reference frame I can just go to a reference frame moving relative to yours and I will see length contracted space quanta and we will therefore disagree on their size. It is actually believed that neither space nor time are fundamental but that they emerge from something more fundamental because spacetime, relativity and quantum physics taken together are not really compatible, see for example \"Space-time is doomed. What replaces it?\" [1]. Susskind explores the idea that entanglement is what holds space together under the name \"ER = EPR\" [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["KTzGBJPuJwM",1],["mceaM2_zQd8",1],["tISy7EJQPzI",1],["9crggox5rbc",1]]},"QpAhX-gsHMs":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a good overview of modern C patterns, including in threads about learning C.","q":{"x":"This video gives a good overview of some modern patterns in C","i":34106582,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34106582,"author":"thom","month":"2022-12","story":"Ask HN: Learn C in 2023?","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview of modern C patterns.","quote":"This video gives a good overview of some modern patterns in C","text":"Modern C by Jens Gustedt is quite good. This video gives a good overview of some modern patterns in C: [VIDEO] I think the best thing you can do is go find a unit testing library you like the look of, maybe some tools for address/leak sanitization, and spend a good amount of time learning CMake and creating a build/project workflow you're happy with. Coming from Rust you've got great tooling and good guardrails in place to keep you on the happy path, I think getting into that groove early in C where you can run experiments and break things in a sane environment is important."},{"id":32609186,"author":"belter","month":"2022-08","story":"Why aren't there C conferences? (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Offered as example of a C conference talk.","quote":"Modern C and What We Can Learn From It - Luca Sas [ ACCU 2021 ]","text":"\"Modern C and What We Can Learn From It - Luca Sas [ ACCU 2021 ]\" - [VIDEO] \"Linux User/Kernel ABI: the realities of how C and C++ programs really talk to the OS - Greg Law\" - [LINK]"},{"id":28168676,"author":"AlbertoGP","month":"2021-08","story":"Show HN: C programming language extension: Cedro pre-processor","stance":"rec","why":"Explains modern C techniques and mentions the Cedro approach.","quote":"If you haven’t watched it, I can recommend it.","text":"Yes, that’s a very clean and robust approach. In the “Deferred resource release → Related work” section, one of the links is to a video from the ACCU 2021 conference by Luca Sas titled “Modern C and What We Can Learn From It” that explains modern C techniques, and your one is mentioned at 23′15″: [VIDEO] If you haven’t watched it, I can recommend it."}],"r":[["Wp7GAKLSGnI",1],["ghtpJnrdgbo",1],["HaEPXoXVf2k",1],["3LopI4YeC4I",1],["hMtu7vV_HmY",1],["cbSu2PXOTOc",1],["75Ju0eM5T2c",1],["48sCx-wBs34",1],["8JOD1AQGqEg",1],["_L3gNaAVjQ4",1],["b1JlYZQG3lI",1]]},"gKWNjFagR9k":{"wy":"Pointed to as the origin story of Hack and a real-world example of OCaml in production.","q":{"x":"Another cool one from CUFP this year is how Facebook is using OCaml to add incremental type inference to their vast PHP code base.","i":6712173,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":7472588,"author":"amirmc","month":"2014-03","story":"What it's like to use Haskell","stance":"rec","why":"Facebook's use of OCaml to create Hack.","quote":"It's where I first heard about Facebook's use of OCaml to create Hack ( )","text":"It's good to hear stories about how FP is being used in industry and I'd like to read more. For those who have an interest in this, I recommend the Commercial Users of Functional Programming workshop ( [LINK] ). It's where I first heard about Facebook's use of OCaml to create Hack ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":7252141,"author":"Xdes","month":"2014-02","story":"List of possible changes, updates, additions for PHP 6","stance":"rec","why":"Introduces Hack as a saner PHP.","quote":"Check out Hack for HHVM[0][1][2]. It's a lot saner than PHP, but it's not documented or well known outside Facebook.","text":"Check out Hack for HHVM[0][1][2]. It's a lot saner than PHP, but it's not documented or well known outside Facebook. [0]: Strangle Loop 2013: Keith Adams, Facebook: Taking PHP Seriously [LINK] [1]: CUFP 2013: Julien Verlaguet, Facebook: Analyzing PHP statically [VIDEO] [2]: HHVM and Hack – Can We Expect Them to Replace PHP? [LINK]"},{"id":6712173,"author":"avsm","month":"2013-11","story":"Real World OCaml","stance":"rec","why":"Facebook using OCaml for incremental type inference on PHP.","quote":"Another cool one from CUFP this year is how Facebook is using OCaml to add incremental type inference to their vast PHP code base.","text":"Another cool one from CUFP this year is how Facebook is using OCaml to add incremental type inference to their vast PHP code base. There's (slightly hard to see video) here, but I'm sure Facebook will publicize it more widely when they're ready. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7417363,"author":"avsm","month":"2014-03","story":"Why OCaml, Why Now?","stance":"reference","why":"Facebook's production use of OCaml compiled to JavaScript.","quote":"Not a great video, but here's the CUFP 2013 talk on the topic","text":"> Last time I checked, the ability to write Javascript using both languages was of experimental and/or novelty use only (hence the \"cupholders\"). Facebook uses an OCaml js_of_ocaml-based frontend for a large chunk of their production code editing. Not a great video, but here's the CUFP 2013 talk on the topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7054774,"author":"itafroma","month":"2014-01","story":"Taking PHP Seriously","stance":"reference","why":"Talk by the inventor of Hack.","quote":"another talk by Julien Verlaguet (to whom Adams credits the invention of Hack)","text":"I tried looking for something by way of an official announcement or release (preview or otherwise), but as Keith Adams joked, it's impossible it Google it, and the [LINK] URL he referenced is not correct. Someone in the Q&A pointed that out, so he mentioned that there'd be a post about it on the HHVM blog, but there doesn't seem to be anything about it in the last few months (this talk took place in September). Someone asked Keith Adams on Twitter at the end of October, where Adams said there was nothing to announce yet: [LINK] The only other thing I could find about it was another talk by Julien Verlaguet (to whom Adams credits the invention of Hack) from October: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["A3oIiH7BLmg",1]]},"u_ktRTWMX3M":{"wy":"HN users recommend it as practical motivation against procrastination and for people unsure what to do next.","q":{"x":"since watching Arnie's take on this I cannot get it out of my head and I have actually found it helpful","i":29032780,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":36086912,"author":"hunkins","month":"2023-05","story":"Need Some Advice","stance":"rec","why":"Motivation for someone facing job rejection","quote":"Some motivation","text":"Of course. Seems like just yesterday that I was in the exact same spot! Congrats on making it to a final round, that's a huge accomplishment. I've been rejected from many companies and interviews, and (spoiler) so has everyone in the industry - the only failure is giving up! You've got this. Some motivation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31305100,"author":"incomingpain","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: How do you “figure out” what to do in life?","stance":"rec","why":"Schwarzenegger speech offered as guidance on finding direction in life","quote":"","text":">I'm increasingly seeing people around me give serious thought to this question. Figuring out what to do in life? I thought I had figured things out, but now when I think again I feel doubtful. So, how do you \"figure\" things out in a way that it's bulletproof. This is something they fail to teach in school because teachers tautologically don't know the answer. Entrepreneurs also don't quite understand and often fall into serial startups until 1 works. Eventually they learn. Marcus Aurelius has something to say: [LINK] Here's probably the going book: [LINK] Arnold's video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29032780,"author":"abraae","month":"2021-10","story":"55 GiB/s FizzBuzz","stance":"rec","why":"Motivational speech that helps with procrastination","quote":"since watching Arnie's take on this I cannot get it out of my head and I have actually found it helpful","text":"I have trouble with procrastination, but since watching Arnie's take on this I cannot get it out of my head and I have actually found it helpful (yet here I am on HN again :() [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["qOTYgcdNrXE",1]]},"wTPGW1PNy_Y":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it to developers unfamiliar with how much leverage and intelligence there is in a SQL database.","q":{"x":"I'd suggest watching this video to get a flavor of how much leverage using the database can give you","i":30297604,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":37581728,"author":"lukaseder","month":"2023-09","story":"Permazen: Language-natural persistence to KV stores","stance":"rec","why":"Author recommends own talk to developers unfamiliar with SQL","quote":"I tend to recommend my famous talk to such developers","text":"> This may be more intuitive for some developers, especially those that don't use SQL all the time. I tend to recommend my famous talk to such developers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30297604,"author":"loudlambda","month":"2022-02","story":"A Hairy PostgreSQL Incident","stance":"rec","why":"Shows leverage gained from using the database","quote":"I'd suggest watching this video to get a flavor of how much leverage using the database can give you","text":"I would suggest that learning SQL (the syntax) isn't the crux--it's just syntax you learn over time based off need, just like learning python--what you need is motivation. I'd suggest watching this video to get a flavor of how much leverage using the database can give you: [VIDEO] If the database can make you more productive and more efficient and make your life easier, you now have incentive to use it more. Another thing that took my use of databases to the next level, was using PostGraphile ( [LINK] ). It takes a PostgreSQL database and provides a GraphQL endpoint for it. Use that as your only backend. You can use PostgreSQL row-level security to do access control (who can see or modify what). You can use PL/pgSQL to write business logic for higher level calculations. You can use constraints and triggers to do data validation. Each of these areas has an initially steep learning curve, and wonky syntax, but once you get over them you can implement many backends extremely quickly with them. And using PostGraphile where you only have SQL available (well, you can also add javascript extensions, but try to limit those) will be a great forcing function that will require you to scour stackoverflow and the excellent PostgreSQL docs to learn many of the useful corners of SQL you'd never learn otherwise."},{"id":23054737,"author":"nerdbaggy","month":"2020-05","story":"Medium-hard SQL interview questions","stance":"rec","why":"How smart SQL query engines are","quote":"This is a good talk about how smart SQL query engines are.","text":"I use to think that too. This is a good talk about how smart SQL query engines are. It is an hour though [VIDEO]"},{"id":32566342,"author":"lukaseder","month":"2022-08","story":"Parsing SQL","stance":"reference","why":"Probably the referenced Lukas Eder talk","quote":"Lukas Eder has some nice presentations about it.","text":">Totally agree. Lukas Eder has some nice presentations about it. Thanks for the shout out! For the record, that's probably the referenced talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21487126,"author":"taffer","month":"2019-11","story":"Modern Data Practice and the SQL Tradition","stance":"reference","why":"SQL's power as a programming tool","quote":"SQL is used to tell a computer what to do, and it is very powerful at it","text":"> further, afaik there's no assignment nor iteration in SQL That was until CTEs were introduced in SQL:1999 > it's not a programming language at all. It's a.. query language. The two are not mutually exclusive. SQL is used to tell a computer what to do, and it is very powerful at it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19886224,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2019-05","story":"Joins In Steps","stance":"reference","why":"Modern databases ignore indexes and switch plans mid-query","quote":"is not particularly gripping","text":"This talk (\"How Modern SQL Databases Come up with Algorithms that You Would Have Never Dreamed Of by Lukas Eder\" [VIDEO] ) is not particularly gripping, but he does claim that modern database engines will ignore indexes if they estimate it will be faster to read all the data than it is to access the index first then read the relevant data. And that Oracle can have multiple execution plans and switch between them mid-query if the first one isn't working out as expected. Could this be a rare case of the mythical sufficiently smart compiler having more information at runtime than the programmer has at coding time, and that if you could hint which index to use, the trade off would be that you'd instead be troubleshooting queries which end up slower because of it?"}]},"w_znRopGtbE":{"wy":"HN points to it as the classic example of home-made semiconductors and a fun way to go down to the transistor level.","q":{"x":"If you want to go further down, check out Jeri Ellsworth's old videos where she built a transistor at home","i":25400940,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25400940,"author":"didericis","month":"2020-12","story":"Become Shell Literate","stance":"rec","why":"Going down to building a transistor at home","quote":"If you want to go further down, check out Jeri Ellsworth's old videos where she built a transistor at home","text":"Another book recommendation: \"Elements of Computing Systems\" by Noam Nisan and Shimon Schocken. It doesn't go all the way down to the physics of semiconductors, but it goes down to logic gates and all the way up to tetris (there's a related course/website called nand to tetris: [LINK] ) If you want to go further down, check out Jeri Ellsworth's old videos where she built a transistor at home: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6018025,"author":"DanBC","month":"2013-07","story":"Valve's flat management structure 'like high school'","stance":"rec","why":"One of Jeri Ellsworth's interesting hardware projects","quote":"Jeri Ellsworth, for people who don't know, does really interesting hardware stuff.","text":"Jeri Ellsworth, for people who don't know, does really interesting hardware stuff. \"TSA body imaging at home\" ( [LINK] ) \"Home made EL wire\" ( [LINK] ) \"Recording Audio onto floppy discs\" ( [LINK] ) \"Home made transistor\" ( [LINK] ) \"Homebrew NMOS transistor\" ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":4727274,"author":"rrmm","month":"2012-11","story":"Build your own FPGA","stance":"rec","why":"Fun homebrew transistor project from a bare wafer","quote":"Check out Jeri Ellsworth's homebrow NMOS transistor for some real fun.","text":"Check out Jeri Ellsworth's homebrow NMOS transistor for some real fun. Just get your bare silicon wafer from E-bay and have at it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37973779,"author":"mikewarot","month":"2023-10","story":"22-Year-Old Builds Chips in His Parents' Garage (2022)","stance":"reference","why":"Early example of home-made semiconductors","quote":"I've been following home-made semiconductors since Jeri Ellsworth made her own NMOS FETs in 2010.","text":"I've been following home-made semiconductors since Jeri Ellsworth made her own NMOS FETs in 2010.[1] When Sam managed to make his own ICs, I was happy for him, and for the hobby. Now he seems to be on the verge of democratizing access to semiconductor production in very low volumes. I've got my own project to try out once I can get my hands on it.[2] Who knows, petaflop chips made in the garage could be right around the corner, in less than 2 decades! [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":29184187,"author":"userbinator","month":"2021-11","story":"How to make a CPU – a simple picture-based explanation","stance":"reference","why":"Deeper process view with larger-scale homemade transistors","quote":"Jeri Ellsworth has gone \"deeper\" into the process and made a few transistors, although at a much larger scale","text":"Jeri Ellsworth has gone \"deeper\" into the process and made a few transistors, although at a much larger scale: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":5628343,"author":"DanBC","month":"2013-04","story":"How I coded in 1985","stance":"reference","why":"Homebrew transistors, though commenter says it feels like cheating","quote":"","text":"( [VIDEO] ) for transistors, but it feels like cheating. Relays are doable from scratch (mining and refining copper; winding coils, etc etc) but it's going to be pretty hard work and you're going to have a very slow computer at the end of it."},{"id":2933768,"author":"Luyt","month":"2011-08","story":"Pentium-III autopsy","stance":"reference","why":"Making NMOS transistors at home with simple chemicals","quote":"Jeri Ellsworth makes NMOS transistors on silicon wafer chips at home, with an oven, rust remover and some other home chemicals","text":"Jeri Ellsworth makes NMOS transistors on silicon wafer chips at home, with an oven, rust remover and some other home chemicals: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1643971,"author":"jws","month":"2010-08","story":"Homebrew silicon fab","stance":"reference","why":"Found via Google as the relevant homebrew fab example","quote":"","text":"Google to the rescue![1] [VIDEO] [1] Though when I googled \"Jeri Ellsworth\", Google suggestions helpfully suggested I would be interested in her cleavage. Shame on you, internet! [2] I wonder what happened to the fatmanandcircuitgirl site. I was watching them build a pinball machine and then it just stopped updating."}],"r":[["8xFLjUt2leM",1],["pBTvmrRGlbE",1],["-Qph8BNrnLY",1],["snz7JJlSZvw",1],["ne6tB2KiZuk",1]]},"4JWwOzEDGss":{"wy":"Mentioned as an example of Sesame Street respecting children's intelligence and as an early gateway to modern classical music.","q":{"x":"Philip Glass scored an animation for Sesame Street.","i":27111817,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27111817,"author":"SavantIdiot","month":"2021-05","story":"'Sesame Street': from radical experiment to beloved TV mainstay","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Philip Glass scored animation that sparked interest in modern classical","quote":"Philip Glass scored an animation for Sesame Street.","text":"Philip Glass scored an animation for Sesame Street. [VIDEO] I blame this for my interest in modern classical. I can't imagine them doing something so progressive today. But that's because they had a captive audience in the 70's."},{"id":18253445,"author":"krapp","month":"2018-10","story":"Caroll Spinney Leaves ‘Sesame Street’ After Nearly 50 Years","stance":"rec","why":"Creative, musical Sesame Street segments that respected children's intelligence","quote":"It didn't talk down to children or insult their intelligence.","text":"Sesame Street had so many great creative and musical segments before it turned into the Elmo show. It didn't talk down to children or insult their intelligence. It inspired children rather than just try to hypnotize them with colors and repetition to shut them up for an hour. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":37623812,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-09","story":"Sesame Street – Count to 12 [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Geometry of Circles segment scored by Philip Glass","quote":"their \"Geometry of Circles\" was written by Philip Glass","text":"Somewhat related, but their \"Geometry of Circles\" was written by Philip Glass: * [VIDEO] * [LINK] * [LINK]"},{"id":35413721,"author":"leviathant","month":"2023-04","story":"If Philip Glass Were a Film","stance":"reference","why":"Sesame Street animation scored by Philip Glass","quote":"the Sesame Street animation that he scored","text":"I thought this was going to be a link to the Sesame Street animation that he scored @ [VIDEO] If I had access to this sixteen years ago, I would have recreated the animations in Flash, but after Flash died out, I stopped playing in that field."}],"r":[["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["-VsmF9m_Nt8",1],["Kr3quGh7pJA",1],["4nigRT2KmCE",1]]},"4kLPyV8lBbs":{"wy":"HN uses it as the expert primer on burnout that dispels common misconceptions.","q":{"x":"There is this excellent talk by Maslach, \"Understanding Burnout\" which dispels most misunderstandings","i":20333431,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20333431,"author":"p0nce","month":"2019-07","story":"Burnout and the Brain (2016)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Dispels most misunderstandings about burnout","quote":"There is this excellent talk by Maslach, \"Understanding Burnout\" which dispels most misunderstandings","text":"Great article. There is this excellent talk by Maslach, \"Understanding Burnout\" which dispels most misunderstandings: [VIDEO] However, as with most conditions it's unlikely you will have any interest in it until being affected."},{"id":18896522,"author":"JakeAl","month":"2019-01","story":"Ask HN: What is the difference between Burnout/Depression/Laziness/Wrong job?","stance":"rec","why":"Expert primer on burnout by Christina Maslach","quote":"This video is a good primer on burnout by an expert.","text":"This video is a good primer on burnout by an expert. \"Understanding Burnout,\" Prof. Christina Maslach (U.C. Berkeley) [VIDEO] This article is a summary of what's in the above speaker's book Recovering from Burnout [LINK] I also want to mention the article above has a link to Mind Tools, which is a web site that has a test you can take but I recommend reading their articles on leadership as it helps one understand their place and the direction they need to take as leaders of their own life as well as leaders in the lives of others, which is an interesting perspective in the context of the listed topics."},{"id":12986670,"author":"0xmohit","month":"2016-11","story":"The psychology of stress and burnout","stance":"reference","why":"Further reading on burnout alongside job-demand factors","quote":"Also: Understanding Burnout [2].","text":"From DOES16 London - Better Faster Cheaper .. How? (slide #29) [0] [1]: Recipe for burnout is inverse of recipe for success Word Overload: Job demands exceed human limits. Lack of control: Inability to influence decisions that affect your job. ... Also: Understanding Burnout [2]. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12171504,"author":"0xmohit","month":"2016-07","story":"How Exhaustion Became a Status Symbol","stance":"reference","why":"Maslach's talk on understanding burnout","quote":"Understanding Burnout [0] by Christina Maslach [1].","text":"Recipe for burnout is inverse of recipe for success: [LINK] Understanding Burnout [0] by Christina Maslach [1]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"}],"r":[["XZ3w_jec1v8",1],["LIb3L4vKZ7U",1]]},"EQs5VxNPhzk":{"wy":"HN commenters share it as a favorite example of upscaled vintage footage that makes the early 1900s feel present, though some find the colorization off.","q":{"x":"I've really come to enjoy these sorts of upscaled videos that allow a view of various cities in the early 1900's.","i":28888461,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":38742725,"author":"peteforde","month":"2023-12","story":"They want you to forget what a film looks like","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite upscaled vintage short, loved by commenter's family","quote":"My personal favorite is","text":"I'm not a film expert, but I like to think I have taste? And yet, somehow the recent Beatles projects reduced me to tears on several occasions. Am I secretly a chud in denial, or are my tastes just offensive? Look, everyone has the right to be an elitist on their own film blog. The author didn't ask us to read what he said. However, his entire thesis is dramatically undermined by the simple fact that he didn't attempt to grab even a single comparison frame from the original releases so that a reader can A/B them and make up their minds for themselves. As to his distaste for those upscaled and colorized vintage shorts, I'm happy to go on record and say that I love them. In fact, watching these clips is the closest thing my family has to a Christmas tradition at this point. My personal favorite is [VIDEO] and frankly, I don't have the time of day for anyone who is upset that I think it's wonderful."},{"id":28888461,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2021-10","story":"The Hindenburg disaster denoised, upscaled, and colorized using ML [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Upscaled early-1900s footage of Wuppertal, Germany","quote":"I've really come to enjoy these sorts of upscaled videos that allow a view of various cities in the early 1900's.","text":"I've really come to enjoy these sorts of upscaled videos that allow a view of various cities in the early 1900's. New York: [LINK] San Francisco: [LINK] Paris: [LINK] Wuppertal, Germany: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24237252,"author":"Animats","month":"2020-08","story":"Ride through Wuppertal on ‘the flying train’ in clear footage from 1902","stance":"reference","why":"4K upgraded version of the 1902 flying train footage","quote":"The 4K upgraded version, with good speed correction and somewhat lame colorization.","text":"The 4K upgraded version, with good speed correction and somewhat lame colorization.[1] Here's a cab ride today, from the back end.[2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":24702137,"author":"Animats","month":"2020-10","story":"Colorized and upscaled footage of a snowball fight filmed in 1896","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The colors are all wrong.","text":"That colorized well, or seemed to, since the original had little color. It had to color \"building\", \"snow\", and \"skin\". That's about it. Here, though, is a similar technique applied to the Wuppertal monorail.[1] The colors are all wrong. The supports was really lime green, the overhead track was brown, and the carriages were a glossy red. Since the training set lacks data for 19th century supported monorails, it's just guessing. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["QWveXdj6oZU",1],["qp0HIF3SfI4",1],["62tIvfP9A2w",1],["SWEts0rlezA",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["vwCdKU9uYnE",1],["Gk-9Fd2mEnI",1],["43CMV8KIs3E",1],["s0ukXDnWlTY",1],["VO_1AdYRGW8",1],["oTpA5jt1g60",1],["29RkHH-psrY",1],["pBiWtJJN5zk",1],["JRcXUuQYR90",1],["rHP-OPXK2ig",1]]},"JAczd3mt3X0":{"wy":"HN treats it as the more informed, balanced take on SpinLaunch's tradeoffs.","q":{"x":"a more honest and detailed look at the company","i":31033253,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29433360,"author":"Robotbeat","month":"2021-12","story":"Scientists find a way to ‘catapult’ rockets into space like ‘slingshots’","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Better, more informed take on SpinLaunch than other videos.","quote":"Scott Manley also has a much better understanding of rockets and space technology than thunderf*t.","text":"Man, I have my opinions on Spinlaunch, too, but I find thunderf*t’s videos (especially the BUSTED ones) unenlightening & unhelpful to rationally discussing the pros and cons of some technology. Scott Manley’s video (if video is the format you prefer) on Spinlaunch was much better. Scott Manley also has a much better understanding of rockets and space technology than thunderf*t. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31033253,"author":"dotnet00","month":"2022-04","story":"NASA will test SpinLaunch's ability to fling satellites into orbit","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed look at SpinLaunch's tradeoffs and heat shield mass.","quote":"a more honest and detailed look at the company","text":"I think their basic idea is sound, it'll probably eventually function. My concerns are if it'll be worth it, since there's a trade-off being made between fuel mass and heat shield mass, and at least based on Scott Manley's summary video this week, the rocket with the heat shield is close to the same mass as the Electron rocket. In which case I'm not sure the complexity of SpinLaunch is worth it. Edit: the video in question: [LINK] and a more honest and detailed look at the company, again from Scott: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31032464,"author":"LeifCarrotson","month":"2022-04","story":"NASA will test SpinLaunch's ability to fling satellites into orbit","stance":"reference","why":"Cautious, balanced view of SpinLaunch's difficulty.","quote":"Scott Manley also put out a video on Spinlaunch","text":"Scott Manley also put out a video on Spinlaunch: [VIDEO] He...encourages caution. It's one thing to call a technology \"debunked\", another to say it's very difficult or that it has a low probability of success."},{"id":30190935,"author":"l33tman","month":"2022-02","story":"New material that can absorb and release enormous amounts of energy","stance":"reference","why":"Example of the risk of a spin-launch failure.","quote":"","text":"A full tank is safer than an almost empty tank.. Also the tank is designed to keep the vapor part of the gasoline too saturated to explode, this is why there is a latch on the fill tube. I found this Quora post: [LINK] And even if it can explode in the situation where there's almost no fuel left, in that case it's not as bad as liberating the full energy of a full tank, which is what you'd do if you have a flywheel spinning with the same total energy as a full tank of gasoline. That would be madness. Heh I came to think of the recent demo of the company that wants to spin up satellites on earth and THROW them into orbit. If something goes wrong in that spin-up, they would destroy the entire launch facility and whatever is in the way. [VIDEO] I once had a CDROM open while the CD was spinning at like 40x or so, it ejected and went into the plaster wall. And that was just a CD.."},{"id":29186636,"author":"andreareina","month":"2021-11","story":"Spinlaunch completes suborbital launch without engine","stance":"reference","why":"Gives release velocity and target speeds for SpinLaunch.","quote":"release velocity was Mach 1, they're targeting Mach ~7 for the final version.","text":"Per Scott Manley[1] release velocity was Mach 1, they're targeting Mach ~7 for the final version. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29212079,"author":"yholio","month":"2021-11","story":"SpinLaunch completes first test flight with rocket-flinging launch system","stance":"reference","why":"Cited about launch detectability and infrared signature.","quote":"","text":"I believe the parent is right, you couldn't hide it. Even if you succeed in building them completely stealthy, it's just a matter of time until the enemy finds out the locations using espionage or signals intelligence, and after that they will place the site unde non-stop satellite surveillance. They are massive and can't be moved, so they are sitting ducks. As for launching, you are guaranteed to produce a thermal signature in the first few seconds of flight, since you hit the high density atmosphere at a 3-5 Km/s speed. You will leave a distinct, large and highly detectable infrared signature and the enemy will be alerted: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["A0FZIwabctw",1],["G0Q4UAiKacw",1]]},"Rqfkuc5mawg":{"wy":"Recommended as a savage, ahead-of-its-time parody of startup and tech culture.","q":{"x":"I can highly, highly , recommend Nathan Barley which was ahead of its time","i":36644276,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36644276,"author":"qingcharles","month":"2023-07","story":"When did Alan Partridge first appear on television?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Chris Morris series ahead of its time.","quote":"I can highly, highly , recommend Nathan Barley which was ahead of its time","text":"If you like Chris Morris material I can highly, highly , recommend Nathan Barley which was ahead of its time: [VIDEO] Sad that his web site is now offline... www.trashbat.co.ck"},{"id":18492004,"author":"cutler","month":"2018-11","story":"If you want to understand Silicon Valley, watch Silicon Valley","stance":"rec","why":"Savage British parody of mid-2000s Shoreditch startup scene.","quote":"a savage British TV parody of the mid-2000s startup scene in Shoreditch, London.","text":"You might also like \"Nathan Barley (2005)\" - a savage British TV parody of the mid-2000s startup scene in Shoreditch, London. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22072553,"author":"smileypete","month":"2020-01","story":"How Swedes were fooled by the book “Surrounded by Idiots”","stance":"reference","why":"Series featuring the rise of the idiots.","quote":"reminds me of 'the rise of the idiots' from the 2006(!) series 'Nathan Barley'","text":"Late to this, but it reminds me of 'the rise of the idiots' from the 2006(!) series 'Nathan Barley' [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["rI8tNMsozo0",1],["QM1iUe6IofM",1],["xFKFoGiGlXQ",1]]},"eiYoBbEZwlk":{"wy":"Linked as the canonical demo of a safety technology that stops a blade before it injures a finger.","q":{"x":"The demo also never gets old. Just too badass.","i":13266507,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":13266507,"author":"nickpsecurity","month":"2016-12","story":"The Resurgence of C Programming","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Demo of tech preventing finger injuries that never gets old","quote":"The demo also never gets old. Just too badass.","text":"Case in point: [VIDEO] Maybe we need to use your sharp tools point with that video. It drives the point home with even experienced people losing fingers to their tools followed by tech modified to prevent it. The demo also never gets old. Just too badass."},{"id":9530459,"author":"toothbrush","month":"2015-05","story":"The View from the Front Seat of the Google Self-Driving Car","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstration of SawStop stopping a blade on contact with a finger","quote":"Here's a demonstration, it's very fluffy but it gets the point across.","text":"Wow, that's super neat, i'd never heard of it. Seems like something that should be absolutely mandatory for all factories equipped with such devices! Go technology! Here's a demonstration, it's very fluffy but it gets the point across. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18551159,"author":"ljf","month":"2018-11","story":"Uber says air transportation drones are closer than we think","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a saw that stops before a finger is injured","quote":"if you try and touch this one with a finger it will stop before it can do damage","text":"/some/ power saws can stop instantly - if you try and touch this one with a finger it will stop before it can do damage: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["w-WMn_zHCVo",1],["CesHr99ezWE",1],["hBMoPUAeLnY",1],["BsVydyC8ZGQ",1]]},"iLmkec_4Rfo":{"wy":"Brought up whenever Vice is discussed, as a memorable scene of Carr pressing its founders.","q":{"x":"One of the best scenes from Page One","i":9046646,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9046646,"author":"krebby","month":"2015-02","story":"David Carr Dies at 58","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Carr confronts Vice founders over their CNN deal in the film Page One.","quote":"One of the best scenes from Page One","text":"One of the best scenes from Page One: [VIDEO] Carr talks to the Vice guys about their deal with CNN: \"Just because you put on a fucking safari helmet and looked at some poop, doesn't give you the right to insult what we do.\""},{"id":35945900,"author":"vanyamarka","month":"2023-05","story":"Vice files for Bankruptcy","stance":"rec","why":"Carr interviews Vice founders, illustrating the value of traditional media coverage.","quote":"There's an interesting confrontation over that piece in the NY Times 'Page Six' documentary","text":"There's an interesting confrontation over that piece in the NY Times 'Page Six' documentary, which filmed David Carr, the Times's then media correspondent, interviewing the Vice Founders: [VIDEO] I think part of the Vice pitch was always that it was a disruptor of the old-fashioned media narratives, uncovering the stories to which boring old media was blind. I tend to agree with Carr's implicit critique here that we undervalue the journalistic, societal value of the sort of unglamorous coverage in which traditional media invests and at which it excels."},{"id":35956250,"author":"keane","month":"2023-05","story":"Vice, Decayed Digital Colossus, Files for Bankruptcy","stance":"reference","why":"Excerpt from Page One showing 2010 tension between Vice and NYT.","quote":"a fun 2010 interaction between Vice and NYT illustrating some tension then between the two outlets","text":"Your observation reminds me of a fun 2010 interaction between Vice and NYT illustrating some tension then between the two outlets that is featured in the documentary Page One: Inside the New York Times . (David Carr and Shane Smith would later laugh together about the exchange during a panel at Vanity Fair's New Establishment Summit in 2014.) Excerpt: [VIDEO] Full: [LINK]"},{"id":9841927,"author":"ambirex","month":"2015-07","story":"The Cult of Vice","stance":"reference","why":"Carr's confrontation with Vice from Page One, recalled whenever Vice comes up.","quote":"everytime Vice is brought up all I can think of is this piece with David Carr from Page One (2011)","text":"Perhaps this is now old, but everytime Vice is brought up all I can think of is this piece with David Carr from Page One (2011) - [VIDEO]"},{"id":9664319,"author":"caminante","month":"2015-06","story":"Gawker Media Employees Vote to Form a Union, and the Bosses Approve","stance":"reference","why":"Carr calls out Vice for looking at poop; cited in defense of Gawker.","quote":"sites like Vice.com where NYT super-journos call them out for looking at poop[1]","text":"Why should the Gawker network do \"actual journalism\" if that need's already met by major outlets? Granted, there are listicle happy properties (Lifehacker), but Gawker et al. filled a void with it's meta-criticism of news. It's not rare for Gawker to cover topics that are too risky for major outlets. Take for example the sports property, deadspin. I read deadspin and the deadspin writers broach newsworthy topics that would get an ESPN writer not only \"banned from the clubhouse\" but suspended by HQ in order to appease the team/league. Deadspin provides signal that major outlets would otherwise avoid or over-cook. Not to over-shill for Gawker, but the commenters are pretty educated and won't let the authors off the hook if the author leaves holes in their argument. I submit that your poop should flow further down the news hill to sites like Vice.com where NYT super-journos call them out for looking at poop[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39477888,"author":"adfm","month":"2024-02","story":"Vice website is shutting down","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"David Carr had their number off the bat","text":"The current top story on vice.com, “Inside the Christian Nationalist Church Where Proud Boys Go to be Baptized,” is a bit thick. “Meaner, faster, and more dynamic.” Ha! David Carr had their number off the bat: [VIDEO]"}]},"49kBWM3RQQ8":{"wy":"Recommended as a practical, step-by-step video path for learning Emacs.","q":{"x":"I learned a lot from this CS prof's videos, and it's a good, practical learning path I'd recommend","i":22883269,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":30934669,"author":"slk500","month":"2022-04","story":"What’s new in Emacs 28.1?","stance":"rec","why":"Video tutorials for starting out with Emacs.","quote":"I recommend start with video tutorials: 1. Mike Zamansky","text":"You have to find specific need that emacs will fulfil. Org-mode is the key. Don't try to use it like IDE for coding for a start. I recommend start with video tutorials: 1. Mike Zamansky [VIDEO] 2. Rainer König org-mode [LINK]"},{"id":22883269,"author":"stakkur","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: Resources to grok Emacs and use it well?","stance":"rec","why":"Practical step-by-step Emacs learning path.","quote":"I learned a lot from this CS prof's videos, and it's a good, practical learning path I'd recommend","text":"I started using Emacs in earnest a few years ago, especially Org-Mode. At first, I tried every customization I came across and loaded up my init file with all the cool mods. But when things went wrong, I had no clue how to fix them. So after about a year I started over, resetting my config and using Emacs customization over custom elisp writing to get a working init file. It helped me learn a lot . This is the path I recommend: do small customizations using Emacs' built-in features, then learn elisp later. I learned a lot from this CS prof's videos, and it's a good, practical learning path I'd recommend: [VIDEO] If you're into writing, I maintain a list of resources for writing with Emacs: [LINK]"},{"id":23375194,"author":"todd8","month":"2020-06","story":"Ask HN: I'm an incoming freshman to college for a CS major, what should I know?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Programming in high school is quite different than the work you will be doing in a CS program. CS is a hard, but not impossible, major. Depending upon the school it will be perhaps easier than a Physics degree and maybe a bit more work but easier than a Math degree. There is too much CS to learn in four years so every program leaves something out. For example, some schools will require a class in Artificial Intelligence while others may not but will instead require a course in Data Science. So it's hard to know exactly what you will be studying, but I can make a few suggestions that should be helpful for someone heading off to college and planning to study CS. Generally, there is a lot of useful math that you may be required to learn. Usually one year of calculus, one semester of differential equations, one semester of linear algebra (matrices, etc.), one semester of statistics or probability, and one or two semesters of discrete math. It's easy to end up with a math minor on your way to a CS degree, and I recommend getting one if you are reasonably good at math. The good thing about this selection of math subjects is that they are all in different areas and are consequently to some degree \"introductory\" or lower division. The trick at this level for doing well in math classes is simply to put in the time to do all the homework, attend all the lectures, and prepare for tests by doing every problem (even the ones not assigned as homework) from the text book sections covered in"}],"r":[["ERbvKrH-GC4",1]]},"8S3B8a8N83k":{"wy":"Recommended as a decent introduction to the Vampire accelerator and a cheaper way to modernize classic Amigas.","q":{"x":"This is a decent introductory video","i":12941116,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":14413641,"author":"amai","month":"2017-05","story":"The A-EON Amiga X5000: An alternate universe where the Amiga platform never died","stance":"rec","why":"Vampire 2 accelerator for Amiga 600, cheaper alternative.","quote":"It seems a lot of fun and is much cheaper (250 Euros).","text":"I think the Vampire 2 FPGA acceleration card for the Amiga 600 deserves to be mentioned: [VIDEO] It seems a lot of fun and is much cheaper (250 Euros)."},{"id":12941116,"author":"ZenoArrow","month":"2016-11","story":"World of Commodore 2016","stance":"rec","why":"Introduction to the Vampire FPGA accelerator for classic Amigas.","quote":"This is a decent introductory video","text":"Good to see the Vampire will be making an appearance, though I'm guessing no new announcements about upcoming releases. For anyone out of the loop with Commodore stuff, the Vampire is an FPGA-based accelerator for classic Amigas. This is a decent introductory video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20494110,"author":"davesmith1983","month":"2019-07","story":"A New Motherboard for Amiga, the Platform That Refuses to Die","stance":"reference","why":"Complete rundown of the Apollo Vampire V2 accelerator.","quote":"Here is a complete rundown of the accelerator","text":"A1200 boards are over 25 years old. A lot of the boards are damaged because of capacitors leaking, just being old. Many of us that still use the Amigas like using \"proper hardware\". I like the idea of taking a 25 year old computer and hacking the machine to play DOOM or going on IRC or even checking my emails. FPGAs have been used for Amiga Accelerators the fastest accelerator (at present) is the Apollo Vampire V2. Here is a complete rundown of the accelerator: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["jVoSQP2yUYA",1],["Qaq9vlfoGnA",1],["KgzQuE1pR1w",1],["fg0_Vhldz-8",1],["R5Gppi-O3a8",1],["GLG9g7BcjKs",1],["ERbvKrH-GC4",1],["iRsSjh5TTqI",1],["iFDe5kUUyT0",1],["Hy4QjmKzF1c",1]]},"AcQifPHcMLE":{"wy":"Recommended as a great example of early VR and pre-computer simulation.","q":{"x":"This tank simulator from the 1970s is a great example of early VR","i":39227305,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41151717,"author":"anfractuosity","month":"2024-08","story":"Sega Jet Rocket: The '70s arcade game with no computer or screen","stance":"rec","why":"Tank simulator driving through a tiny model world","quote":"reminds me a bit of two Tom Scott videos","text":"Very cool, reminds me a bit of two Tom Scott videos (these ones do use displays though, but combined with little model worlds) * [VIDEO] - 1970s tank simulator drives through a tiny world * [LINK] - Submarine simulator"},{"id":39227305,"author":"bux93","month":"2024-02","story":"A man who invented VR goggles 50 years too soon (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"1970s tank simulator as an early VR example","quote":"This tank simulator from the 1970s is a great example of early VR","text":"Using VR goggles that have no capability of viewing anything VR makes them just regular goggles though? This tank simulator from the 1970s is a great example of early VR: [VIDEO] I wonder if there's anything earlier.."},{"id":45332978,"author":"bitwize","month":"2025-09","story":"Simulating a Machine from the 80s","stance":"reference","why":"Swiss Military Museum's 1970s tank simulator with a diorama and camera","quote":"The operator drives a little camera around a large diorama providing a first-person view of simulated terrain","text":"The Swiss Military Museum maintains an exhibit with a 1970s tank simulator that guests can try out. It consists of a tank cockpit in a hydraulically mounted chamber that can pitch and roll with the changing terrain. The operator drives a little camera around a large diorama providing a first-person view of simulated terrain; a \"foot\" on the camera senses the terrain which is then translated into movements of the chamber. Apparently all of the equipment is original except the computer; as computer parts became harder to replace, eventually they just used a Raspberry Pi. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41914816,"author":"jtxt","month":"2024-10","story":"Building a Game with the Real Engine","stance":"reference","why":"Early tank simulator using a camera on a gantry over a diorama","quote":"Reminds of an early tank simulator","text":"Reminds of an early tank simulator where the driver in the cockpit saw a video feed from a camera on a gantry they were slowly driving around a diarama. [VIDEO] \"Real engine\" for just one player at a time though."}],"r":[["nIKhUizkXxA",1],["JXpoALotxf0",1],["KrksBdWcZgQ",1],["F8JCh0owT4w",1]]},"FBsP-bmDLOo":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an even-handed physicist's take for 5G skeptics, noting it is probably safe but under-studied.","q":{"x":"this is an even-handed (reasonably well-informed) take on the matter from physicist Sabine Hossenfelder","i":30881021,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30881021,"author":"ninkendo","month":"2022-04","story":"No increased risk of brain tumours for mobile phone users, new study finds","stance":"rec","why":"Even-handed physicist take on 5G millimeter wave safety and open questions","quote":"this is an even-handed (reasonably well-informed) take on the matter from physicist Sabine Hossenfelder","text":"If you’re actually curious about the topic, this is an even-handed (reasonably well-informed) take on the matter from physicist Sabine Hossenfelder: [VIDEO] One of the interesting points in the video is that 5G’s high frequency band of 24-48GHz is _not_ well understood with studies. The typical cell phone radiation/cancer studies deal with the much lower-frequency bands (hundreds of MHz or 1-5GHz), but that doesn’t necessarily transfer to the new higher frequencies. Per the video, it is known that frequencies that high can warm the skin and irritate the eyes if they’re at really high power, but it’s not known exactly what power threshold for such millimeter waves is considered “safe”, and the number of studies isn’t sufficient to completely dismiss any safety concerns. She’s not saying “5G isn’t safe”, but rather “5G hasn’t been completely determined safe yet because more studies are needed for the newer mm-wave spectrum” and “studies showing the safety of previous generations of cell phone radiation don’t necessarily apply to the new frequency band.” It’s not necessarily alarm-worthy, but it’s more nuanced than just “crystal-worshipping hippies”…"},{"id":30705048,"author":"tejohnso","month":"2022-03","story":"Microwave ovens and their hazards","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why 5G is probably not dangerous but warrants more study","quote":"Sabine Hossenfelder has a great explanation of 5G controversy[1]","text":"Sabine Hossenfelder has a great explanation of 5G controversy[1] that is somewhat relevant. Explains why it is probably not dangerous, but also indicates why some additional study is warranted. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30822080,"author":"tejohnso","month":"2022-03","story":"5G Skeptic","stance":"rec","why":"Suggested viewing for 5G skeptics","quote":"you can also suggest they watch","text":"While you're ignoring everything they say about radiation, you can also suggest they watch \"All you need to know to understand 5G\" by Sabine Hossenfelder. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25605353,"author":"NiceWayToDoIT","month":"2021-01","story":"The Wonderful Uses of Asbestos (1942) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Notes few studies exist on millimeter wave long-term health effects","quote":"there are basically no studies.","text":"Regarding 5G I thought the same but according to Sabine it is not quite clear it is safe: [VIDEO] [14:00] \"Now the thing is if you look what is known about the health risks from long-term exposure to the new millimeter waves band, there are basically no studies. \""}],"r":[["mScpHTIi-kM",1],["gp4U5aH_T6A",1],["taaEzHI9xyY",1]]},"K1xnYFCZ9Yg":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an engaging, brief overview of the RNA world idea.","q":{"x":"Stated Clearly has a great video about the RNA world hypothesis ( ) if anyone's looking for an engaging overview.","i":29481558,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29481558,"author":"joshquin","month":"2021-12","story":"The RNA World Hypothesis","stance":"rec","why":"Engaging overview of the RNA world hypothesis.","quote":"Stated Clearly has a great video about the RNA world hypothesis ( ) if anyone's looking for an engaging overview.","text":"Stated Clearly has a great video about the RNA world hypothesis ( [VIDEO] ) if anyone's looking for an engaging overview."},{"id":13107571,"author":"lawpoop","month":"2016-12","story":"A New Physics Theory of Life (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Brief overview of how evolution could occur with RNA chains only.","quote":"Here's a brief overview of the theory that shows how evolution could happen with chains of RNA only","text":"Evolution probably goes back before cells. The first living thing would have to be a self-replicating molecule that uses energy. Different forms of this self-replicating molecule would have different reproductive success. There is a not-unpopular theory that the original form of life on earth was an RNA molecule. RNA is a special molecule, because it can both act as a store of data, and as an enzyme for catalyzing reactions. According to this theory, specialized data storage in the form of DNA evolved later, along with specialized enzymes. Here's a brief overview of the theory that shows how evolution could happen with chains of RNA only: [VIDEO] Jack Szostak at Harvard has a great series of videos explaining this theory, and also a number of experiments that he and his students have performed. [LINK]"},{"id":14110540,"author":"JumpCrisscross","month":"2017-04","story":"Mimicking an impact on Earth’s early atmosphere yields all 4 RNA bases","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for the RNA world hypothesis.","quote":"","text":"You may be looking for the RNA world hypothesis [1] [2]. In essence, random bits of RNA combine and replicate \"in the wild\". The bits that replicate better become more prevalent, et cetera, et cetera . This system needs no metabolic process per se . Heating and cooling and/or an oscillating chemical environment , e.g. from the day and night cycle or the tides or a belching vent, could provide the energy to power these cycles. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["PqPGOhXoprU",1]]},"NM51qOpwcIM":{"wy":"HN uses it to show that Lady Gaga is a genuinely skilled musician and writer of her own songs.","q":{"x":"she is also a phenomenal musician. Here's a video from before she was famous","i":7402044,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7402044,"author":"jamiequint","month":"2014-03","story":"Secret raises $8.6 million Series A","stance":"rec","why":"Pre-fame performance showing musicianship","quote":"she is also a phenomenal musician. Here's a video from before she was famous","text":"re: Lady Gaga, hype had something to do with it but she is also a phenomenal musician. Here's a video from before she was famous: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6116757,"author":"smoyer","month":"2013-07","story":"Ruins of forgotten empires: APL languages","stance":"rec","why":"Bluesy early Lady Gaga performance","quote":"she was so bluesy in the NYU talent show","text":"So true ... I don't really appreciate her current music (I mean like Brahms or Mozart), but she was so bluesy in the NYU talent show ( [VIDEO] ). I could listen to music like that all day."},{"id":26647590,"author":"mewse-hn","month":"2021-03","story":"Hit songwriters ask pop stars to stop taking credit for songs they didn't write","stance":"reference","why":"Gaga performing an original composition in college","quote":"This is a video of her performing an original composition in college","text":"Lady Gaga has chops and is a real artist. This is a video of her performing an original composition in college: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7402076,"author":"grinich","month":"2014-03","story":"Secret raises $8.6 million Series A","stance":"reference","why":"Early performance supporting her musical credentials","quote":"Lady Gaga (aka Stefani Germanotta) is actually an incredible musician.","text":"Lady Gaga (aka Stefani Germanotta) is actually an incredible musician. She studied at the prestigious NYU Tisch School of the Arts, and was one of the few students given early admission at the age of 17. It's an ignorant jab to discredit anything popular as being without merit. Loads of people said Facebook was a just silly toy for college kids. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["GTQnarzmTOc",1]]},"Uwuv05aZ6ug":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an entertaining talk on how working memory shapes code and how to reduce cognitive load.","q":{"x":"Raymond Hettinger gave an entertaining talk [0] on a related topic.","i":30482281,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30482281,"author":"skimdesk","month":"2022-02","story":"Coding and Confusion","stance":"rec","why":"How working memory limits shape code and reduce cognitive load.","quote":"Raymond Hettinger gave an entertaining talk [0] on a related topic.","text":"Raymond Hettinger gave an entertaining talk [0] on a related topic. He taled about how the working memory limits are reflected in code, and how we can reduce the necessary cognitive load of understanding a piece of code. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24382105,"author":"sireat","month":"2020-09","story":"List of YouTube channels for improving web development and programming skills","stance":"rec","why":"Synced version of Hettinger's talk, who teaches simply.","quote":"there is a sync fixed version of the talk","text":"Raymond Hettinger is such a treasure! He keeps things simple and in the process you actually learn. I tend to overload the registers when teaching and students do not learn as much. All teachers know the feeling when students eyes glaze over, something that is so much harder to detect in the age of Zoom and Teams. PS [VIDEO] there is a sync fixed version of the talk"},{"id":47552194,"author":"zahlman","month":"2026-03","story":"Everything old is new again: memory optimization","stance":"reference","why":"Hettinger explains design philosophy of the random module.","quote":"Raymond Hettinger explains the philosophy well while discussing the `random` standard library module","text":"> It's a bit cleaner. That's pretty much the reason why. Raymond Hettinger explains the philosophy well while discussing the `random` standard library module: [VIDEO] I feel like much of this has been forgotten of late, though. From what I've seen, i's really quite hard to get anything added to the standard library unless you're a core dev who's sufficiently well liked among other core devs, in which case you can pretty much just do it. Everyone else will (understandably) be put through a PhD thesis defense, then asked to try the idea out as a PyPI package first (and somehow also popularize the package), and then if it somehow catches on that way, get declined anyway because it's easy for everyone to just get it from PyPI (see e.g. Requests). I personally was directed to PyPI once when I was proposing new methods for the builtin `str` . Where the entire point was not to have to import or instantiate anything."}],"r":[["a1zDuOPkMSw",1],["DC8tO16xdrY",1]]},"Wpzvaqypav8":{"wy":"HN commenters link it when discussions turn to dam failures, bridge collapses, or the unglamorous work of maintenance.","q":{"x":"John Oliver did a pretty good piece on infrastructure","i":11215829,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":13791782,"author":"frik","month":"2017-03","story":"Images and video showing extent of Oroville dam damage","stance":"rec","why":"Funny but very real take on infrastructure","quote":"this one is quite funny even if it's very real","text":"actually the imgur.com comments were very insightful one of several recommended videos about the topic, this one is quite funny even if it's very real: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11215829,"author":"jrockway","month":"2016-03","story":"Mosul dam engineers warn it could fail at any time, killing 1M people","stance":"rec","why":"John Oliver piece on neglected infrastructure maintenance","quote":"John Oliver did a pretty good piece on infrastructure","text":"John Oliver did a pretty good piece on infrastructure: [VIDEO] No politician gets to cut a ribbon with a giant pair of scissors when they fund routine maintenance. So why bother."},{"id":37276028,"author":"moomin","month":"2023-08","story":"Let Maintainers Be Maintainers","stance":"reference","why":"John Oliver episode on US infrastructure maintenance problems","quote":"John Oliver points out the problems America has on infrastructure maintenance","text":"This reminds me of the episode of Last Week Tonight where John Oliver points out the problems America has on infrastructure maintenance: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30115014,"author":"StephenSmith","month":"2022-01","story":"Bridge collapse in Pittsburgh’s Frick Park","stance":"reference","why":"Oliver's segment mentions the bridge with a catch structure underneath","quote":"I used to live next to the bridge mentioned here by John Oliver.","text":"I used to live next to the bridge mentioned here by John Oliver. Pittsburgh is something. [VIDEO] \"One of these arch bridges actually has a structure built under it to catch falling deck ... see that structure underneath it, they actually built that to catch falling concrete.\" ... \"They built a bridge under the bridge...\" They've since repaired this one."},{"id":26075179,"author":"obscura","month":"2021-02","story":"The World’s Aging Dams","stance":"reference","why":"John Oliver on unglamorous infrastructure funding","quote":"John Oliver","text":"Added to the problem is the general lack of funding around the world for infrastructure, owing to the fact that politicians control the purse strings and infrastructure isn't \"sexy\" [1][2]. Building something new that can be named after someone is no doubt easier to fund than the maintenance of something that already exists. \"we always forget that maintenance in perpetuity is much much harder\" I imagine that part of the problem is that the heavy maintenance for large engineering projects begins years or decades after completion. Upon completion, it's hard to imagine these huge creations falling apart; add to this an element of \"this won't be my problem (I won't be here in 50 years)\" and you've got a recipe for short-term thinking - especially by those who don't have the expertise (read: the politicians and bureaucrats). [1] [LINK] [2] John Oliver: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13697366,"author":"colanderman","month":"2017-02","story":"California Dam Crisis Could Have Been Averted","stance":"reference","why":"Example of infrastructure woes covered by John Oliver","quote":"I live in a liberal bubble and hear about infrastructure woes often enough, for example from John Oliver here","text":"I live in a liberal bubble and hear about infrastructure woes often enough, for example from John Oliver here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14297995,"author":"spc476","month":"2017-05","story":"Maintainers make the world go round: Innovation is an overrated ideology","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"You know, if Hollywood made the blockbuster movie \"Infrastructure\" [1], maintenance might be views as a \"good thing.\" [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["nq_SpRBXRmE",1],["zT58pzY41wA",1],["9q3uNcJh4pc",1],["cIcbAMO6sxo",1]]},"b5P3QDm61go":{"wy":"Cited in iMac G4 threads as a charming ad reminiscent of Pixar's Luxo Jr.","q":{"x":"I still remember this ad for the G4 which feels very similar to Luxo Jr. from Pixar. Really fun.","i":28802885,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":43190581,"author":"bsimpson","month":"2025-02","story":"iMac G4(K)","stance":"rec","why":"A great 2002 iMac G4 ad","quote":"there may be people here who are too young to remember how great the ad for this was","text":"I'm realizing that there may be people here who are too young to remember how great the ad for this was: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28802885,"author":"filereaper","month":"2021-10","story":"M1 Inside a G4 Sunflower iMac","stance":"rec","why":"Playful ad reminiscent of Luxo Jr.","quote":"I still remember this ad for the G4 which feels very similar to Luxo Jr. from Pixar. Really fun.","text":"Totally agree about the playfulness. I still remember this ad for the G4 which feels very similar to Luxo Jr. from Pixar. Really fun. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24751909,"author":"tonyedgecombe","month":"2020-10","story":"iMac PowerPC G4","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the iMac G4 lamp design whose arm drooped","quote":"The weak link was the arm, they tended to suffer from droop after a while and it was difficult to repair.","text":"The weak link was the arm, they tended to suffer from droop after a while and it was difficult to repair. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["2y5Pv4yN0b0",1],["Hqw57GJSrac",1],["BzB5xtGGsTc",1],["HSxSgbNQi-g",1],["F9cO3-MLHOM",1],["ue-1JoJQaEg",1],["DgVS-zXgMTk",1],["0UjpmT5noto",1],["MkJkyMuBm3g",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["1fZTOjd_bOQ",1]]},"c1QmB5bW_WQ":{"wy":"Pointed to as a lay-audience explanation of nuclear waste, safety and economics.","q":{"x":"Not only is it solvable, it's basically been solved for years.","i":29658361,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29658361,"author":"mhh__","month":"2021-12","story":"Germany is closing half of its reactors before the end of the year","stance":"rec","why":"Lay-audience explanation that nuclear waste is essentially solved.","quote":"Not only is it solvable, it's basically been solved for years.","text":"Not only is it solvable, it's basically been solved for years. [VIDEO] is aimed at the lay audience. The issue is that radiation is a silent killer, so it is extremely easy people with vested interests in (say) fossil fuels to exploit our inability to access risk. Coal hurts people every single day, but it doesn't have the same poetic weight as radiation so this is lost on the general public. Nuclear power is expensive, however. Dealing with the waste is also an issue but firstly, forcing us to deal with the waste seriously is potentially not such a bad thing (assuming we need nuclear in the long run, which unless storage comes a very long way we presumably will), and secondly this is the kind of question where a government with a lot of political capital needs to just find some deserted land to build a long term solution."},{"id":28090641,"author":"marto1","month":"2021-08","story":"You've got $30B to spend and a climate crisis. Nuclear or solar?","stance":"rec","why":"Channel covering nuclear energy economics and operation.","quote":"check out the Illinois Energy youtube channel.","text":"On that note for anyone interested in the economics(and working in general) of nuclear energy check out the Illinois Energy youtube channel. Here's a quiz video of theirs: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31093788,"author":"AareyBaba","month":"2022-04","story":"Utilities Want to Convert Coal Plants to Nuclear; Skeptics Abound","stance":"reference","why":"Covers nuclear accident scenarios and automatic shutdown.","quote":"This video (llinois EnergyProf) covers the accident scenarios for nuclear power","text":"This video (llinois EnergyProf) covers the accident scenarios for nuclear power [VIDEO] Short answer, the design is such that reactor shuts down automatically with loss of cooling."}],"r":[["uxykI30fS54",1],["GJCF-Ufapu8",1],["cIQ9IXSUzuM",1]]},"ffngZOB1U2A":{"wy":"HN points to it as a good documentary on GOG's history and the difficulty of tracking down rights holders.","q":{"x":"Noclip has a great documentary about it","i":30594569,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30594569,"author":"wildpeaks","month":"2022-03","story":"Russia to legalize software piracy","stance":"rec","why":"History of GOG's origin in a piracy-heavy market","quote":"Noclip has a great documentary about it","text":"An interesting point from the history of GOG is that it originated from a market where piracy was the norm, Noclip has a great documentary about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18128930,"author":"TimGremalm","month":"2018-10","story":"10 years of gog.com","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary about GOG","quote":"Noclip did an interesting documentary about GOG.","text":"Noclip did an interesting documentary about GOG. \"GOG: Preserving Gaming's Past & Future\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":45834648,"author":"aequitas","month":"2025-11","story":"Internet Archive's legal fights are over, but its founder mourns what was lost","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"GOG shows that paying for drm-free games in a country where copyright was unenforced and piracy offered a better UX can still be profitable: [LINK] , [VIDEO]"},{"id":45620896,"author":"mr_sturd","month":"2025-10","story":"GOG has had to hire private investigators to track down IP rights holders","stance":"reference","why":"Notes difficulty of tracking down rights holders","quote":"half of the battle was tracking down rights holders","text":"I'm sure this was alluded to in NOCLIP's YouTube documentary on GOG[0]. It was said then that half of the battle was tracking down rights holders as IP had sometimes been absorbed through multiple acquisitions by the time they came to restore some games. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":33887592,"author":"teawrecks","month":"2022-12","story":"Apple announces upgrade to App Store pricing","stance":"reference","why":"Describes how software proliferated in Poland during early computers","quote":"Check out this brief description of how software proliferated in Poland during the early years of computers (3:09-8:27)","text":"Something else to consider is motivating piracy. If they sell a piece of software for a flat price in all geos, the ones who can't afford it will invest more time in pirating the software. As a side effect, piracy is normalized in those communities as a necessary way of life in the digital world. Naturally, their tools and methods will leak out to other communities and make piracy easier in places that CAN afford them. Devs would rather sell to those communities at a \"loss\" than ostracize them and deal with the fallout. Check out this brief description of how software proliferated in Poland during the early years of computers (3:09-8:27): [VIDEO]"},{"id":29260422,"author":"mrkramer","month":"2021-11","story":"Microsoft gaming chief calls for industry-wide game preservation","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary on GOG preserving old games","quote":"GOG.com[1][2][3] revives and preserves old video games.","text":"GOG.com[1][2][3] revives and preserves old video games. [1] gog.com [2] [LINK] [3] GOG: Preserving Gaming's Past & Future Documentary: [VIDEO]"}]},"gTmhS6hcY-A":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it whenever Chuck E. Cheese animatronics come up, as the best history of the band's direct ancestor and its devoted preservationists.","q":{"x":"There is an excellent documentary on the history and some of the fans preserving the technology.","i":40333694,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42359953,"author":"tracerbulletx","month":"2024-12","story":"Chuck E. Cheese's animatronics band bows out","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on the direct ancestor of the band","quote":"Anybody vaguely interested in this story would probably enjoy this documentary","text":"Anybody vaguely interested in this story would probably enjoy this documentary about the the direct ancestor of this band. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40333694,"author":"doug_life","month":"2024-05","story":"Farewell, Chuck E. Cheese Animatronic Band","stance":"rec","why":"History of the Rock-afire band and fans preserving its technology","quote":"There is an excellent documentary on the history and some of the fans preserving the technology.","text":"Somewhat related is the The Rock-afire Explosion band from ShowBiz Pizza Place. There is an excellent documentary on the history and some of the fans preserving the technology. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34395756,"author":"deadly_syn","month":"2023-01","story":"Chuck E. Cheese still uses floppy disks in 2023, but not for long","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"WARNING: RANTY STREAM OF CONCIOUSNESS ESSAY AHEAD I just recently started building FOSS in this space in stealthmode, things are not looking good long term. Animatronics as an artform are facing extinction. There are VERY few people in the community with technical expertise to maintain the mechanical and technical aspects of the characters. And companies are abandoning them en masse in favor of screens. To illustrate the HN relevant challenges lets walk through the \"stack\" of a singing rat. You have the mech itself, its guts a stationary frame made of some material with mechanical extremities attached to it. that are usually animated using either compressed air, or servos. As well as some kind of control board witha bank of relays for actuating said servos/valves. Each character is then connected to a central control box that takes in movements for the entire stage as well as audio or even video to play back in sync. Those movements are all encoded differently just to make this super easy btw :) Lets actually talk about movements next! There are many different companies that produced different shows for Familiy Entertainment Centers and theme parks, all of them programming movements on bespoke systems, as no standard had ever existed. Those movements being stored in a variety of ways. Many of the original Chuck E Cheese and Showbiz Pizza place signals from the late 70s to the early 90s being stored on Reel to Reel tape. Left audio channel being the human audio and then the ri"}]},"ulCdoCfw-bY":{"wy":"Linked as a nice summary of how civilizations could harvest black hole energy.","q":{"x":"I also watched this in the past, which has some fun ideas","i":28168714,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28168714,"author":"kadoban","month":"2021-08","story":"What if Planet 9 is a Primordial Black Hole? (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Fun ideas about harvesting black hole energy with mirrors.","quote":"I also watched this in the past, which has some fun ideas","text":"There's also the Penrose process which is theoretically useful on spinning black holes. I also watched this in the past, which has some fun ideas (that are probably a little fanciful?) [VIDEO] tl;dw: put a mirror around it and shine light in, get back lots of energy and/or blow up your whole solar system. I'm not sure if any of that would apply to a ~planet mass black hole spinning with ~planet sized angular momentum, or how solid the physics even is, but it sounds cool anyway."},{"id":17432185,"author":"Malic","month":"2018-06","story":"What life could be like for civilizations 1 trillion years from now","stance":"rec","why":"Kurzgesagt on black holes as homes for civilizations.","quote":"Kurzgesagt had some nice summaries of","text":"Kurzgesagt had some nice summaries of: 1) Why white dwarves might be the last home for life in the universe - [LINK] 2) ...or maybe it will be black holes - [VIDEO]"},{"id":28231403,"author":"Qub3d","month":"2021-08","story":"A Dyson sphere around a black hole","stance":"reference","why":"Steal rotational energy from spinning black holes.","quote":"Kurtzgesagt made a video explaining some possibilities.","text":"Kurtzgesagt made a video explaining some possibilities. In short, you can throw mass or radiation at a spinning black hole and \"steal\" rotational energy from it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19580920,"author":"ainar-g","month":"2019-04","story":"Saudi Arabia threatens to ditch dollar oil trades to stop 'NOPEC'","stance":"reference","why":"Extracting energy from black holes.","quote":"","text":"I used to play with an idea of a science fiction novel, where the united Earth's currency is essentially kcal/kJ. The problem is that if you have a flammable and radioactive material, do you count the energy you get from it by burning it, or the energy from throwing it into a nuclear fission reactor, or the energy of throwing it into a black hole in a particular angle[1]? [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LrObZ_HZZUc",1],["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["z1ROpIKZe-c",1],["RJh9yTIBY48",1],["JTfhYyTuT44",1]]},"zdkyGDqU7xA":{"wy":"Pointed to by hobbyists interested in building flat-panel DIY speakers from cheap materials.","q":{"x":"where he goes into all sorts of experiments he ran on the exciters to find the right design.","i":18354769,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":31180424,"author":"staindk","month":"2022-04","story":"MIT researchers develop a paper thin loudspeaker","stance":"rec","why":"First in a series of DIY flat speaker videos.","quote":"Interesting stuff.","text":"Yeah was going to say, this reminded me of this[1] video (series of videos actually, but this is the first in the series). Interesting stuff. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18354769,"author":"linsomniac","month":"2018-11","story":"Build Your Own Professional-Grade Audio Amp on the Sort-Of Cheap","stance":"rec","why":"Experiments on cheap exciters to find the right speaker design.","quote":"where he goes into all sorts of experiments he ran on the exciters to find the right design.","text":"I kind of want to build one of these amps and connect it to a set of speaker built with these cheap exciters... One of the Youtube channels I follow messed around with them [LINK] but he got that from this other video [VIDEO] where he goes into all sorts of experiments he ran on the exciters to find the right design."},{"id":38170246,"author":"linsomniac","month":"2023-11","story":"Ask HN: Comment here about whatever you're passionate about at the moment","stance":"reference","why":"Flat foam-core panel speakers.","quote":"I keep toying with building either the flat panel speakers made out of foam-core board from Tech Ingredients on YouTube","text":"What sort are you thinking about building? I keep toying with building either the flat panel speakers made out of foam-core board from Tech Ingredients on YouTube, or the big giant speakers he made. [VIDEO] [LINK] edited: Thought DIY Perks did the big speakers, found them on Tech Ingredients."},{"id":18102213,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2018-09","story":"E-Ink Monitors: Ready for Prime Time?","stance":"reference","why":"Acoustic exciters turn sheet material into speakers.","quote":"","text":"A 40 Hz model is already down to $1000. We may be on the cusp of some kind of sea change here. How insanely light could an E-Ink flatscreen monitor get? You don't need a backlight, so the power requirements would be much smaller and that would be a huge advantage to making something light. I could imagine an updated version of a \"Sunflower\" iMac built using one. MacOS resembling a magazine seems to be what Steve Jobs envisioned and what Apple is aiming for right now. Could they be made in such a way as to resemble good sheet material for acoustic exciters? [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["SKhcan8pk2w",1],["BickMFHAZR0",1],["8AyHH9G9et0",1],["-k-V3ESHcfA",1],["Wpv-Qb-dB6g",1]]},"4rTv9wvvat8":{"wy":"Recommended as a great visual explanation of the infall experience, including when spaghettification happens.","q":{"x":"see this great YouTube video of what you would see if you fell into a Black Hole.","i":30697798,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30697798,"author":"bocytron","month":"2022-03","story":"Visualizing black holes with general relativistic ray tracing","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains what you would see falling into a black hole.","quote":"see this great YouTube video of what you would see if you fell into a Black Hole.","text":"Awesome. Also, see this great YouTube video of what you would see if you fell into a Black Hole. Is also has a very good explanations of what you are seeing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44246627,"author":"ben_w","month":"2025-06","story":"The Gentle Singularity","stance":"reference","why":"Shows you still see your own legs crossing the horizon.","quote":"","text":"While I prefer \"event horizon\" over \"singularity\", part of the reason I blogged about this distinction years ago was that the event horizon always seems to be ahead of you as you fall in to a black hole*. My blog posts didn't age all that well, and I've learned to be a little more sceptical about the speed of technological change, just as the political events over the intervening years have made me more aware of how fast political realities can change: [LINK] and [LINK] * at least until the rate of change of curvature gets so high you're spaghetti, you're (approximately) co-moving with the light from your own body. This means that when you cross the event horizon, you still see your own legs, even though the space the light is in is moving towards the singularity faster than the light itself moves through that space: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32063893,"author":"y0ssar1an","month":"2022-07","story":"Deepest infrared image of universe","stance":"reference","why":"Spaghettification only occurs near the singularity.","quote":"According to this video, the spaghettification doesn't happen until the last few seconds/minutes","text":"According to this video, the spaghettification doesn't happen until the last few seconds/minutes, when you're close to the singularity. [VIDEO]"}]},"Nyvxt1svxso":{"wy":"Quoted whenever threads touch on elites, exclusivity and education, with 'it's a big club' as shorthand.","q":{"x":"Thoughtful, funny, masterfully delivered.","i":39918941,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39918941,"author":"Ozzie_osman","month":"2024-04","story":"George Carlin estate forces \"AI Carlin\" off the Internet for good","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite clip, thoughtful and funny","quote":"Thoughtful, funny, masterfully delivered.","text":"One of my favorite clips ever is [VIDEO] It's a big club and you ain't in it! Thoughtful, funny, masterfully delivered."},{"id":48039559,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2026-05","story":"The Disadvantages of an Elite Education (2008)","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin commentary cited on nepotism and exclusivity","quote":"I'm reminded of the prescient 2005 commentary of George Carlin","text":"I'm reminded of the prescient 2005 commentary of George Carlin: \"it's a big club and you ain't in it\" [1]. Hollywood, in particular, is almost completely nepo baby captured. This is an oft-repeated trend where an industry goes into decline and the children of those who originally succeeded end up dominating it. I think there's a lot of this in politics too, particularly because government jobs (including staffers on campaigns and for representatives) don't pay a lot so you really have to come from an affluent background to afford to live. It used to be that if you wanted to be a cast member on SNL you had to go to Harvard because of the Harvard Lampoon. I've seen this issue with the doctor pipeline too. Various analyses show that coming from a high socioeconomic background is a massive advantage, even with med schools trying to provide more opportunities to candidates from a lower socioeconomic background. A few med schools now because of endowments have gone tuition-free but even here it seems (it's early days) like wealthier candidates get more of these opportunities. As a wealthier person you don't need to \"waste\" time on a job. You can do resume-packing activities (research, volunteering). So circling back, elite education's role (IMHO) is to be exclusionary. It's to maintain this structure. \"Social proof\" is extremely important because a lot of opportunities in life aren't about talent or skill but connections and social factors. You go to Stanford and do CS and you get "},{"id":47274755,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2026-03","story":"Elite Overproduction","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin's point that most people aren't in the club","quote":"As George Carlin said, you're not in it [1].","text":"There's a double-whammy here of the Myth of Meritocracy under capitalism and Prosperity Gospel under Protestantism. After the Civil War we compensated slave owners . After the GFC we bailed out the banks. The government giving money to poor people is somehow a moral hazard yet the wealthy not only expect government handouts, they demand them. What's interesting AI is I think many people in tech don't yet realize how this is going to impact them. Jobs losses, depressed wages, unpaid extra work and constant layoff churn. The heady heights of the 2010s will seem like a fairy tale. Except this time, unlike a century ago, there is no labor movement. It's been decimated. There is no effective pushback against further wealth concentration to like 100,000 people. The Jeff Bezoes of the world will demand even more government money so they can have $205 billion instead of $200 billion and things will get really bad until eventually we have a Russian or french type revolution. Too many people think it's a big club when it isn't. As George Carlin said, you're not in it [1]. People actively advocate for their own worsening material conditions because they're deluded into thinking they'll be Jeff Bezos one day. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45646418,"author":"mindcrash","month":"2025-10","story":"Carefully Educated to Be Idiots","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin on elites wanting obedient workers","quote":"George Carlin said it best way back in 2005","text":"George Carlin said it best way back in 2005: \"[...]THEY’VE GOT YOU BY THE BALLS! They spend billions of dollars every year lobbying, lobbying to get what they want. Well, we know what they want. They want more for themselves and less for everybody else. But I’ll tell you what they don’t want. They don’t want a population of citizens capable of critical thinking. They don’t want well-informed, well educated people capable of critical thinking. They’re not interested in that. That doesn’t help them. That’s against their interest. That’s right. They don’t want people who are smart enough to sit around the kitchen table and figure out how badly they’re getting fucked by a system that threw them overboard 30 fucking years ago. They don’t want that. You know what they want? They want OBEDIENT WORKERS. OBEDIENT WORKERS. People who are just smart enough to run the machines and do the paperwork and just dumb enough to passively accept all these increasingly shittier jobs with the lower pay, the longer hours, the reduced benefits, the end of overtime and the vanishing pension that disappears the minute you go to collect it.[...]\" [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":41155441,"author":"zoogeny","month":"2024-08","story":"Apprentice, Journeyman, and Master: The Medieval Guild (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin bit cited on exclusivity of guilds","quote":"George Carlin has a famous comedy bit where he states \"It's a big club and you ain't in it\" [1].","text":"It is important to note, especially given the positive view given to the guild system in this article, that it is written on a masonic philosophy page. The freemasons have a long and esoteric history that should be viewed with some skepticism. Secret fraternal orders have a sketchy history. George Carlin has a famous comedy bit where he states \"It's a big club and you ain't in it\" [1]. My own feeling is that guilds of all sorts prioritize exclusivity for the purposes of bestowing power on some select few. As the article states \"Master’s were few and far between\". This is similar to how luxury brands maintain their high value: exclusivity. The standardization and guarantees of quality seem to be secondary to the pyramid scheme nature of ascension within these organizations (in the same way that the quality of luxury goods is often secondary to their exclusivity). It reminds me in some ways of the concept of \"familiars\" in vampire lore, humans who willingly toil away for their masters hoping one day to be elevated to the same level. It is a complex topic because of the positives and negatives of these systems being highly intertwined. To this day in Canada there is an apprenticeship system for trades. However, it is no longer an inner circle of masters deciding who gets the special status, it is a regional qualification body with clear guidelines, training, testing and certification. As a society we haven't at all gotten away from the degenerate aspects of guilds. Think of the "},{"id":37733156,"author":"fwungy","month":"2023-10","story":"Fine, I'll run a regression analysis. But it won't make you happy","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's a big club, and you ain't in it. - George Carlin","text":"Not really. They do it all the time. Legislator votes for the right bills and her niece gets a cushy job somewhere in exchange. You can't prove anything wrong happened. It's a big club, and you ain't in it. - George Carlin [VIDEO]"},{"id":35514489,"author":"StillBored","month":"2023-04","story":"TSMC Sales Miss for Second Straight Quarter on Tepid Demand","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"How about some George Carlin, RIP","text":"I sorta think your conflating authoritarian socialism (aka communism) with democratic socialism which is as large a mistake as conflating pure democracy with corrupt representative democracy where a minority of the population is used to rubber stamp an oligarchy. Similarly capitalism in its purest forms is basically feudalism because the \"people\" won't have any dollars to vote with. And time and time again it seems the benevolent Henry Ford style capitalists are proven to be the minority. So one might say that without the socialist scare in the late 1800's early 1900's which forced the hands of the capitalists all those quality of live improvements everyone like to go on about probably wouldn't exist. We might all by working 80 hour weeks and scraping by in company hovels eating whatever gruel the company store gives us. But then again, a large portion of the US population is wildly unaware of how the bottom 30% live, while ignoring their own debt slavery. So enjoy your bread/circuses while the rich take an even larger slice of the economic pie. edit: How about some George Carlin, RIP [VIDEO]"}]},"bKH5nGLgi08":{"wy":"Cited in discussions of open firmware and trust as a thorough reverse engineering of the PSP.","q":{"x":"(Btw, watch the whole talk, it's nothing short of incredible.)","i":28473962,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28473962,"author":"TobTobXX","month":"2021-09","story":"Ministry of Freedom – GNU+Linux laptops with Libreboot preinstalled","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Reverse engineering of PSP; found nothing evil","quote":"(Btw, watch the whole talk, it's nothing short of incredible.)","text":"There's this great talk from a CCC about reverse engeneering the PSP: Uncover, Understand, Own - Regaining Control Over Your AMD CPU [VIDEO] At 47:10, they mention that they haven't found anything evil. Ofc, this isn't hard proof, but if I trust anyone's answer, then it's theirs. (Btw, watch the whole talk, it's nothing short of incredible.)"},{"id":28609825,"author":"TobTobXX","month":"2021-09","story":"The Framework is the most exciting laptop I've used","stance":"rec","why":"Reverse engineering the PSP found nothing evil","quote":"There's this great talk from a CCC about reverse engeneering the PSP","text":"There's this great talk from a CCC about reverse engeneering the PSP: Uncover, Understand, Own - Regaining Control Over Your AMD CPU [VIDEO] At 47:10, they mention that they haven't found anything evil. Ofc, this isn't hard proof, but if I trust anyone's answer, then it's theirs. I think the likelihood of it being malicious is nonzero, but small enough that I'd condemn active backdoors into the realm of conspiracy theories. There's always the possibility of it being exploited by others, but c'mon: Basically ANY other exploit would be way easier to distribute and activate than one in the PSP."},{"id":28480195,"author":"garaetjjte","month":"2021-09","story":"Libreboot: Freedom-Respecting Boot Firmware","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to show PSP cannot be disabled","quote":"","text":"That's switch for TPM features only. You cannot disable PSP as it is responsible for x86 initialization. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26399533,"author":"didericis","month":"2021-03","story":"I want a computer that I own","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence analysts think the PSP is safe","quote":"","text":"If you’re referring to Intel ME and the AMD PSP, people who have analyzed the PSP seem to think it’s safe: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21908889,"author":"peter_d_sherman","month":"2019-12","story":"Uncover, Understand, Own – Regaining Control over Your AMD CPU [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Posts the talk abstract on the AMD PSP","quote":"","text":"Excerpt: \"The AMD Platform Security Processor (PSP) is a dedicated ARM CPU inside your AMD processor and runs undocumented, proprietary firmware provided by AMD. It is a processor inside your processor that you don't control. It is essential for system startup. In fact, in runs before the main processor is even started and is responsible for bootstrapping all other components. This talk presents our efforts investigating the PSP internals and functionality and how you can better understand it. Our talk is divided into three parts: The first part covers the firmware structure of the PSP and how we analyzed this proprietary firmware. We will demonstrate how to extract and replace individual firmware components of the PSP and how to observe the PSP during boot. The second part covers the functionality of the PSP and how it interacts with other components of the x86 CPU like the DRAM controller or System Management Unit (SMU). We will present our method to gain access to the, otherwise hidden, debug output. The talk concludes with a security analysis of the PSP firmware. We will demonstrate how to provide custom firmare to run on the PSP and introduce our toolchain that helps building custom applications for the PSP. This talk documents the PSP firmware's proprietary filesystem and provides insights into reverse-engineering such a deeply embedded system. It further sheds light on how we might regain trust in AMD CPUs despite the delicate nature of the PSP.\" Researchers: Robert Bu"}]},"4loZGYqaZ7I":{"wy":"Recommended as a fun, detailed account of how the PS3's security was broken.","q":{"x":"talk[1] at 27C3 on jailbreaking the PS3 is really fun, and they discuss this in detail.","i":10738213,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10738213,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2015-12","story":"Developer claims 'PS4 officially jailbroken'","stance":"rec","why":"Fun talk on jailbreaking PS3 and Sony's ECDSA mistakes.","quote":"talk[1] at 27C3 on jailbreaking the PS3 is really fun, and they discuss this in detail.","text":"DRM does not require a closed/controlled ecosystem. The best protection against the development of game-copying modchips on the PS3 was the ability to run Linux on it as an official feature. The serious jailbreaking (which necessarily allowed game-copying) was done only after they pissed off the \"other os\" crowd by removing it in a later patch. (the fail0verflow's talk[1] at 27C3 on jailbreaking the PS3 is really fun, and they discuss this in detail. (also: as discussed about 35min into [1], using the same nonce for the ECDSA signature of every game? So the keys can be derived from two games, without knowing most of the curve parameters? What was Sony thinking?!) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7055944,"author":"nemothekid","month":"2014-01","story":"Requirements for DRM in HTML are confidential","stance":"reference","why":"Reveals the PS3 DRM was badly implemented.","quote":"Once a magnifying glass was put under the PS3","text":"The PS3 only went uncracked for so long because Sony offered the option to install Linux on the PS3. Once Sony removed that option the people with the smarts to crack the PS3 did so. Once a magnifying glass was put under the PS3 it turned out that the \"DRM\" was actually horribly implemented (like Rand() functions that were essentially returning constants). [VIDEO]"},{"id":2622002,"author":"rkalla","month":"2011-06","story":"Sony Music Brazil hacked ","stance":"reference","why":"fail0verflow's PS3 security break at 27C3.","quote":"fail0verflow group circumvents the PS3's security system using this work from GeoHot","text":"More or less. 1. Sony removes \"Install Other Operating Systems\" option from the PS3 OS. 2. Out of frustration at corporate policy for REMOVING major features from hardware/device paid for and owned by millions, the hackers start working. 3. Months later, GeoHot releases (what I understand to be) the root private encryption keys for the device. 3.5 (forgot this) fail0verflow group circumvents the PS3's security system using this work from GeoHot - [VIDEO] 4. Other hacking groups, now with the keys to the kingdom, begin working on hacking the PS3 to allow the installation of any software, not just officially released/signed/blessed releases. This results in a \"jailbreak\" for the PS3, much like what jailbreaking does to the lock-down security on an iPhone. (This is when things start to go south) 5. A technique for loading your own software onto the PS3, circumventing the system's security checks comes out. (Now the door to pirating PS3 games is open. Download images, burn the Blu-rays, pop them in the PS3 and play). 6. Another hacking group, using some portion of this manipulation, actually manage to get their PS3's logged into the private developer-based PSN network (it's a full copy of the real PSN network specifically for developers actively working on titles that need to test things like updates or addon downloads/installs). 7. It is discovered that the PSN-Dev network does not do real credit card authentication before items are purchased and downloaded. So for example, if I"}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1],["BYRTvoZ3Rho",1]]},"7k1ehaE0bdU":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a primary source for what Zuckerberg says in his own words, such as on AI writing most new code, WhatsApp, and Apple interoperability.","q":{"x":"I think a reasonable person would also listen to what he says about his own motivations.","i":42837025,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42715939,"author":"tim333","month":"2025-01","story":"Meta announces 5% cuts in preparation for 'intense year'","stance":"rec","why":"Zuckerberg interview with calm discussion of masculine energy","quote":"it's quite a good interview","text":"No not really - it's quite a good interview. Here's the masculine energy bit if you want to judge for yourself. You only have to watch a couple of minutes to get an idea [VIDEO] To me \"ranting\" is not true. He calmly says he was brought up with girls and it's quite nice go get out and do martial arts for a change. To me that's normal and getting upset about 'men' not getting free tampons is kinda weird."},{"id":42837025,"author":"johnmw","month":"2025-01","story":"Mark Zuckerberg: This Man Is a Coward","stance":"rec","why":"Zuckerberg explains his own motivations in detail","quote":"I think a reasonable person would also listen to what he says about his own motivations.","text":"I don't have a horse in this race, but to be fair, Mark goes into great detail about all this on the Joe Rogan podcast [0]. I think a reasonable person would also listen to what he says about his own motivations. If you have already listened and have an opinion, then please ignore this suggestion! [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":48717432,"author":"vlod","month":"2026-06","story":"GitHub – librepods-org/librepods: AirPods liberated from Apple's ecosystem","stance":"reference","why":"Zuckerberg on Apple interoperability with Ray-Bans and AirPods","quote":"I listened to Mark Zuck on Rogan about his experience with Apple connecting his Ray Bands to the AirPods.","text":"In regards to Apple hostility. There's obviously the whole app store shenanigans where devs often feel that they have arbitrary rules. DHH has talked about it as has Tim Sweeney. I listened to Mark Zuck on Rogan about his experience with Apple connecting his Ray Bands to the AirPods. [0] In that interview, he suggests they won't help with the integration (for stupid reasons) and that he expects that'll come out with a competing product. I think being a dev and working with the Apple eco (outside of 'normal') is a pita. Yes it's subjective. I'm hesitant to even purchase Apple's excellent hardware for this reason. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43569800,"author":"floralhangnail","month":"2025-04","story":"Matrix.org Will Migrate to MAS","stance":"reference","why":"Zuckerberg alludes Meta can screenshot WhatsApp messages","quote":"At about 2:33:15 here, Zuckerberg somewhat alludes that Meta can take screenshots of WhatsApp messages.","text":"At about 2:33:15 here, Zuckerberg somewhat alludes that Meta can take screenshots of WhatsApp messages. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42891662,"author":"GoatInGrey","month":"2025-01","story":"Meta in talks to reincorporate in Texas or another state, WSJ reports","stance":"reference","why":"Zuckerberg on AI writing most new code, with human augmentation","quote":"He said that they anticipate the majority of new code to come from AI models rather than human engineers.","text":"> a week later stating that the LLMs that his researchers / engineers are working on will soon be able to replace them. This is a pessimistic interpretation of Mark's words that has been trumpeted in the media. Which I am appalled to admit. He said that they anticipate the majority of new code to come from AI models rather than human engineers. He then adds that they expect developers to be augmented by these tools. Which tracks as you still need somebody to drive the AI and validate or correct their outputs. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["IkdziSLYzHw",1],["YMqgiXLjvRs",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["OxhTQdcieQE",1],["ob_GX50Za6c",1],["lcjdwSY2AzM",1],["baFaEvBywGc",1],["obCjODeoLVw",1],["iSVHp6CAyQ8",1],["J-M98KLgaUU",1],["9NOncx2jU0Q",1],["Ak4on5uTaTg",1]]},"7q9vIMXSTzc":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a detailed source on Servo's design and performance, including plans to move Servo code into Gecko.","q":{"x":"I think the LCA 2015 one[1] has the most details in that regard.","i":15076717,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14104587,"author":"buster","month":"2017-04","story":"Servo nightly builds on Windows now available","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation on Rust and Servo.","quote":"There is another nice presentation on Rust and Servo","text":"After seeing a youtube demo[[1] of how the aggressive parallelisation that is done in Servo (safely possible by using Rust) speeds up this browser, i'm really excited for Servo! Also, i think this will give us a great base for all kinds of browser embedding what is not based on ancient webkit-gtk. There is another nice presentation on Rust and Servo: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":15076717,"author":"metajack","month":"2017-08","story":"Inside a fast CSS engine","stance":"reference","why":"Contains older Servo performance numbers.","quote":"I think the LCA 2015 one[1] has the most details in that regard.","text":"I suspect you will hear a lot of numbers around the time of the 57 launch, and until then people will keep quiet as they try to make the numbers that get revealed as good as possible. For old numbers, you can get some in some of my old presentations at conferences. I think the LCA 2015 one[1] has the most details in that regard. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15077263,"author":"lkbm","month":"2017-08","story":"Inside a fast CSS engine","stance":"reference","why":"Briefly mentions power usage with parallelization.","quote":"Metajack mention power usage with parallelization briefly here[0], but doesn't provide the data.","text":"Metajack mention power usage with parallelization briefly here[0], but doesn't provide the data. [0] [VIDEO] (2015)"},{"id":8904132,"author":"gulpahum","month":"2015-01","story":"Servo: Building a Parallel Browser [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Talk's future plans: moving Servo code into Gecko.","quote":"they show the future plans and the plan is to start using bits of Servo code in Gecko","text":"In the video (at 35:39), they show the future plans and the plan is to start using bits of Servo code in Gecko. So, they will replace small parts of C++ code with Rust. [VIDEO] One plan was to first replace easy parts, like image decoders (bmp/ico formats). [LINK] It won't be a one big replacement, more like a gradual take over."}]},"Suugn-p5C1M":{"wy":"It is the go-to demonstration HN links whenever traffic shockwaves, phantom jams or cascading failures come up.","q":{"x":"They told 22 drivers to drive in a circular path at 30 km/h. A traffic jam appeared, and moved backward.","i":785303,"y":"2009"},"m":[{"id":6478463,"author":"DigitalJack","month":"2013-10","story":"How traffic actually works","stance":"rec","why":"Circular track study showing back-propagating waves from constant-speed driving.","quote":"but have a look at this video.","text":"This article accounts for some of what causes traffic jams, but have a look at this video. It shows a study done where people tried to drive on a circular track at a constant speed. Things start out okay, but it quickly devolves into the back propagating wave. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46953891,"author":"asdff","month":"2026-02","story":"Hard-braking events as indicators of road segment crash risk","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how stop-and-go traffic snowballs from misjudged follow distance.","quote":"This video shows how stop and go traffic forms and snowballs with no real impetus beyond mis estimated follow distance.","text":"[VIDEO] This is the natural response of tapping the brakes for any slowdown: people naturally over compensating and chain reactions happening behind them. This video shows how stop and go traffic forms and snowballs with no real impetus beyond mis estimated follow distance."},{"id":44742198,"author":"mitthrowaway2","month":"2025-07","story":"A short post on short trains","stance":"reference","why":"Classic experiment demonstrating traffic jams from positive feedback in following distance.","quote":"here is a classic experiment demonstrating the effect","text":"I'm going to double-down on this point because it does matter. In the real world some people are tailgating, but that does not cause traffic jams if and only if the road is running below peak capacity, where negative feedback self-corrects those traffic density deviations and gradually smooths everything back out. (Unless the tailgater causes a crash, of course). But if the average following distance is such that the road is exactly at the peak of throughput, or any smaller, then any momentary dip into tighter following distances pushes the road it into a positive feedback operating mode, which triggers a traffic jam. If you haven't seen it, here is a classic experiment demonstrating the effect: [VIDEO] (But obviously the solution to traffic isn't 5 more lanes, it's viable alternatives to driving!)"},{"id":44330358,"author":"8note","month":"2025-06","story":"Congestion pricing in Manhattan is a predictable success","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"how do you explain phenomena like shockwave traffic jams","text":"as far as i can read, your argument is that traffic jams are impossible, because congestion acts as a dynamic cost function to keep the road at its highest utility, when the throughput is highest. unless you disagree with the that definition of the utility of the road? how do you explain phenomena like shockwave traffic jams, where otherwise high utility roads get sections of nearly stopped traffic. eg. [VIDEO] in a closed system (30s of video) can you spend some time showing your work, and both propose and prove what the cost function of congestion is? then, it should be clear whether the government set cost is higher or lower, and under what conditions. id especially want to see the limiting behaviour - standstill traffic. my gur sense is that the cost of congestion should be going towards infinity, but im interested in how the constant value from the government is still higher."},{"id":39685149,"author":"cyanydeez","month":"2024-03","story":"My Clients, the Liars","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how one person stopping creates a lasting kink in traffic.","quote":"you have one person suddenly stop and now there's a permanent kink in the behavior of the traffic.","text":"[VIDEO] basically, people are dealing with a ghost. you have one person suddenly stop and now there's a permanent kink in the behavior of the traffic."},{"id":30555611,"author":"lotsofpulp","month":"2022-03","story":"Tesla driver dies after his Model 3 stops on highway, gets hit by two other","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates that near road capacity, stop-and-go traffic is unavoidable.","quote":"There simply is not a way to avoid the consequences of being near or above a road’s capacity.","text":"Even moving slower will not prevent stop and go traffic if the road has reached its capacity, due to the various different accelerations and decelerations of cars. My overarching point is something like this video: [VIDEO] There simply is not a way to avoid the consequences of being near or above a road’s capacity. Of course, I recommend everyone to play it safe and stay far enough back to avoid any liability of being too close to the car in front. But the reality is we make lots of calculations and choices in driving and at various times, we choose farther distances and other times nearer distances (even though we know it is riskier) and these collective decisions will propagate down through the whole road."},{"id":30723899,"author":"cogman10","month":"2022-03","story":"Looming food shortages is the next ‘slow-moving disaster’ to hit world","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"you create massive shockwaves which can pull everything from 90mph to 10mph.","text":"This was a topic of a few Asimov books. In describing the big cities of earth, the more complicated and JIT nature of getting food where it needs to be meant that where previously an hour delay was tolerable, that minute delays could severely impact everyone with massive ripple effects. It seems like what we are seeing WRT the supply chain is the equivalent of putting more and more traffic on a road and gradually increasing the speed limit to deal with delivery bottle necks (rather than making new roads/routes). Of course, the issue is that whenever someone cuts someone else off or whatever, you create massive shockwaves which can pull everything from 90mph to 10mph. [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18955450,"author":"karmakaze","month":"2019-01","story":"Altruistic Autonomy: Beating Congestion on Shared Roads","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"As density increases there starts to be shock waves that bounce longitundinally","text":"This model is overlooking some aspects of traffic flow. The most important one is that flow does not increase and decrease smoothly. As density increases there starts to be shock waves that bounce longitundinally[0]. It would also makes sense to also distribute some autonomous vehicles along the train to minimize these shock waves. A much simplier solution is to autonomously centre between the vehicle in front and rear[1]. Doing so smoothly is simple local logic which may improve flow rates dramatically but I don't know if it's been implemented anywhere. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":12978802,"author":"shopkins","month":"2016-11","story":"Fewer than 3% of cars sold in the U.S. have manual transmissions","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"see traffic shockwaves","text":"I think if it's annoying, you're not driving in traffic the right way. Sure, it's annoying for aggressive stop-and-go driving, but if you maintain a consistent speed with the traffic in front of you (see traffic shockwaves [0]), a manual transmission is better than an automatic. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10632305,"author":"exo762","month":"2015-11","story":"Why the focus should be mass transit instead of tolls to fix traffic congestion","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"this jam is a direct result of very high latency of human reaction.","text":"Self-driving cars are the necessary but not sufficient condition to solve traffic problems. Start with that and remove 1-person-1-car model [0], add ride-sharing software, ban human drivers [1] and done, no more traffic problems. As for your example - this jam is a direct result of very high latency of human reaction. It causes stop-and-go in jams [2]. [0] Or it will rot and occupy parking space for 97% of time. [1] This will likely to average speed and reduce time spent waiting for lights. [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5028749,"author":"sp332","month":"2013-01","story":"Systemic traffic jams caused by small groups of drivers, study says","stance":"reference","why":"Shows jams occurring in simple situations without any single culprit.","quote":"They can also occur in very simple situations like in this video","text":"Did you read the article? There is no single car that causes a jam. In fact according to previous research [LINK] (see \"Conclusions\" at the bottom), even if all cars follow the same rules a jam can still occur. They can also occur in very simple situations like in this video [VIDEO]"},{"id":4187258,"author":"SoftwareMaven","month":"2012-07","story":"Facebook Not Feeling Friendly With Nasdaq","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Little (and not so little) permutations causing shockwaves through the system.","text":"NASDAQ telling the market \"you have until noon Monday to prove your losses\" most definitely did cause people to dump stock in the first two days of trading. Confusion over whether trades were happening because NASDAQ wasn't confirming orders probably caused investors to be skittish, which could easily have had cascading negative effects. It's hard to say if FB's stock was overpriced. The GM thing couldn't have helped. But to say NASDAQ had nothing to do with it is silly. I look at FB's IPO and think of this[1]. Little (and not so little) permutations causing shockwaves through the system. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3608679,"author":"malandrew","month":"2012-02","story":"Computers may control intersections for self-driving cars","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I imagine that computers could completely prevent shockwave traffic jams.","text":"TBH, if you are going to have smart intersections controlling autonomous cars, you might as well try to move to roundabouts as much as possible. Roundabouts provide continuous flow and probably represent a much more solvable problem since it involves attenuating the speed of approaching vehicles and vehicles currently circling. I imagine throwing stopping into the mix complicates things significantly I imagine that computers could completely prevent shockwave traffic jams. [VIDEO]"},{"id":785303,"author":"zck","month":"2009-08","story":"Traffic Hacks: How One Driver Can Vastly Improve Traffic","stance":"reference","why":"Experiment where 22 drivers on a circle produce a spontaneous backward-moving jam.","quote":"They told 22 drivers to drive in a circular path at 30 km/h. A traffic jam appeared, and moved backward.","text":"Some Japanese scientists did a study to show how traffic jams can appear of of nowhere. They told 22 drivers to drive in a circular path at 30 km/h. A traffic jam appeared, and moved backward. The video is here: [VIDEO]"}]},"TEBMlXRjhZY":{"wy":"HN mentions it as an example of voice-driven editing, and one commenter says it clarified how Vim commands compose.","q":{"x":"I found VimSpeak [1] interesting because I somehow understood how to really compose vim commands","i":46665734,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":46665734,"author":"riedel","month":"2026-01","story":"I set all 376 Vim options and I'm still a fool","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how vim commands compose, aiding deeper understanding.","quote":"I found VimSpeak [1] interesting because I somehow understood how to really compose vim commands","text":"While I had times discovering myself to accidentally putting 'i' accidentally into MS Word docs and having vom key binding ls in VS Code (and formally eclipse) as well for that reason, I still feel clumsy. I think this is because I mostly rely on muscle memory rather than understanding deeply what I am doing to also allow me to go beyond what I normally do ) which already makes me faster than in any other editor). I found VimSpeak [1] interesting because I somehow understood how to really compose vim commands in a way [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13516863,"author":"melling","month":"2017-01","story":"Typing Practice for Programmers","stance":"reference","why":"Example of voice-driven vim editing.","quote":"We're getting closer with voice. VimSpeak","text":"An IDE does greatly reduce typing. What we need next is some advanced voice input added to our editors. This \"John Henry\" mentality that developers have about typing never seems to go away. We should be building smarter tools. We're getting closer with voice. VimSpeak: [VIDEO] Using Dragon: [LINK] Using Kaldi: [LINK] This toolkit seems to be the most widely used: [LINK] Better predictive typing would also be useful. Computer languages often greatly narrow what you probably want to type next, because of scope, for example."},{"id":13132636,"author":"pmoriarty","month":"2016-12","story":"Why I switched from Vim to Emacs","stance":"reference","why":"Voice input for vim as an example of alternative editor input.","quote":"","text":"Here's voice input for emacs: [1], [2] And for vim (inspired by [2]): [3] I'm sure gesture tracking would probably not be too hard, using any of a number of VR input devices now on the market. Eye tracking would be harder, as I'm not aware of any consumer-level eye-tracking technologies out there. You could certainly have head-tracking pretty easily, though. Now integrating this in a way that would actually be fluid, natural, and useful is another story, though. Also, I'm personally not sure how many big body movements I want to be doing while using my computer (unless I'm trying to exercise, play a game, draw, conduct music, or lose weight). Ideally, I'd want to be as motionless as possible while being as comfortable as possible and while having maximum flexibility and efficiency of input. Keyboards are pretty good for that. [1] - [LINK] [2] - [LINK] [3] - [VIDEO]"}]},"UphN1_7nP8U":{"wy":"HN uses it as visual evidence that JPEG XL decodes progressively while AVIF shows nothing until fully loaded.","q":{"x":"AVIF only shows when it is 100% finished.","i":36805765,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":39561677,"author":"149765","month":"2024-03","story":"JPEG XL and the Pareto Front","stance":"rec","why":"Compares progressive decoding of jpeg, jxl and avif","quote":"There's more on the same channel, generation loss ones are really interesting.","text":"A couple of videos comparing progressive decoding of jpeg, jxl and avif: [VIDEO] [LINK] There's more on the same channel, generation loss ones are really interesting."},{"id":48956393,"author":"cubefox","month":"2026-07","story":"Regressive JPEGs","stance":"reference","why":"Example of progressive image display on slow connections","quote":"it's expected to speed up displaying large image files","text":"Progressive decoding isn't expected to speed up decoding, it's expected to speed up displaying large image files, especially for downloads via slow mobile connections. Example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37581480,"author":"supermatt","month":"2023-09","story":"First automatic JPEG-XL cloud service","stance":"reference","why":"Shows progressive decoding at reduced file sizes","quote":"High quality progressive decoding at reduced filesizes is a big positive for me.","text":"High quality progressive decoding at reduced filesizes is a big positive for me. There is no other format that supports that. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36805765,"author":"cubefox","month":"2023-07","story":"JPEG XL: How It Started, How It’s Going","stance":"reference","why":"Shows AVIF lacks progressive decoding","quote":"AVIF only shows when it is 100% finished.","text":"Apart from limited resolution probably the biggest problem with AVIF: It doesn't support progressive decoding. Which could effectively cancel out its smaller file size for any web applications. AVIF only shows when it is 100% finished. See [VIDEO] This comparison video is admittedly a little unfair though, because AVIF would have easily 30% lower file size than JPEG XL on ordinary images with medium quality."},{"id":27581195,"author":"twotwotwo","month":"2021-06","story":"JPEG XL","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of progressive decoding in image formats","quote":"Progressive decoding. Like the original .jpg, .jxl can give you a low-quality image when a fraction of the file is loaded","text":"Some notable features of JPEG XL that don't show up in side-by-sides: 1) Progressive decoding. Like the original .jpg, .jxl can give you a low-quality image when a fraction of the file is loaded, then a decent-quality image, then the final image. This can give JPEG XL the edge in perceived load speed even when the full .avif is smaller than the full .jxl. (Old demo from a JXL contributor at [VIDEO] ) 2) Fast conversion: JPEG XL encoding/decoding is fast without dedicated hardware. Facebook found encode/decode speed and progressive decoding to be points in favor of JPEG XL for their use: [LINK] 3) .jpg repacking: JPEG XL can pack a JPEG1 about 20% smaller without any additional loss; the original .jpg file can be recovered bit-for-bit. 4) Lossless mode. JXL's lossless mode is the successor to FLIF/FUIF, is really good, and also has progressive decoding. AVIF has a lossless mode too, but JPEG XL seems ahead here. (I know the parent comment is from a JXL contributor, I'm saying this for other folks.) I think those will give JPEG XL a niche on the Web. Meanwhile I suspect e.g. Android phone cameras will save .avif someday, like iPhones save .heic now. Phones want the encode hardware anyway for video, and you can crunch a zillion megapixels down to a smaller file with AVIF before attention-grabbing artifacts crop up--at low bitrates AVIF seems good at preserving sharp lines and mostly blurring low-contrast details (compare Tiny images). Finally, worth noting the codecs are differe"}]},"ZW8gWgpptI8":{"wy":"HN people cite it for solo-dev inspiration and for the rendering technique behind the game's look.","q":{"x":"You might want to watch this for inspiration","i":25377213,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25377213,"author":"butz","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: I'm quitting my job, will create a game – any advice?","stance":"rec","why":"Postmortem on single-developer struggles and success.","quote":"You might want to watch this for inspiration","text":"You might want to watch this for inspiration: Short Hike Post-mortem ( [VIDEO] ) about single developer struggles. Of course, this is one of successful stories, so your mileage might (and will) vary. Give yourself tight deadlines and see if you can stick to them. Do not be afraid to bail out early, if you see that game development is not working out. I'd suggest keeping your daily job until you at least have minimal working prototype of your game and get positive feedback. As for games on mobile platforms, be prepared if you will create something new and unique - it will have dozen of clones on day three after launch."},{"id":33824929,"author":"NortySpock","month":"2022-12","story":"Isometric Pixel Art","stance":"reference","why":"Developer used low-poly models hidden behind a pixel shader.","quote":"GDC presentation by the developer of A Short Hike","text":"Not the same method, but as I recall the developer of the game A Short Hike did a similar procedure - mostly to allow him to quickly draft 3D models and hide the low-poly models, artifacts, and \"glitches\" behind the pixel shader. [VIDEO] GDC presentation by the developer of A Short Hike My review: a fun, calming, and enjoyable 2 hour game with a heartwarming ending."},{"id":31623475,"author":"annnoo","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Non-violent video games with great stories?","stance":"reference","why":"Developer explains how A Short Hike was made.","quote":"The developer held a talk on the GDC how he made the game","text":"Maybe not the greatest story but definitely one of the loveliest games I've played was A Short Hike. It is a kinda short (~5-6h) feel good game in which you more or less just have to explore a holiday island and meet different other Animals The developer held a talk on the GDC how he made the game: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27785045,"author":"yorak","month":"2021-07","story":"SpriteStack Voxel Editor","stance":"reference","why":"GDC talk discusses the shader used in A Short Hike.","quote":"IIRC the shader a Short Hike used was discussed in the GDC talk by the game's author","text":"Nice. The resulting graphics style reminds me of a Short Hike, which is a family friendly bite sized 3D adventure and exploration game. I'm not affiliated in any way, just enjoyed the game with my kid. It has a nice balance of story, challenges, and some platformer action. [LINK] Edit: IIRC the shader a Short Hike used was discussed in the GDC talk by the game's author: [VIDEO]"}]},"_DrsVhzbLzU":{"wy":"Cited in threads on wrongful convictions and executions as a pithy rebuttal of the deterrence argument.","q":{"x":"It's not a deterrent killing the wrong people!","i":41657910,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":20572541,"author":"arethuza","month":"2019-07","story":"'Five Eyes' nations discuss backdoor access to WhatsApp","stance":"rec","why":"Question Time clip on capital punishment","quote":"I recommend watching this clip from Question Time on the subject of capital punishment","text":"I recommend watching this clip from Question Time on the subject of capital punishment: [VIDEO] I'm still shocked that we have someone as Home Secretary who thought capital punishment was a good idea."},{"id":41657910,"author":"amiga386","month":"2024-09","story":"Who is Marcellus Williams: Execution in Missouri despite evidence of innocence","stance":"reference","why":"Hislop rebuts deterrence argument about executing wrong people","quote":"It's not a deterrent killing the wrong people!","text":"It doesn't matter how heinous the murder was if you have the wrong suspect. [VIDEO] > Priti Patel: this is about having deterrents... > Ian Hislop: It's not a deterrent killing the wrong people!"},{"id":13927750,"author":"linuxkerneldev","month":"2017-03","story":"'My life was ruined by a typo'","stance":"reference","why":"Exchange showing punishing the innocent is no deterrent","quote":"the minister seems incapable of understanding that punishing people who turned out to be innocent isn't an effective system","text":"This is a very good point. There seems to be no coverage of whether the actual perpetrator was ever caught. We employ and compensate the police, admins and fund the judicial system, at least partly in order to serve as a deterrent to criminals. If our system has a high percentage of false positives or punishes innocent people then that reduces the efficacy of the system as a deterrent. I'm reminded of this exchange between a journalist and a British justice minister where the minister seems incapable of understanding that punishing people who turned out to be innocent isn't an effective system. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26397841,"author":"alfredlind","month":"2021-03","story":"Google HR 'suggested medical leave' for racism victims","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I would strongly suggest watching this short clip of her talking about this in such a ridiculous way.","text":"> We have the most racially diverse Cabinet ever, elected by a massive majority. Priti and Rishi enjoy huge popularity. insular = ignorant of or uninterested in cultures, ideas, or peoples outside one's own experience. I have examined Priti Patel's statements and behavior. Her moral and cultural compass would be what I would exactly define as insular. For example, going on TV justifying applying the death penalty to innocent people which predominantly affects minority populations. I would strongly suggest watching this short clip of her talking about this in such a ridiculous way. She keeps repeating the phrase \"does what it says on the tin\" and \"ultimate burden of proof\" to justify killing innocent people. [VIDEO] She's also on record supporting any and all of Israeli policies without accountability. [VIDEO] She's on record bullying people. [VIDEO] She's on record making unsubstantiated claims that minorities were refusing vaccines when the data and polling suggests otherwise and some evidence even suggests that authorities are purposely using that as an excuse to deny giving vaccines to minority populations. [LINK] , [LINK] In short, Priti Patel is a pretty insular personality, ie: a person who just happens to have brown skin but in fact reflect a highly insular conservative viewpoint. \"Model\" minorities such as her are often used by power structures to justify further reinforcement of highly biased policies that perpetuate a cycle of disenfranchisement. In other words, hav"}]},"cqggW08BWO0":{"wy":"HN commenters post it whenever surveillance or social-media privacy stories seem to confirm the joke, noting how prescient it looks.","q":{"x":"I love this The Onion video","i":21287222,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21287222,"author":"agota","month":"2019-10","story":"Eat, Drink and Be Wary: Ex-CIA Officer Reveals How Eateries Are Key to Spycraft","stance":"rec","why":"Onion news report satirizing Facebook as a CIA project","quote":"I love this The Onion video","text":"I love this The Onion video that is a news report on the CIA's most successful project ever, Facebook: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36558861,"author":"berkle4455","month":"2023-07","story":"Twitter Is DDOSing Itself","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The Onion called it many years ago","text":"I agree, but there are hundreds if not thousands of AI startups trying to make their own relevant LLM, and they're going to be scraping Twitter. The Onion called it many years ago [1]: \"400 billion tweets and not one useful bit of data was ever transmitted\". [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13512598,"author":"nickpsecurity","month":"2017-01","story":"Sergey Brin joins protest against immigration order at SFO","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"even The Onion saw how helpful such companies are","text":"My impression is that a Never Collect Data Usable Against Minorities Pledge shouldn't be signed by people supporting exactly that to tune of billions of dollars and nearly as many users under surveillance. Unless they're full of shit. Hell, even The Onion saw how helpful such companies are: [VIDEO] I remember watching it knowing it was satire but most of the benefits were still true. I bet the people against discrimination and such at those locations are still coding away at the algorithms and data stores to make it happen. Easier to put your name on something than to make real sacrifices. Do email me, though, when a story pops up that Google or Facebook got rid of all saved data that could be used to hurt unpopular groups all thanks to such people in the company."},{"id":3527510,"author":"ender7","month":"2012-01","story":"FBI to monitor Facebook, Twitter, Myspace","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Once again, the Onion got there first.","text":"Once again, the Onion got there first. [VIDEO] I will start worrying when companies are legally required to let the FBI trawl through the databases without a warrant. It's hard to make a law against scraping public information."},{"id":17484935,"author":"zerostar07","month":"2018-07","story":"No Joke: The Onion Faces Layoffs by Univision, Says Report","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Plot twist: The onion becomes the CIA project that fools people on using Facebook, another CIA project: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5840769,"author":"theatraine","month":"2013-06","story":"Thousands dead, millions deprived of civil liberties? (2001)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The Onion's parody on Facebook's CIA ownership is now sounding eerily accurate.","text":"The Onion's parody on Facebook's CIA ownership is now sounding eerily accurate. For those who haven't seen it: [VIDEO]"}]},"oSCX78-8-q0":{"wy":"Cited as a striking example of atom-level manipulation and STM imaging, including the ripples viewers ask about.","q":{"x":"This IBM video[1] was made with an STM scanning tunneling microscope, which sees outer electrons.","i":23148047,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":11977700,"author":"kens","month":"2016-06","story":"What are “actual pictures” of atoms actually pictures of?","stance":"rec","why":"IBM stop-motion movie made from individual atoms.","quote":"IBM made a stop-motion animated movie from individual atoms.","text":"The linked video \"A Boy And His Atom: The World's Smallest Movie\" is entertaining. IBM made a stop-motion animated movie from individual atoms. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43476004,"author":"mncharity","month":"2025-03","story":"What Killed Innovation?","stance":"reference","why":"Stop-motion animation using CO molecules, used in usability testing.","quote":"stop-motion animation using CO molecules","text":"> So what next? LLM discussion of visualizations? I did guerrilla usability testing around teaching scale, which included this video[1] (stop-motion animation using CO molecules). Lots of people asked \"What are those ripples?\". IBM even had a supplementary webpage addressing this (which I no longer see, even on archive). People could easily ask this with me standing beside them, but not so much if viewing the content online. Which raised the UI question of how to encourage such. With LLMs, perhaps people will be able to ask questions of a visualization? What is that? Why is that? What about ...? I don't understand ... Does this mean ...? [1] IBM's A boy and his atom [VIDEO] Making of: [LINK]"},{"id":32830683,"author":"mncharity","month":"2022-09","story":"The enduring allure of Choose Your Own Adventure books","stance":"reference","why":"Stop-motion cartoon drawn with atoms prompts viewer questions about the ripples.","quote":"I've shown people IBM's stop-motion cartoon drawn with atoms","text":"Using gaze tracking with a CYOA-structured video or book permits another aspect of oral storytelling - noticing and adapting to listener interest. Attention focused on some character gets more scenes with them. Or the opposite? For personalized science education, might one wander the richly interwoven tapestry of stories that is science, following one's interests? I've shown people IBM's stop-motion cartoon drawn with atoms, [VIDEO] , and they'd often ask about the ripples. IBM wrote supplementary content on them, perhaps because of such common interest. But when watching yt alone, its hard to engage, to follow up. Links in the description? Pop-up cards? With gaze tracking, one might notice the attention, and provide opportunity in that teachable moment. As if a mentor, tutor. Gaze tracking without extra hardware is still rough. And patents. But slowly it's becoming something to explore."},{"id":27967531,"author":"kragen","month":"2021-07","story":"Making The World's Most Detailed Print Maps","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"A notable inconsistency, or exaggeration: > we print our maps using the highest resolution mediums available. ... There's no sense printing at a higher resolution than humans are able to perceive. For print, 300 dpi is the gold standard . Higher resolution media such as [LINK] are around 20000 dpi. Current semiconductor processes [LINK] have feature sizes around 20 nm, which is about 1.3 million dpi. This is significantly denser than 300 dpi. It's easy to see the difference between a page printed at 300 dpi and one printed at 600 dpi, so I'd say even 300 dpi doesn't reach \"higher resolution than humans are able to perceive\". How long have more detailed mediums (or media) been available? In 01949 George Harrison reported improving the control loop of a ruling engine to a precision of, in the medieval units then in use, 0.2 micro-inches (5 nm): [LINK] so that he could cut grooves for a diffraction grating to that precision, which evidently amounts to a precision of 5 million dpi. This seems to have been about a factor of 70 improvement over what Michelson had achieved before 01900. But serious difficulties attended any attempts to use such diffraction ruling engines to cut irregular patterns such as these maps—as well, of course, as limits in the data bandwidth of the necessary control systems. More detailed media still have been demonstrated; in 01989 hackers at IBM demonstrated the ability to use an STM to position atoms with atomic precision (≈0.1 nm) [LINK] and in 02013 oth"},{"id":23148047,"author":"mncharity","month":"2020-05","story":"Visualizing Electricity","stance":"reference","why":"Made with an STM, which sees outer electrons.","quote":"This IBM video[1] was made with an STM scanning tunneling microscope, which sees outer electrons.","text":"Apropos visualizing atoms and their electrons, here's a fun sidebar. This IBM video[1] was made with an STM scanning tunneling microscope, which sees outer electrons. So while it shows the IIRC carbon monoxide molecules, standing up like buoys, as little balls, the background copper surface of delocalized electrons looks smooth, with ripples. You can't see the individual copper atoms. But if you instead used an AFM atomic force microscope, which can feel inner electrons, then you could - here's one of silicon[2]. When crafting and teaching abstract representations, it's easy to forget that these are real physical objects. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":23259346,"author":"mncharity","month":"2020-05","story":"X","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> can be a huge barrier to entry for the student I did a category theory class a few months back. Huge barrier at times - so many moving pieces. \"Err, which properties do 𝒞 and mumble(𝒟) have here , in this paragraph???\" I wanted to create an interactive version of some topics. Just mouseover elaboration and graphics, to reduce the amount of state one has to keep track of mentally, and its associated cognitive load. But the state of in-browser rendering of category diagrams wasn't quite there yet. Best part of the class was the \"after-math\" (that's a 3-interpretation pun). An iterative improvement on past versions of the class, formalizing an observed custom - after the 1 hr math lecture, the room was reserved for a second hour, so people could hang out, with the instructors, in front of blackboards. In part, clarifying and explaining things in different ways. One instructor observed something very vaguely like \"I'd never approach things the way I do in lecture, if I was tutoring someone one-on-one.\" One can imagine technical fixes. Mouse-over equations. FAQs. Presenting the material from multiple perspectives. Many of them could implemented on clay tablets. And that newfangled wood pulp. My best understanding is, that as a society, incentives are poorly aligned with creating non-wretched education content in the sciences, and I assume in math. So my hope for XR in education, is not just the power of tools like eye tracking, but that incentives are disrupted enough to get us "},{"id":22498342,"author":"mncharity","month":"2020-03","story":"Watching Magnets Rust","stance":"reference","why":"STM imaging of CO molecules on copper shows surface electron ripples.","quote":"STM imaging of cold CO molecules sitting on a copper surface","text":"> but without chemical bonds? Metallic bonds. My understanding is all bonding is atoms sticking together electrostatically, with (insanely fast) \"slow\" lumbering nuclei, nudged around by their local e-field, and wacky electrons, adopting weird spatial distributions based on the current nuclei positions, setting that field. The bond taxonomy descriptively sorts by characteristics. Here, one is delocalization of valence electrons. Perhaps picture [VIDEO] , STM imaging of cold CO molecules sitting on a copper surface, IIRC. You can't see the individual copper atoms, and there are extended ripples in surface electron density."},{"id":22651233,"author":"mncharity","month":"2020-03","story":"Ask HN: What projects are you working on now?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"IEEE VR starts tomorrow (Sunday-Thursday). Entirely online. Registration is waitlisted, until they see how their infrastructure holds up. But they also plan live streaming video of all presentation sessions (papers, panels, workshops, and keynotes) on Twitch, open to anyone. streams: [LINK] schedule: [LINK] hashtag: [LINK] Also working on a video-rich[1] tweet thread \"Atoms are {little, balls, sticky, jiggly, bouncy, stacking, etc}\", as motivation while working towards an exemplar of transformatively improved science education content, which I hope will speed conversations about that. Anyone have any favorite media of atoms? Also on desktop panning using head tracking. Also on using RealSense t265 tracking cameras with Google Mediapipe's TF hand tracking.[2] Also on a next rev of DIY 3D shutter glasses. Also on... sigh. [1] example media: balls [VIDEO] , sticky [LINK] , ball in the ball (nuclei) [LINK] [2] low-performance browser demo: [LINK]"},{"id":20523194,"author":"self_awareness","month":"2019-07","story":"Quantum Darwinism, an Idea to Explain Objective Reality, Passes First Tests","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"From the first paragraph of the article: > An atom typically can’t be assigned a definite position, for example — we can merely calculate the probability of finding it in various places. Is this really the case? Or maybe author meant 'electron' instead of 'atom'? I mean, if we wouldn't be able of calculating the position of an Atom, how could \"A Boy And His Atom\" would be possible? [VIDEO]"},{"id":19829052,"author":"mncharity","month":"2019-05","story":"How to Calm a Crying Baby Like a Mesopotamian","stance":"reference","why":"Stop-motion animation with molecules, viewers ask about the ripples.","quote":"stop-motion animation with individual molecules","text":"> It's mind blowing :) When people watch this[1], and I'm standing next to them, they'll often ask about the ripples. But if they were watching it alone, there isn't an easy way to do that. Someone did an art project, where you sit and watch an interesting drama video. But why was it interesting? There's an eye tracker, and the video is a graph of scenes, like an old \"choose your own adventure\" story. So if you're interested in character A... the video explores/emphasizes character A. AR/VR is coming. With eye tracking (eventually... patents). So... Imagine science education that's consistently mind blowing, rather than boring. What might that be like? Though a cautionary note: there's apparently a problem of students, who have consistently had excellent teachers, clearly and motivationally presenting material, reaching college without the skills or inclination to themselves wrestle with a body of knowledge and distill understanding. Another opportunity for improvement. :) [1] stop-motion animation with individual molecules: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15530298,"author":"mncharity","month":"2017-10","story":"The Xerox Alto, Smalltalk, and Rewriting a Running GUI","stance":"reference","why":"Example educational video where viewers ask about the ripples.","quote":"say IBM's atom animation","text":"> I'm not as pessimistic as Bret Victor [...] ABriefRant I'd actually go one step further than that \"rant\". In the real world, small physical motions usually have little control significance. Mouse movement and keyboards are among the many exceptions. With XR's hand tracking, that can change. For example, while gaming is focused on \"feeling like it's real\", so game hands overlap real hands, for getting work done, it's nice to have stretchy arms, so you can reach across the room to grab things. For optimal long-term ergonomics, you want a mix - sometimes you want the exercise of waving your arms, and sometimes you want to achieve the same end by merely twitching a hand resting on a knee or keyboard. So not only did the video reduce all of human motion vocabulary, including gaze and voice, down to a single finger over a sad UI, but even that single finger was used in an impoverished manner. ;) > XR will work much better with some pointing and voice input Eye tracking as well. \"Look; point; click\" has a seemingly redundant step. When someone is watching an education video, say IBM's atom animation[1], you want to both be able to field common questions like \"What are the ripples?\", but also notice that they are looking at the ripples, and volunteer related content. One can do prototyping now, with WebVR, google speech recognition, and in-browser or google voice synthesis. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6782156,"author":"moocowduckquack","month":"2013-11","story":"Will 2-D tin be the next super material?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I expect it to be about as difficult as this - [LINK] - but not quite as difficult as this - [VIDEO] - or this - [LINK]"}],"r":[["eOti3kKWX-c",1],["z8VhNF_0I5c",1],["ND2TeNfcmKA",1]]},"xQ3oJlt4GrI":{"wy":"Cited in threads on chip packaging and cooling as a vivid look at vintage IBM mainframe module construction.","q":{"x":"Dave from EEVBlog also made a video about it","i":25974704,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":25974704,"author":"nom","month":"2021-01","story":"IBM paperweight teardown: Reverse-engineering 1970s memory chips","stance":"rec","why":"EEVblog teardown of a vintage IBM mainframe processor module.","quote":"Dave from EEVBlog also made a video about it","text":"All the vintage mainframe stuff is pretty mind blowing. There is an awesome hidden gem on YouTube: CPU Galaxy. He has a huge collection of vintage CPUs. IBM 9121 Mainframe CPU [LINK] Dave from EEVBlog also made a video about it [VIDEO]"},{"id":33919940,"author":"ilyt","month":"2022-12","story":"One Startup’s Cooling Chip May Surge Processor Power","stance":"reference","why":"Shows IBM module with spring-loaded pistons cooling each chiplet.","quote":"It have a bunch of pistons on springs touching each chiplet, all in mineral oil.","text":"This one does[1]. It have a bunch of pistons on springs touching each chiplet, all in mineral oil. Price bit out of range for typical desktop user tho * [VIDEO]"},{"id":28255680,"author":"formerly_proven","month":"2021-08","story":"An inside look at the custom CPUs in Tesla's Dojo Supercomputer","stance":"reference","why":"Shows 1980s IBM mainframe thermal and packaging similar to Dojo.","quote":"","text":"The thermal solution and packaging looks a lot like mainframe systems from the 80s (IBM, Siemens, Japanese). Siemens H100: [LINK] [LINK] IBM: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["WFCvkkDSfIU",1],["iRkZcTg1JWU",1]]},"1miGzTwK28U":{"wy":"HN commenters mostly cite it critically, as the kind of source behind cannabis-cures-cancer claims that overstate early lab results.","m":[{"id":21873534,"author":"drummer","month":"2019-12","story":"Social media bots often tweet fake health claims about cannabis: study","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The main component of RSO actually has been studied and proven to kill cancer.","text":"The main component of RSO actually has been studied and proven to kill cancer. You need RSO with very high THC concentration. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21111024,"author":"kd3","month":"2019-09","story":"Life as a cancer patient: ‘it feels like dying from the drugs meant to save me’","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"You can find tons of info online about THC killing cancer.","text":"This is not misinfo. Chemo is misinfo. You can find tons of info online about THC killing cancer. Here is a video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18405858,"author":"kareldonk369","month":"2018-11","story":"A cure for cancer: how to kill a killer","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The cure for cancer already exists.","text":"The cure for cancer already exists. We've had it for ages. It's THC found in the cannabis plants. See research from, among others, Compultense University in Spain.[1] Many people suffering from various forms of cancer have been cured by using cannabis oil made from cannabis with high THC concentrations. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"6epzmRZk6UU":{"wy":"HN mentions it mostly as a reference point in Max Headroom threads, as the essential Art of Noise tie-in to that 80s character.","q":{"x":"you can't talk about Max Headroom without talking about The Art of Noise","i":29321282,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":35837465,"author":"wrp","month":"2023-05","story":"The Lofi Magic of VHS Audio","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Nostalgia tripping on the 1980s seems to rarely if ever include Max Headroom.","text":"I'm surprised at how often I see adults rewatching Flight of the Navigator , even though it's very much a kid's movie. Harry and the Hendersons is particularly fun to watch now if you are a Poirot fan. Nostalgia tripping on the 1980s seems to rarely if ever include Max Headroom. To me, he was the essence of my high school zeitgeist. Maybe younger people today think he was too weird to have actually been a big deal. ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":29321282,"author":"cronix","month":"2021-11","story":"The Max Headroom Incident","stance":"reference","why":"Art of Noise with Max Headroom","quote":"you can't talk about Max Headroom without talking about The Art of Noise","text":"Hey, you can't talk about Max Headroom without talking about The Art of Noise. The Art of Noise (with Max Headroom) - Paranomia (1986): [VIDEO]"},{"id":29327323,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2021-11","story":"The Max Headroom Incident","stance":"reference","why":"Part of a list of Max Headroom media","quote":"","text":"The Art of Noise with Max Headroom - Paranoimia (Official Video) [VIDEO] Coca-Cola Max Headroom | Max Headroom Coke commercials [LINK] Max Headroom on Sesame Street [LINK] The Headroom Collection on Letterman, 1986-1990 [LINK] Terry Wogan interviews Max Headroom [LINK] On Max Headroom: The Most Misunderstood Joke on TV [LINK] Back To The Future - Cafe 80`s [LINK] Altered Carbon Carnage: Who is Carnage? Who is Matt Frewer who plays Carnage? [LINK] Star Trek the Next Generation: A Matter Of Time [LINK] Matt Frewer Interview: Doctor Doctor, Max Headroom, Altered Carbon: Carnage, Orphan Black, Eureka [LINK]"},{"id":21982910,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-01","story":"Peak map: Visualize the elevation of any area using a ridgeline chart","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Very well done! I like how it can exaggerate the height arbitrarily enough to make it really stand out. It would be cool to make the lines rotate and translate and scan around over the map like Max Headroom's background! [VIDEO] I wonder how you could go about implementing it as a high performance shader?"}]},"9sJUDx7iEJw":{"wy":"HN posts it as a lighthearted joke link in free software and licensing threads.","m":[{"id":32540718,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2022-08","story":"There were almost two internets. Then, the CIA destroyed one","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The most horrifying thing about the \"Computer-generated image of Project CyberSyn operations room\" on the Project Cybersyn Wikipedia page is that they're displaying the lyrics of RMS's Free Software Song (which he wrote in 1991) on the fake monitors in the \"Close Up of the Data Feed\": [LINK] SHARE THE SOFTWARE ## YOU'LL BE FREE JOIN US NOW AND SHARE THE SOFTWARE ## HACKER YOU'LL BE FREE HACKER YOU'LL BE FREE JOIN US NOW ## JOIN US NOW } } #### } #### YOU'LL BE FREE } JOIN US NOW AND SHARE THE SOFTWARE #### HACKER } #### } } ## YOU'LL BE FREE JOIN US NOW ## HACKER ## YOU'LL BE FREE ## HACKER ## JOIN US NOW ## YOU'LL BE FREE ## HACKER [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] The CyberSyn multimedia \"reconstruction\": [LINK]"},{"id":19945242,"author":"O_H_E","month":"2019-05","story":"A song about the GNU public license","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"He is drumming on the laptop.","text":"Oh boy. This is an awesome gem in the open source world. Did you know about RMS's song: [VIDEO] He is drumming on the laptop. [laugh cry]"},{"id":17233002,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2018-06","story":"Why Open Source Misses the Point of Free Software (2016)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"There aren't so many Epic Rock & Roll Anthems about","text":"Yeah, but as a brand, it sucks. There aren't so many Epic Rock & Roll Anthems about \"Collaborative Software\". But there is one about \"Free Software\", though: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15985003,"author":"sabalaba","month":"2017-12","story":"Nvidia’s New Policy Limits GeForce Data Center Usage","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Join us now and share the software!","text":"This should be a rallying cry to the developer community to stop using software with restrictive licenses. [VIDEO] “Join us now and share the software!” In all seriousness anybody using CUDA should immediately start checking out and contributing to ROCm, AMD’s Open Source Compute Platform: [LINK]"},{"id":11592213,"author":"supernintendo","month":"2016-04","story":"ACM 2015 Technical Award Recipients","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"And now for a song,","text":"Love him or hate him, Stallman's contributions have been instrumental in shaping the landscape of technology as we know it today. I have nothing but respect for him and the Free Software Foundation. And now for a song, [VIDEO]"},{"id":8514350,"author":"virgoerns","month":"2014-10","story":"An open source engine clone of Age of Empires II","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Oh, and a mandatory link when we're talking about Free Software","text":"It's all about software licenses. 1) free as in \"gratis\" (freeware software) - you can download it for free, install it and use it. And that's all. Usually it's already compiled so you don't have an access to the source code. Sometimes you are forbidden to copy it and give it to a friend (because you both should e.g. download it from author's web page). 2) free as in \"freedom\" (free software) - you can do literally anything with this kind of software because it's licensed in a way that doesn't restrict the user in any manner (i.e. license preserves user freedom). Run it, fork it, modify it, sell it, give it away to your friends (or to anyone), you can do anything. There are several free licenses which handle these cases in a different way (e.g. GPL, LGPL, Apache License, MIT License, Boost License, Vim License and so on). They're sometimes called copyleft licenses (as opposite to copyright term). If you're interested, here's the wiki page comparing most of free licenses: [LINK] Please note that it doesn't mean that the software is being given away \"for free (gratis)\" (but usually it is) - it only means that once you obtain it, you can do anything you want with it. Oh, and a mandatory link when we're talking about Free Software: [VIDEO]"}]},"AeeeEDSekG8":{"wy":"Cited as Musk's own account of Tesla's founding and early financial struggles, often in disputes over the company's history.","q":{"x":"in this interview he talks about the fact he had “no money” and had to borrow money for rent around that time","i":32080396,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34047028,"author":"jjoonathan","month":"2022-12","story":"Twitter Suspends PG's Account","stance":"reference","why":"Musk's side of the Tesla founding dispute","quote":"the Musk counter-interview","text":"> Tesla existed before Musk. So I've watched the Eberhard interview [1] and the Musk counter-interview [2] and I'm still not decided on this. Did Eberhard do a bunch of useful early-day heavy-lifting only to get elbowed out by Musk once things were ticking along and it was time to take credit? Or was he incompetent and un-invested, forcing Musk to reluctantly take back the reins? I'd love to hear an insider opinion, but I'm not going to go digging. I've already spent far too much time today litigating other peoples' bullshit founder drama, lol. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33470574,"author":"yoshieee","month":"2022-11","story":"Twitter’s mass layoffs have begun","stance":"reference","why":"Musk's own account of Tesla's early days","quote":"Lengthy source","text":"Also you weren't wrong about Tesla. Elon did basically create Tesla (as well as Straubel). Quick summary: Originally \"Tesla\" the brand, was started by Martin and Marc, but all they had was a brand and a vision but no physical product nor prototype. At around the same time, Elon wanted to build an electric car based off the tzero by AC Propulsion. He was introduced to Martin and Marc who had a similar vision, and basically Elon invested money into Tesla Motors. It wasn't until Elon put in money, that the first prototype was built (the tesla roadster). Arguably, if Elon didn't join the team and bring on Straubel, Tesla wouldn't be what it is today. Likely wouldn't even have a launched consumer product. Even Elon was the one that managed to get the trademark \"Tesla Motors\" which wasn't even owned by Martin or Marc originally. Lengthy source: [VIDEO] Granted it's out of Elon's mouth but there's tons of other sources that corroborates with that story. source: [LINK] source: [LINK] source: [LINK] source: [LINK]"},{"id":32080396,"author":"ghufran_syed","month":"2022-07","story":"Twitter Sues Elon Musk","stance":"reference","why":"Musk describes having no money and borrowing for rent in 2008","quote":"in this interview he talks about the fact he had “no money” and had to borrow money for rent around that time","text":"I think his original plan was to have around $40 million left over even if tesla and spacex failed, but when they both ran into difficulties in 2008-9 IIRC, I think he put the rest of his money into both companies to keep them going [VIDEO] [EDIT: in this interview he talks about the fact he had “no money” and had to borrow money for rent around that time]"}]},"CDeG4S-mJts":{"wy":"Cited as a joke about git's notoriously esoteric interface, usually in threads about git or other Hitler-parody videos.","m":[{"id":31754438,"author":"CrazyPyroLinux","month":"2022-06","story":"A Git Origin Story (2018)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"But I heard Linus \"wrote git as a joke [on] a weekend coding binge, jacked up on blow in Vegas.","text":"But I heard Linus \"wrote git as a joke [on] a weekend coding binge, jacked up on blow in Vegas.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":12197102,"author":"krisdol","month":"2016-07","story":"Hitler Uses Docker, Annotated","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"My personal favorite is Hitler uses Git","text":"It's a long-running meme to use this movie clip and subtitle it in different ways. Some do a better job than others. My personal favorite is Hitler uses Git[1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5631963,"author":"sergiotapia","month":"2013-04","story":"Git pull --rebase until it hurts","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Git is fast, but it's a clusterfuck of weird command calls and esoteric flags.","text":"There's another post on the frontpage saying to NOT use rebase. This video comes to mind: [VIDEO] Git is fast, but it's a clusterfuck of weird command calls and esoteric flags. I kind of miss Mercurial in this regard, but I had to make the switch due to the popularity of Github. Having open source projects is a very nice way to show potential employers that you are a good asset."}]},"FiCqlYKRlAA":{"wy":"Cited as an example of electrostatic haptic display technology that could offer something better than vibration.","m":[{"id":14055841,"author":"Pica_soO","month":"2017-04","story":"Apple's Touch Bar Could Be a Flop","stance":"reference","why":"Example of haptic display technology","quote":"Haptic Display by Bosch","text":"They should have gone all the way- and revolutionize the whole laptop-keyboard away, with an additional touch screen, with synthetic haptic keys on demand and a background illuminated e-ink display. Add a GUI designed just for this to the whole Setup, and let it be executed by Perfectionists like apples- and they would rule the HW-Valley till kingdom come. Haptic Display by Bosch: [VIDEO] 10C-Gui: [LINK] If anyone could get this done - its apple."},{"id":12949281,"author":"spyder","month":"2016-11","story":"How can I avoid my MacBook Pro giving me minor shocks?","stance":"reference","why":"Video accompanying TeslaTouch electrovibration paper","quote":"This effect can be used for tactile feedback on touchscreens","text":"This effect can be used for tactile feedback on touchscreens: TeslaTouch: Electrovibration for Touch Surfaces: [LINK] Video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3726776,"author":"warfangle","month":"2012-03","story":"Coding Horror - Welcome to the Post PC Era","stance":"reference","why":"Example of electrostatic haptic feedback tech","quote":"electrostatic haptic feedback for something better than a goddamn vibration","text":"And yet, there are so many more things that can still be improved\" Quality daytime viewing, via LiquiVista (or similar)[1]; electrostatic haptic feedback for something better than a goddamn vibration [2]. 1. [LINK] (LiquiVista) 2. [VIDEO] (Senseg)"}]},"HHjgK6p4nrw":{"wy":"HN uses it as a reference for startup advice such as not befriending your VC, linear thinking in valuations, and protecting ideas.","q":{"x":"This lecture by Kawasaki addresses the topic","i":35746502,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35746502,"author":"Silverback_VII","month":"2023-04","story":"Ask HN: How to Protect Ideas and Implementation?","stance":"reference","why":"Kawasaki lecture addressing idea protection","quote":"This lecture by Kawasaki addresses the topic","text":"This lecture by Kawasaki addresses the topic: [VIDEO] If a large corporation steals your idea, you can try to sue if you have taken measures to protect your idea, and in rare cases, even succeed. However, it won't really get you any further."},{"id":6773097,"author":"vinchuco","month":"2013-11","story":"Bitcoin Target Price: $23,809.52","stance":"reference","why":"Kawasaki on linear thinking in valuations","quote":"about linear thinking in valuations.","text":"It reminded me of one of the mistakes Guy Kawasaki said here [VIDEO] about linear thinking in valuations. Not that I disagree with the post. I could only verify those figures in the future."},{"id":5587340,"author":"dottrap","month":"2013-04","story":"VC Firms Don't Back You, VC Partners Do","stance":"reference","why":"Kawasaki's top mistakes including befriending your VC","quote":"Item 10: Befriending your VC.","text":"Careful. This borders on Guy Kawasaki's Top 10 Mistakes by Entrepreneurs, Item 10: Befriending your VC. [VIDEO]"}]},"Hg9F1Qjv3iU":{"wy":"Cited for Federighi's statement that Apple silicon Macs will run other operating systems via virtualization rather than direct booting.","q":{"x":"we're not direct booting an alternate operating system, it's purely virtualization","i":25051105,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":29598995,"author":"sigjuice","month":"2021-12","story":"Apple Helps Asahi Linux","stance":"reference","why":"Federighi interview on virtualization over direct boot","quote":"Craig Federighi says “We’re not direct booting an alternate operating system.","text":"Is there another interview that goes into the details of Apple's boot setup goals? [VIDEO] In this one, Craig Federighi says “We’re not direct booting an alternate operating system. Purely virtualization is the route. These hypervisors can be very efficient, so the need to direct boot shouldn’t really be the concern.”"},{"id":26210666,"author":"rudedogg","month":"2021-02","story":"OpenBSD boots multi-user on Apple M1 hardware","stance":"reference","why":"Federighi clear Apple only supports macOS on its hardware","quote":"Apple was very clear that they are only supporting macOS on their hardware.","text":"They're quoting Craig Federighi: [VIDEO] I don't get the downvotes of the parent post, the interpretation is completely reasonable. The point is that Apple was very clear that they are only supporting macOS on their hardware."},{"id":25050758,"author":"Asmod4n","month":"2020-11","story":"Introducing the next generation of Mac","stance":"reference","why":"Federighi on booting alternate operating systems on Apple silicon","quote":"here a podcast with Craig Federighi on that matter","text":"During WWDC they said you can't use any other operating System at boot time, only through virtual Machines. Update: here a podcast with Craig Federighi on that matter [VIDEO]"},{"id":25051105,"author":"shaicoleman","month":"2020-11","story":"Introducing the next generation of Mac","stance":"reference","why":"Federighi says only virtualization, no direct booting","quote":"we're not direct booting an alternate operating system, it's purely virtualization","text":"Link with timestamp: [VIDEO] \"we're not direct booting an alternate operating system, it's purely virtualization\""},{"id":25061478,"author":"vbezhenar","month":"2020-11","story":"Introducing the next generation of Mac","stance":"reference","why":"WWDC talk claiming alternate OS cannot be booted","quote":"","text":"Here's WWDC talk claiming that you can't: [VIDEO] I'm talking about ARM macs, not Intel ones."},{"id":25076667,"author":"cercatrova","month":"2020-11","story":"Docker fails to launch on Apple Silicon","stance":"reference","why":"Federighi says other OSes via virtualization, not bare metal","quote":"Craig Federighi said that it will be virtualization rather than booting other OS on the metal","text":"Presumably they are locked down. When asked about alternate OS, Craig Federighi said that it will be virtualization rather than booting other OS on the metal, ie Linux (and possibly Windows) VMs rather than Bootcamp. That's one of the big things stopping me from switching from the Intel version. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25077923,"author":"areoform","month":"2020-11","story":"macOS unable to open any non-Apple application","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Apple's Craig Federighi assures us that the people making such assertions are \"tools","text":"So yesterday I wrote about the blurring lines of ownership, and people came back with some fairly disparate responses. It's fair to say that I was mostly dismissed. [LINK] And this is why I won't be moving to Apple silicon. Apple already has the ability to restrict whats apps I can run (they can simply toggle a switch for all users to \"no unsigned binaries\"), and congrats! Apple is the sole decider of what we get to use on our computers. Of course Apple's Craig Federighi assures us that the people making such assertions are \"tools\" ( [VIDEO] , timestamp 53:33) and they have no intention whatsoever of taking away our ability to do general compute on the machines we buy and own. Except... Apple can already decide what binaries you can execute. Should they choose to. Apple is now restricting what other OSes you can boot into. As they've chosen to. Apple can now make their machine reject a new, third-party repair part like a bad transplant. Should they choose to. It's clear where they're going. And I'm jumping ship. It's painful to do so, given how invested I am in the ecosystem, but we're already beyond the threshold that many of us would have left earlier in the decade. --- edit - It's also really hard as a designer + developer + would-be researcher in the making to find a good computer. Most non-Apple laptops don't have very good color accuracy. They also don't have good trackpads, and their keyboard + trackpad alignment is wonky (it's off-center in a lot of cases! How weird i"}]},"IzQ3gFRj0Bc":{"wy":"HN cites it in nuclear power debates for the point that recycling waste is largely a solved problem, though costly to build.","q":{"x":"recycling nuclear waste is kind of a solved problem","i":45231756,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45231756,"author":"dvtkrlbs","month":"2025-09","story":"EU court rules nuclear energy is clean energy","stance":"reference","why":"Video says recycling waste is solved but costlier to build.","quote":"recycling nuclear waste is kind of a solved problem","text":"The annoying thing is recycling nuclear waste is kind of a solved problem. I've watched this video a while ago but iirc it is just more expensive to build a reactor that can also recycle its own waste. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36750896,"author":"ttfkam","month":"2023-07","story":"Nuclear Fission Fuel Is Inexhaustible (2022)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in an argument for nuclear power.","quote":"","text":"How well do solar and wind infrastructures handle tornados and hurricanes? Also, consistent wind resources are notably absent in the South/Southeast. There are no Hoover Dam-like sites available there. Do you honestly expect to tell tens of millions of people that they should just be content with a single hurricane knocking out their electricity grid for a month? That's not realistic. You cannot plan an electrical future on the hope that the weather is always pleasant and consistent. This is one of the reasons why fossil fuel dominance continues in those regions. Go ahead and draw a map of those places where hurricanes and tornados are most prevalent and their large scale adoption of solar and wind. They're not stupid. They're not just political. There are real concerns you are handwaving away because you (likely) like in a region that does not have these same concerns. What works for California will not work for Georgia, Florida, or Nebraska as-is. Baseball-size hail destroys huge solar arrays let alone softball-size [0]. Nuclear plants laugh at hail and would handle and have(!) weathered tornados and hurricanes just fine. Check the numbers. Even accounting for nuclear disasters outside the US like Chernobyl and Fukushima, the number of deaths attributed to nuclear power per unit of electricity is lower than any fossil fuel, lower than wind, and about equal to solar. Just imagine if we put the same R&D force behind nuclear that we've done for solar. You think solar's cheap? "},{"id":36369112,"author":"alexcombessie","month":"2023-06","story":"Finland's plan to bury spent nuclear fuel for 100k years","stance":"reference","why":"Expert discussion on whether recycling nuclear fuel will become the main approach.","quote":"I saw an interesting interview of experts about this last month","text":"It’s a great milestone to establish a complete & safe lifecycle for nuclear fuel. From what I understood of current technology, recycling used nuclear fuel is too expensive and impractical today, so it makes economic sense to invest in long-term waste storage. But I do wonder if in 30-50 years, the main way to deal with nuclear waste would be recycling. I saw an interesting interview of experts about this last month: [VIDEO]"}]},"N9qYF9DZPdw":{"wy":"Mostly invoked as a joke when nerd stereotypes come up, and once for a trivia card visible in the video.","m":[{"id":48538357,"author":"Imustaskforhelp","month":"2026-06","story":"What the Fuck Happened to Nerds","stance":"reference","why":"Song and comments showing appreciation of nerd culture","quote":"I wish to recommend Weird Al video song about White & Nerdy[0]","text":"I think that the title should be more, what the fuck happened to tech executives as compared to nerds. because previously it was mostly the nerds who were at the forefront of the innovation (they still are), but they now have a playbook where they see all the other people (grifters) who are entering tech for money and the playbook of the attention economy and doing that because its a profitable strategy. It's basically the fact that there are multiple companies where a grift culture is promoted within tech (ironically I am on YC website and YC had a company which you might've heard called delve :D) As people realized that the technology has value and finance people realizing it to pour head over fist money into it. With such eggregious trillions of dollars worth of money (basically the whole economy getting floated by tech), you are bound to see people within this do the grift playbook and talk about themselves and succeed and that has become the playbook. So I think this is what has happened to nerd culture. It simply became profitable and then commoditized and used by people who could then grift. BUT people are respecting the nerd culture (well the non grift version of it) a lot more For some reason, I wish to recommend Weird Al video song about White & Nerdy[0] and how people within the comments are saying that Nerd culture has its own unique identity and many if not all appreciating the nerd culture [0] [VIDEO] So TLDR: people like the previous nerd culture and it still e"},{"id":20250999,"author":"peter_d_sherman","month":"2019-06","story":"43-Man Squamish","stance":"reference","why":"Video briefly shows a trivia card referencing 43-Man Squamish","quote":"at approximately 1:10 of Wierd Al Yankovic's \"White And Nerdy\" video","text":"Fun fact: This page is about 43-Man Squamish, referred to by the 6th question on the faux Trivial Pursuit game card shown for a brief instant, at approximately 1:10 of Wierd Al Yankovic's \"White And Nerdy\" video: Video: [VIDEO] Trivial Pursuit Game Card: [LINK]"},{"id":23622721,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2020-06","story":"Segway ends production of its original scooter","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Segway made it into Weird Al's \"White and Nerdy\" parody song","text":"> \" if anything can ever be qualified as \"dorky\", this is \" Segway made it into Weird Al's \"White and Nerdy\" parody song [VIDEO]"},{"id":5551803,"author":"davidroberts","month":"2013-04","story":"Steve Jobs and Jeff Bezos meet \"Ginger\" (2003)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Al Yankovic's song \"White and Nerdy\" captures the image perfectly","text":"You forgot the fact that riding one makes you look like a total geek, and not in a good way. Al Yankovic's song \"White and Nerdy\" captures the image perfectly: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2133752,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2011-01","story":"Dungeons & Dragons ruled a threat to prison security","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This makes me think of Weird Al's \"White and Nerdy","text":"This makes me think of Weird Al's \"White and Nerdy\" [VIDEO] I attended a military academy, which mildly resembled prison in a few respects. We weren't allowed out of the dorms after 7pm, and an instructor would make sure we were all in bed (checking off our names on a checklist) and turn the light out in our rooms for us. Then he'd stay there and make sure we didn't leave our rooms until just before Reveille. The jocks and bad atitude cases would sometimes dress up as commandos in all-black outfits and go on \"missions\" to sneak off have trysts with girlfriends or pull pranks (like shining the breasts of one female statue on campus.) They'd often get caught, which was bad because it was technically a dismissal offense. Me, I was part of a group that snuck around after lights out to gather and play D&D. Actually, we didn't sneak. We just put on dress uniform, and the security guards assumed we were Officer of the Day. No, I did not have a girlfriend in High School."}]},"Q0_AqpdwqK4":{"wy":"HN cites it mainly critically, for a clip in which Biden talks about getting a Ukrainian prosecutor fired.","m":[{"id":21155683,"author":"anon9001","month":"2019-10","story":"Facebook Now Allows Politicians to Lie in Paid Ads","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I know HN isn't really the place for this, but since you asked and I did a fairly deep dive into this just to know what's going on, here's what seems to have happened, complete with evidence to the contrary: From Wikipedia [LINK] : > Shokin claimed in May 2019 that he had been investigating Burisma Holdings.[28][29][30][31] However, Vitaliy Kasko, who had been Shokin's deputy overseeing international cooperation before resigning in February 2016 citing corruption in the office, provided documents to Bloomberg News indicating that under Shokin, the investigation into Burisma had been dormant.[32] Also, the investigation into Burisma only pertained to events happening before Hunter Biden joined the company.[33] I believe that's true, and the cited sources seem to check out, and predate this whole scandal. It's worth noting that a lot of these Ukrainian politicians are pretty sketchy and they have a big problem with corruption, so there are other geopolitical interests at play with these Ukrainian political figures. To be sure I'm not being duped by Wikipedia, I find Google News searches for articles about the key players in any controversy that were published before the MSM started caring give a way clearer picture of what's going on. It can help you figure out who's spinning what. It's helpful to know that Shokin was widely condemned for being a corrupt prosecutor that, ironically, wasn't pursuing corruption cases against political allies. From March 2016: [LINK] It's pretty c"},{"id":24969260,"author":"noworriesnate","month":"2020-11","story":"'You are no longer my mother': How the election is dividing American families","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here's a video of Joe Biden bragging about threatening to withhold aid","text":"Here's a video of Joe Biden bragging about threatening to withhold aid to Ukraine in order to get the prosecutor who was investigating Hunter fired. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21154573,"author":"tomjakubowski","month":"2019-10","story":"Why do companies with unbounded resources still have terrible moderation?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Biden openly bragged about getting Shokin fired","text":"Biden openly bragged about getting Shokin fired, though not under oath, and he stopped short of saying it was to protect his son. [VIDEO] edit: phone brain skipped over the \"on his behalf\" part of the sentence, I thought that they'd said there was no evidence Biden even pressured the government to fire Shokin."},{"id":21108933,"author":"povertyworld","month":"2019-09","story":"The Whistle-Blower Knows How to Write","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"52 minutes in","text":"So, shouldn't you be in favor of the investigation into Biden? Since that is exactly what he did. \"This plane's leaving in about 6 hours. If the prosecutor [investigating his son's firm] isn't fired, you're not getting the money. Well, son of a bitch, got fired!\" 52 minutes in: [VIDEO]"}]},"RFO6ZhUW38w":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a quick, witty reference whenever someone warns against being so open-minded that you become gullible.","q":{"x":"you need to have an open mind, but be careful to not your brain fall off","i":37266530,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37788262,"author":"eigenket","month":"2023-10","story":"Great Pyramid of Cholula","stance":"reference","why":"Tim Minchin song about open minds.","quote":"another smart person wrote a song with that name.","text":"All those things are indeed possible, and keeping an open mind is generally positive. That said in the specific case of the formations in Bosnia, reputable geologists have investigated them, and have said that they are well-understood natural formations. The flatirons in Bosnia are no more human structures than the ones in Colorado or Texas. A smart person once said: if you open your mind too much your brain will fall out, and another smart person wrote a song with that name. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37266530,"author":"BrandoElFollito","month":"2023-08","story":"How to Drill Your Own Water Well","stance":"reference","why":"Tim Minchin on keeping an open mind without gullibility.","quote":"you need to have an open mind, but be careful to not your brain fall off","text":"In my days of fighting paranormal activities and beliefs (homeopathy, wells witchers, ...) we were usually told that we do not have open minds and do not accept anything outside of our limited science. For one, you need to have an open mind, but be careful to not your brain fall off ( [VIDEO] ) Then, we were very much interested in seeing these unusual things happening. As I mentioned it in a previous comment, I declared at the radio that I would immediately switch my PhD topic (in physics) to study them. Because, you know, Nobel prize. I did not get to see anything unusual, did a uneventful PhD and, well, do not have said Nobel prize. But the important thing was: I wanted to measure. To have an experiment where what was unusual would be measurable. This means that tests with well diggers were a complete failure when they were asked to find water in controlled conditions. OTOH, they were good when in the wild because (probably) they could read the landscape and see signs of water (conscientiously or not). Same with homeopathy: when I have a headache I get an aspirin and before it had time to reach my stomach I feel better. I also once swam away from what I thought were sharks and I probably broke a swimming record because my arms were moving like a blender. This is to say that scientists do not limi themselves - they just want to see and measure something to say that this is a thing. Unfortunately as soon as they do it the whole paranormal things fall apart. But we are still "},{"id":6773188,"author":"duncan_bayne","month":"2013-11","story":"10 Reason Why Eminem's Music Can Help Your StartUp","stance":"reference","why":"Tim Minchin song about open minds and brains falling out.","quote":"Tim Minchin had something to say about this","text":"Tim Minchin had something to say about this (\"if you open your mind too much your brains will fall out\"): [VIDEO] I doubt I'll change your mind in one post, but: Eminem's music, Eminem's culture is the exact opposite of the entrepreneurial enlightenment culture that breeds success."}]},"SRU3NemA95k":{"wy":"Mentioned as a reminder of the original Surface table, cool but too expensive.","m":[{"id":45757235,"author":"mrighele","month":"2025-10","story":"Board: New game console recognizes physical pieces, with an open SDK","stance":"reference","why":"Original Surface coffee table was cool but too expensive.","quote":"Reminds of Microsoft Surface, the original coffee table","text":"Reminds of Microsoft Surface, the original coffee table [1]. It was quite cool at the time, but too expensive for what could have been useful application. Hopefully for them, this will have more luck. On a side not, the website is completely blank on Firefox. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13208344,"author":"DavidWanjiru","month":"2016-12","story":"Microsoft’s Surface Hub is now apparently a billion-dollar business","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"No, not this one. The one I'm talking about had people talking about the product and how you could use it. The part with the song I'm talking about was a food restaurant setting (as opposed to a bar setting) Edit: The one I have in mind was more like this one in terms of setting - [VIDEO]"},{"id":10213261,"author":"facorreia","month":"2015-09","story":"A Screen Big Enough for Us","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Like the Microsoft Surface multimedia coffee table","text":"> If we decided to build a touch screen device several times the size of the iPad Pro, it wouldn’t just be a new device, or even a new device category. It would be a completely new kind of experience with no comparison in the history of computing. Like the Microsoft Surface multimedia coffee table: [VIDEO]"}]},"UOYi4NzxlhE":{"wy":"Cited as a reference scene when commenters see signs of impending economic or institutional trouble.","q":{"x":"These issues remind of this scene in Margin Call where two character talk about the impending 2008 crisis","i":37362916,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":46149823,"author":"paganel","month":"2025-12","story":"Microsoft drops AI sales targets in half after salespeople miss their quotas","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Margin Call , an excellent movie on the GFC.","text":"> Pure greed would have a strong incentive to understand what the market is actually demanding in order to maximize profits. Not necessarily, just look at this clip [1] from Margin Call , an excellent movie on the GFC. As Jeremy Irons is saying in that clip, the market (as usually understood in classical economy, with producers making things for clients/customers to purchase) is of no importance to today's market economy, almost all that matters, at the hundreds of billions - multi-trillion dollars-levels, is for your company \"to play the music\" as best as the other (necessarily very big) market participants, \"nothing more, nothing less\" (again, to quote Irons in that movie). There's nothing in it about \"making what people/customers want\" and all that, which is regarded as accessory, that is if it is taken into consideration at all. As another poster is mentioning in this thread, this is all the direct result of the financialization of much of the Western economy, this is how things work at this level, given these (financiliazed) inputs. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37362916,"author":"anshumankmr","month":"2023-09","story":"Groundwater depletion in India may triple due to climate warming, study finds","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"These issues remind of this scene in Margin Call where two character talk about the impending 2008 crisis","text":"I live in Indiranagar and this is a sad reality of life here. These issues remind of this scene in Margin Call where two character talk about the impending 2008 crisis [VIDEO] . There are many major issues in India's growth story, and I feel the music is gonna stop really soon."},{"id":34519553,"author":"jacknews","month":"2023-01","story":"Man worth $7.48B says Google should cut 28,000 more people after layoffs","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Indeed. [VIDEO] It's a useful skill, but not worth 7.48B IMHO. They say hate the game, not the player. But then, the players have chosen this game, and often don't want it to change."}]},"VBUUx0jUKxc":{"wy":"HN links it as stark evidence in discussions of police accountability and use of force, with a warning about its content.","q":{"x":"If you haven't seen the video [0], you really should before commenting.","i":20425234,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":23577316,"author":"malandrew","month":"2020-06","story":"Want to Solve Venture Capital's Diversity Problem? Start with Pension Funds","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"videos of Daniel Leetin Shaver","text":"> There's plenty of videos of white people being killed by police that you all just ignore. Two perfect examples are the videos of Daniel Leetin Shaver ( [VIDEO] ) and Tony Timpa ( [LINK] ). Ending qualified immunity, no-knock warrants, civil asset forfeiture, over-criminalization, mandatory minimums and militarization of police are not race specific issues. They affect all Americans."},{"id":23589548,"author":"kerkeslager","month":"2020-06","story":"Freedom of Speech: A Right for Everybody, or Only for Like-Minded People?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> You can't logically defeat bigots in a way that actually matters to them. The goal of public debate is never to persuade the person you're debating, it's to persuade those watching the debate. Sometimes you can persuade the person you're talking to, but that take a lot more sophisticated understanding of the other person than you're going to achieve if you simply dismiss them as illogical. > If this were the case, then there wouldn't be bigots. That simply doesn't follow. Bigots do change their minds sometimes, slowly over time. > The existence of systemic racism is not controversial among experts and hasn't been for a long time. Yet people insist on arguing about it forever. They don't care that logical arguments dismantle their beliefs. It's not that they don't care, it's that they don't agree that they are logical. Keep in mind, also, that logic is only as good as the evidence you feed into it. Logic in a vacuum of evidence is completely useless. People don't always change, but they do sometimes change. Also, keep in mind that censorship isn't the only poor strategy the left is employing here. Sure, getting rid of censorship and just arguing with people won't fix things, but that's in part because the argumentation of the left is crap too. The cry of many people on the left these days is, \"Come to the left, we'll call you a racist!\" and they're surprised when this actually pushes people to the right . People think minds can't be changed because they've never actually lea"},{"id":46751363,"author":"int_19h","month":"2026-01","story":"Man shot and killed by federal agents in south Minneapolis this morning","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Extreme violence has been normalized among American LEOs for a long time now.","text":"Is it the first video of American law enforcement shooting someone that triggered this reaction? This is from 8 years ago: [VIDEO] . Extreme violence has been normalized among American LEOs for a long time now. Look up \"killology\" for some more on this. If you're in US, check if your local PD or sheriff's office signed up its agents for a Dave Grossman seminar or training course; you might be unpleasantly surprised. And now that ICE job ads are essentially an open invitation to come be violent for a \"righteous cause\", it's exactly those types of people that end up there in even larger concentrations. But make no mistake, none of this is new in any way other than the sheer scale of it."},{"id":33085353,"author":"int_19h","month":"2022-10","story":"LAPD Officer Killed During Training Exercise Was Investigating Cops About Rape","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Cops can do this","text":"Well, not quite. Cops can do this: [VIDEO] And get away with it as a \"justified\" killing. Any regular person in the same situation would have been convicted of murder."},{"id":32325104,"author":"int_19h","month":"2022-08","story":"FBI is hiding details about a raid on Americans’ safe deposit boxes","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"this is not a murder, somehow","text":"The American police is the most powerful gang in the country, not the least because they're largely immune to legal repercussions for the crimes they commit. For example, this is not a murder, somehow: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26036577,"author":"Uberphallus","month":"2021-02","story":"Zero arrests in 6 months of health care professionals replacing police officers","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"That's funny[0]. I'm not American, so don't pull the white guilt card on me. I've spent a total of 12 days in the US in my life, and I was pointed a gun by the police once. That's infinity percent more than in any other country I've lived visited or lived in, and I've traveled my fair share off the beaten path. When they saw their mistake, they spouted the generic excuse that I matched a description, something that wouldn't fly in any other first world country, but is somehow accepted in the US. Anyway, I'm white and I did nothing wrong, so I guess that officer just pointed that gun as joke with no intention to kill,[1] right? US police kills citizens at a rate seen only in developing countries. According to the wiki[2], US police kills 10 times as often per capita as the worst European offender of significant population (France), and ranks higher than places like Angola, Colombia or Rwanda, that have insanely higher crime rates. Let that sink in, and think whether the problem is race based or not. But don't worry, Biden won, the rates will remain the same but it will no longer be an issue worth being reported in the media ;) [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":23683385,"author":"kerkeslager","month":"2020-06","story":"Reddit shuts down subreddits including r/The_Donald and r/ChapoTrapHouse","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I find it's effective in getting people to take the complete lack of police accountability more seriously","text":"> How effective has \"confronting fascism with the truth\" ever been, when the fascist is not being upfront about it, but rather posting hundreds of subtly racist memes and dog whistles to reddit? I don't feel like I did this question justice with my previous answer. The most obvious example would be the George Floyd video, and in a larger sense, the extremely effective tool of filming police as they harass, plant evidence on, beat, and murder the people they're supposed to serve. In the current situation, I think the results of that video speak for themselves. In response to the pervasive narrative that focuses on looters and not on cops murdering black people (I linked to five minutes in, but the whole thing is worth a watch): [LINK] This tends to be persuasive in my experience. This doesn't address racism because it's a white guy, but I find it's effective in getting people to take the complete lack of police accountability more seriously: [VIDEO] That officer (Philip Brailsford) was acquitted in a trial where the prosecutor moved to strike the video from evidence, and then the officer was rehired by Mesa Police briefly, before being given early medical retirement based on a claim of PTSD from the murder, earning him a $2500/mo pension. Watch any of those videos that you haven't seen, and see how that feels, and realize that that's how they make people who were on the fence about these issues feel."},{"id":20425234,"author":"Sohcahtoa82","month":"2019-07","story":"Cop who famously murdered unarmed man in hotel has been awarded $2,500/mo","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"If you haven't seen the video [0], you really should before commenting.","text":"Who the hell are these juries that are allowing cops to get away with murder? As much as people like to complain about how corrupt the police are in how they cover up for each other, cases like this are decided by juries. EDIT: If you haven't seen the video [0], you really should before commenting. This cop had no interest in actually apprehending Daniel Shaver. If he did, he would've done a more standard procedure like telling him to lay on his stomach with his hands either forward or on his back. There was no reason to tell him to walk forward on his knees, legs cross behind him, and arms up. You try doing that. Maintaining balance is a bit difficult. Now imagine trying to do that with the police pointing a gun at you. This cop wanted to kill someone and create a story where he was the hero. For fuck's sake, the cop had \"YOU'RE FUCKED\" etched into the side of his gun! [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17329500,"author":"orf","month":"2018-06","story":"Judge says ‘literal but nonsensical’ Google translation isn’t consent for search","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Warning, this is terrible","text":"Or aggressively pulling his trousers up whilst lying in a hallway begging for his life. Warning, this is terrible: [VIDEO]"}]},"VTSCppeFzX4":{"wy":"HN uses it as a joke reference in threads about alcohol, cannabis and the Ballmer Peak, not as a recommendation.","q":{"x":"There's this great sketch about being slightly drunk","i":36428571,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":43761414,"author":"mrtksn","month":"2025-04","story":"Marijuana hospital visits linked to dementia diagnosis within 5 years – a study","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is about a specific condition but it shouldn't be a surprise to anyone really. I remember when there was a huge push on reddit and everywhere to decriminalize and later legalize marijuana and I was a supporter of the movement and still support it but only as a liberty principle. After getting into smoking it regularly in college I had the chance to meet long time users and without exception all of them had cognitive issues. One day I was late for a party and everyone was high when I arrived and I realized that I simply can't stand them because everything they said was the lowest IQ stuff I ever heard and they were pretending that its creative. Quit the thing and never looked back. Stick to the alcohol and drink within the culture of it and you will be fine. It is bad for your body even at small quantities but at least you keep your mind intact and alcohol actually enhances the creativity by lowering your guard and not interfering with your intelligence. The culture part is important, binge drinking can be fun at college at times but the culture I'm referring is the culture where alcohol is being consumed socially together with some food or at event. Avoid drinking alone and other high risk activities associated with drinking. I keep reading about how alcohol consumption is dropping, pubs are closing and this makes me sad because I see how young people lock themselves into apartments and smoke weed all day. I don't believe that anything good comes out of it, no wonder the"},{"id":40062927,"author":"Y_Y","month":"2024-04","story":"The Ballmer Peak: An Empirical Search","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"has long been known to certain elite segments of society","text":"Of course the finding from this paper of the optimality of slightly less than two drinks has long been known to certain elite segments of society[0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36428571,"author":"mrtksn","month":"2023-06","story":"Brains on Drugs: How tinkering with consciousness became a societal sin","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"There's this great sketch about being slightly drunk","text":"Alcohol is an interesting beast, certainly bad for your physical health but IMHO it's invaluable for your mental health in small doses. I'm not exactly a drug expert, my experience is limited to a few substances but nothing ever comes close to alcohol. It melts away all the fake constraints(the limits and problems which you only imagine to have) that cause stress and break your flow. All this in moderation of course, being completely drunk doesn't help. I will be send to oblivion for this but the worst is the weed, it lowers your IQ so much that you think you are so creative for each dumb idea(and you can't have any ideas with depth in that state). Alcohol is not like that, up until a point your mental capabilities(not the reflexes) are intact. There's this great sketch about being slightly drunk: [VIDEO] And this XKCD: [LINK]"},{"id":30021774,"author":"Mordisquitos","month":"2022-01","story":"Cannabis use produces persistent cognitive impairments: meta review","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"a very relevant and topical sketch from That Mitchell & Webb Look","text":"> Another thing that I have learned is that alcohol is an upper at low quantities (1 to 2 standard glasses of beer), it gets your heart rate up, etc. It is a downer at larger quantities (e.g. drunk people at 10 standard glasses of beer). If you don't drink much alcohol, then you are most likely experiencing it as an upper, it doesn't continue that way. > I like to drink 1 to 2 glasses as well because of what you said, but also because it's an upper. This part of your comment reminded me of a very relevant and topical sketch from That Mitchell & Webb Look : [VIDEO]"}]},"Xybzyv8qbOc":{"wy":"Cited in AOT discussions as evidence that Oracle was moving toward AOT in the JDK and as an explanation of the deoptimization concerns.","q":{"x":"It's explained in the talk","i":12617374,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":13217282,"author":"shagie","month":"2016-12","story":"Building a Production Server Swift App","stance":"reference","why":"See-also for JEP 295 AOT","quote":"","text":"> Compiles to binary which could bring benefits depending on your needs (compared to JS, Java, Scala..) While its not there _yet_, JEP 295: Ahead-of-Time Compilation ( [LINK] ) is active, it was just updated last week, is targeted and a priority 1 item. I have reasonable confidence that it will get there in the near future. See also: JVMLS 2015 - Java Goes AOT [VIDEO]"},{"id":12617334,"author":"mike_hearn","month":"2016-10","story":"Ahead-of-Time Compilation","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier talk on the AOT project","quote":"There was a talk about it last year","text":"There was a talk about it last year: [VIDEO] The project seems to have gone slower than I expected, perhaps because Chris Thalinger moved to Twitter."},{"id":12617374,"author":"mike_hearn","month":"2016-10","story":"Ahead-of-Time Compilation","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why AOT concerns deoptimization","quote":"It's explained in the talk","text":"It's explained in the talk \"Java goes AOT\": [VIDEO] Basically they thought it'd de-opt too much. I'm not totally sure it's the case but they'd be the experts on that."},{"id":11683464,"author":"sievebrain","month":"2016-05","story":"Scala Native","stance":"reference","why":"HotSpot team AOT talk mostly eliminating warmup time","quote":"The Hotspot team are adding AOT compilation to the JVM.","text":"Hi, What is the rationale for doing this, given that the Graal / JVM guys are already developing an AOT compiler which can compile Scala to native binaries? Is this motivated by performance? The desire to write C libraries in Scala? What? Given the high level, dynamic nature of Scala I am skeptical LLVM+Boehm GC will result in faster code. I expect it would result in slower code. EDIT: OK, I just saw the link to your talk below. I feel you should know a few things: 1) The Hotspot team are adding AOT compilation to the JVM. There is a talk on it here. This mostly eliminates warmup time: [VIDEO] You can't eliminate warmup time entirely because profile guided on-the-fly optimisation is actually quite powerful and an AOT compiler that doesn't have profile data to work with can't generate code that's as good. But they support tiered AOT, where the AOT compiled code does basic profiling of itself, and then it can still trigger profile-guided JIT compilation if you want to. 2) Interop with native is being heavily worked on in project Panama: another JVM upgrade project. With the current prototype you can feed it arbitrary header files that are parsed with clang, and it generates Java interfacing files that are efficiently compiled (no overhead). There's a Pointer<T> type and so on. 3) Stack allocation isn't so easy to do unless you are willing to pretty wildly violate the safety of the language. It's also been found to not help much when very adventurous JVM implementors tried it an"},{"id":11094856,"author":"the8472","month":"2016-02","story":"JavaCPP","stance":"reference","why":"Oracle's commercial AOT feature","quote":"","text":"Afaik IBM's JVM supports class data sharing, which effectively provides AOT behavior once they have been JITed. For oracle's VM they're working on a commercial feature [VIDEO] There also are things like nailgun which simply prestart a JVM and then delegate to the loaded JVM when you invoke some command. It may be a bit of a workaround, but if your use-case is to frequently start extremely shortlived programs that might be worth it."},{"id":10323885,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2015-10","story":"Windows 10 and .NET Native","stance":"reference","why":"Oracle's AOT stance shift shown at JVMLS 2015","quote":"","text":"There have been AOT compilers from third party JVM vendors almost since the begining. Used mostly by companies that didn't want to expose their bytecode. However Sun decided to bet the farm on JIT and was against having AOT compilation on the reference JDK. Meaning anyone that wanted a AOT toolchain for Java had to go shopping. Now, it appears Oracle is of a different opinion. \"Java Goes AOT\" at JVM Language Summit 2015 [VIDEO] Being used to memory safe languages with AOT compilation like Turbo Pascal, Modula-2, Modula-3, Oberon, Delphi, Ada among many others, I also would have prefer that Java and .NET had offered the same type of toolchain from the beginning. Now almost 20 years later, we need to teach young generations that their default implementations were just that, implementations. As they got used to the (wrong) idea that memory safe languages imply a VM."},{"id":10324125,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2015-10","story":"Windows 10 and .NET Native","stance":"reference","why":"Oracle planning AOT for JDK around Java 10","quote":"Oracle is planning to finally add it to the standard JDK around Java 10 timeframe","text":"Oracle is planning to finally add it to the standard JDK around Java 10 timeframe, but it might be a premium feature similar to the mission control licensing. [VIDEO]"}]},"ddbxFi3-UO4":{"wy":"Linked as the original organ version of the piece, with a visual score that makes the fugue easy to follow.","q":{"x":"Compare the Little Organ Fugue as originally intended","i":44108316,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":48946179,"author":"kortex","month":"2026-07","story":"EEG shows brain can simultaneous encode two speech streams","stance":"reference","why":"Piece used for overload trance meditation","quote":"Bach's Little Fugue in G minor was and is one of my favorites.","text":"One of the first ways I learned to meditate/access different states of mind was a technique described as \"overload trance\" (IIRC). The premise was, listen to a piece of music, focusing on one musical component, then while keeping that focus, add another element, and another, etc. It works best with pieces with a lot of ostinato (repetition/vamp) and Bach's Little Fugue in G minor was and is one of my favorites. Really fun to play too, though i get tripped up as soon as the feet get involved. Also, Utopia by Astral Projection (that whole album Trust in Trance 3 is great for this). Bonus points to mentally visualizing something in time with the music (I like orbs whirling around like atomic orbitals). You can really tie up much of your mental processing this way and I find it much easier than traditional zen meditation, trying to bring focus back to eg. breathing. Little fugue: [VIDEO] Trust in trance 3 playlist: [LINK]"},{"id":44108316,"author":"username223","month":"2025-05","story":"The Art of Fugue – Contrapunctus I (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Original organ version of Little Fugue","quote":"Compare the Little Organ Fugue as originally intended","text":"Bach in general is amazing in terms of surviving all sorts of transcription and mutilation. Compare the Little Organ Fugue as originally intended: [VIDEO] to the a cappella version by the Swingle Singers: [LINK] Some music is very much tied to a particular instrument, but Bach is more or less universal."},{"id":17924017,"author":"pfooti","month":"2018-09","story":"You can't play Bach on Facebook because Sony says they own his compositions","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Headline seems demonstrably untrue [0]. Better framing should be, \"contentID incorrectly identifies YouTube sample as being part of a totally legally copyrighted recording of a Bach work\". (Bracketing for now the rightness or copyright at all, I suppose) 0: [VIDEO]"}]},"f1ab6uxg908":{"wy":"Used by commenters as an example of coordinated media narratives and repeated talking points.","m":[{"id":31334151,"author":"moduspol","month":"2022-05","story":"The ACLU Has Lost Its Way","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It was two years of incessant media coverage, fully including open discussion of whether or not Trump or his family members would be indicted. If you expected anything less, you were not consuming mainstream media [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25367694,"author":"graycat","month":"2020-12","story":"YouTube to remove content that alleges widespread election fraud","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the gang up, pile, party talking point collection in","text":"Wow! Good to see that that is what you had in mind!!! I count such statements as personality characteristics, unpolished, maybe gauche behavior, not welcome in country club society unless are worth > $5 billion! I'm really sensitive to lying, but such statements don't even start to raise my fur. Gee, I was worried that you'd found some actual lies, e.g., like we have heard nearly daily (I won't say hourly) from the FBI about Page, Flynn, and Manafort and from Pelosi and Schiff about Trump. For the mainstream media, I gave up on them after the gang up, pile, party talking point collection in [VIDEO] That is from 1-2 years ago and fun to watch now. For what the next few weeks holds, I'm finding some interesting reading in [LINK] with In the Supreme Court of the United States STATE OF TEXAS, Plaintiff, v. COMMONWEALTH OF PENNSYLVANIA, STATE OF GEORGIA, STATE OF MICHIGAN, AND STATE OF WISCONSIN, Defendants. On Motion for Leave to File Bill of Complaint BRIEF OF STATE OF MISSOURI AND 16 OTHER STATES AS AMICI CURIAE IN SUPPORT OF PLAINTIFF’S MOTION FOR LEAVE TO FILE BILL OF COMPLAINT with Article II requires that each State “shall appoint” its Presidential electors “in such Manner as the Legislature thereof may direct.” U.S. CONST. art. II, § 1, cl. 4 (emphasis added); see also id. art. I, § 4, cl. 2 (providing that, in each State, the “Legislature thereof” shall establish “[t]he Times, Places and Manner of holding Elections for Senators and Representatives”). Wow! I'd missed that "},{"id":25368830,"author":"graycat","month":"2020-12","story":"YouTube to remove content that alleges widespread election fraud","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"On > \"Nobody loves the Bible more than I do\" okay at [LINK] is \"You know, when you hold up a Bible, and nobody loves the Bible more than I do, when you hold up a Bible, you don't then put it down and go around lying and doing a lot of things that are wrong,\" Trump scolded Cruz. So, the source is not the best, but, still, the quote seems accurate. Sorry, I don't call that a lie. It's not even as bad as \"This car was driven by a little old lady only to church on Sundays.\" The usual startup seed round pitch deck predictions are worse than the bible quote. If you want to find an actual lie, maybe you can. I'm surprised the media apparently has not tried. One place to start is what he has said about spending money to rebuild the US military. IIRC he has mentioned some $trillions. I'd like to see the INCREASES over what the DoD has been getting right along, and those might not be $trillions. I.e., a significant fraction of the DoD budget, maybe his $trillions, went for just on-going, routine expenses and not rebuilding. Even if I'm correct here, I still don't call that a 100% lie -- it's more like bending some definitions in budgeting and accounting. But, again, much of the mainstream media seems eager to find Trump lies, so if Trump was wrong here the media should have gotten some headlines about it. > The idea that Trump hasn't told a single lie in 5 years is so comically false that one doesn't even know where to begin. I'm still willing to hear, wanting to hear, waiting to hear "},{"id":42061992,"author":"graycat","month":"2024-11","story":"Trump wins presidency for second time","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here is evidence of biased, cooked up, gang up, pile on, organized mob attack from 2017","text":"Right. In the US, on politics and the issues, getting the information and \"evidence\" is a really big problem. I have and/or have seen good evidence for all that I mentioned, but such evidence is NOT wanted by or common in the media which means that I have no well written, comprehensive, single reference to give. Uh, YOU try: Write a document with good evidence, details, quotes, video clips, etc., and see how much interest the US MSM (mainstream media) has in publishing it!!! I predict you will regard your effort, no matter how carefully done, as a waste of time. E.g., so far I've never seen even one credible graph over, say, the last 16 years, of, say, the US CPI (consumer price index). Same for budget deficits, spending bills, balance of foreign exchange, Fed loans, spending on the war in Ukraine (was there actually ANY spending or did we, instead, actually just ship war supplies produced in the US?) -- the actual details are absurdly messy, sloppy, missing, etc. Clearly, bluntly the details do not SELL -- won't get a big audience. To give good evidence here would exceed by several times the 10,000 bytes or so limit that Hacker News seems to have on a single post. US media credibility? Here is evidence of biased, cooked up, gang up, pile on, organized mob attack from 2017: [VIDEO] With that example, there is less than zero credibility. So, for your \"evidence\", don't expect that from the US media. I wish, profoundly wish, have posted many times on social media, that the US ne"},{"id":40162192,"author":"graycat","month":"2024-04","story":"FCC votes to restore net neutrality rules","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"> (faster if Trump gets another term). I didn't keep track and don't have a good list, but a guess is that Trump did push through a lot of regulatory changes. If the media would publish a well documented list ...!! From all I've seen, I like Trump, but apparently a lot of people don't. I wonder where am I going wrong. Why do some people not like him? A guess is the now old collection of video clips from the MSM (mainstream media) still at [VIDEO] Sooo, recently I watched several videos (still at YouTube) of episodes of Trump's old TV show The Apprentice . (1) From the business world I've seen, this guy was definitely, uh, different ! In a way, tough to criticize since apparently he was very successful. (2) A surprise was the propensity of mess ups, in fighting of the apparently carefully selected candidates. When I think back, yup, I did see a lot of that but guessed it was incidental and would go away and wasn't too bad -- I was wrong, and Trump's TV show was closer to right. How Trump handled (2) was good to see, although maybe some of it was just \"TV\"."},{"id":40162755,"author":"graycat","month":"2024-04","story":"FCC votes to restore net neutrality rules","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"if anything, by now they are entertaining!","text":"> Are you being sarcastic? Did you miss the part where he waged an attempted coup against the US government to remain in power? I never understood that: I watched his speech. All I saw looked reasonable, appropriate, prudent. It seemed he was careful to advise no violence. That there was an \"attempted coup\" makes no sense to me. I watched his speech and saw nothing wrong. > I mean... he was just found by a court to have committed rape. I didn't and don't see that. But, if what you say is correct, then that would explain why some people don't like him. From your post, it looks like there is some deep bitterness about Trump. I don't see why, but okay. For one explanation there is that old collection of media video clips [VIDEO] Apparently the media was totally convinced that those clips would doom Trump; maybe those clips are why some people don't like him. Watch the clips -- if anything, by now they are entertaining! They have much of the largest of the MSM (mainstream media) doing a big gang up, pile on of \"bombshell\", \"done, no question about that\", etc. that never happened. Maybe in low level town and city politics nearly everyone interested in politics at all has some really strong reasons to like the Democrat Party. If my startup works, maybe I'll discover that the local Democrats will do good things for me but the Republicans won't. Hmm."},{"id":38220333,"author":"narrator","month":"2023-11","story":"How the GOP muzzled the quiet coalition that fought foreign propaganda","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"My favorite montage of videos from that time","text":"The same people who were behind the four years of Trump/Russia investigations that yielded nothing. My favorite montage of videos from that time: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28195314,"author":"narrator","month":"2021-08","story":"The Bullshit","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The hilarious thing about watching this is is the seemingly coordinated narrative everywhere","text":"I think one of my favorite cases of bullshit was the 2 years spent investigating Trump for Russian collusion, and nothing happened despite \"Anonymous sources say...\" stories every week in the New York Times. The hilarious thing about watching this is is the seemingly coordinated narrative everywhere with everyone using the same phrase like they all are just repeating \"the truth\" from some central source where they write the teleprompter scripts. \"The walls are closing in\" [VIDEO] That's what the bullshit is. The almost identical media headlines and catch phrases everywhere. The 100s of identical bot tweets. Now I just read Telegram channels and I see the enormous anti-lockdown protests taking place worldwide. There are literally hundreds of thousands of people in France right now protesting vaccine passports across the country and nobody has heard about it because of a hermetically sealed mainstream media blackout. I come to Hacker News when I want to see what the propagandized classes who read whatever \"news\" app came with their phone are believing. Everyone here is pretty smart, you're just in Plato's cave looking at the shadows on the walls. The bottom line is, if you see that all your news sources are repeating each other, it may be time to diversify your news sources."},{"id":25369836,"author":"graycat","month":"2020-12","story":"YouTube to remove content that alleges widespread election fraud","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here is an example of the media pushing the narrative with repeating lies","text":"You are attacking me personally and contributing nothing rational about the issues. So, you look like a troll. There was E. Bernays and his idea of a narrative as the basis of a propaganda campaign. Well, the mainstream media (MSM), e.g., ABC, AP, Boston Globe, CBS, CNN, LAT, MSNBC, NBC, NYT, Reuters, The Atlantic, WaPo, WSJ, seem to have signed up to push the Bernays narrative. In a few words, the narrative is \"Orange Man Bad\". No meaningful data, no reasonable rationality, just an attack. Next there is the Nazi Minister of Propaganda Dr. Josef Goebbels with his \"If you tell a lie often enough, then people will believe it. Eventually even you will come to believe it.\" So, the second part of the propaganda effort is to repeat the narrative lies over and over. Eventually a lot of people come to believe, or come to believe that others believe so that there must be something to it or that they must at least fit in. Here is an example of the media pushing the narrative with repeating lies: [VIDEO] I hope that right away you saw that you were watching propaganda and refused to watch again. The MSM is desperate and about to go out of business. Apparently they sold out cheap. Some people stand to get big money and power if Biden will actually do all he has proposed, e.g., sending US companies and jobs out of the country and bringing cheap products and labor into the country. Really the people to get the benefits from Biden are under 1% of the population -- essentially everyone else "}]},"iHfJRON3b-w":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it as a joke or as a prior example of inflating a company's valuation through a small share sale.","q":{"x":"Max Fosh already did something like this[0]","i":44880311,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44880311,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-08","story":"Let's get real about the one-person billion dollar company","stance":"reference","why":"Prior example of inflating company valuation via a small share sale.","quote":"Max Fosh already did something like this[0]","text":"The author is forgetting another scenario: a multibillionaire creates a company and values the business at $1bn. Though of course Max Fosh already did something like this[0] Which also makes me wonder, is this not a bet that can be filled simply by will? There's a lot of abstraction to money, so can't someone like Altman just find a one person startup and invest in them such that this happens? I think this makes the whole thing a lot trickier than it appears at face value. Personally, I wouldn't be all that shocked if something like this happened because it could probably end up being worth more in advertising than the the tens(?) of millions to invest in the company. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":48506339,"author":"whh","month":"2026-06","story":"SpaceX raises $75B in its IPO, making Elon Musk the first trillionaire","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Funny and related","text":"> \"at least on paper\" This fact regularly seems to be forgotten by a lot of media reporting. People see \"Musk is a trillionaire\" and assume he has anywhere near that amount of liquid cash, completely ignoring that the company itself actually just lost $4.28bn. Value is simply what the market is willing to pay for a share at the margin. As a founder, my own startup has a valuation, but that sure as hell doesn't make me a millionaire. Oxfam has this daft 2% wealth tax idea on any assets over £10m. If we actually started taxing people based on paper valuations, most startups founders would be bankrupt by Series A. Funny and related: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38571938,"author":"ta1243","month":"2023-12","story":"The wealth of the 25 richest families in the world soared 43% in the last year","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> It would be the same thing as me asking my friend to invest $1 in my business, giving them 1 share out of 10 billion shares, and then claiming my net worth was 10 billion dollars. Or selling one share for £50, be richer than Elon Musk, and then get accused of fraud [VIDEO]"}]},"iJunxkln578":{"wy":"Cited as a concrete example of a big renewable-plus-interconnector project in threads about nuclear costs, grid interconnects and submarine cables.","q":{"x":"This video also suggests that this sort of renewable project will have an average cost of 48 GBP/MWh versus 92.50 GBP/MWh for new nuclear.","i":28896821,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":31557180,"author":"patrickk","month":"2022-05","story":"Big money, nuclear subsidies, and systemic corruption (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a Morocco-UK solar/wind/battery project.","quote":"Morocco-UK solar/wind/battery project","text":"> In the long run I hope to see high capacity transcontinental HVDC lines linking continents so we can buy solar power from the other side of the Earth when it's night where we are, and sell our surplus when the sun is shining -- no batteries needed. That's hard and expensive. There are a bunch of projects in this space. I love going down this rabbit hole. The EU supergrid is already being built out, and doesn't require linking far flung continents since it links Norwegian hydro and North Sea wind power, among other sources. A mix of geographically distributed hydro, wind, solar, batteries and perhaps geothermal will negate the need for relying on solar + HVDC stretched around the globe along with the geopolitical risks. Some examples from past HN comments: [LINK] [LINK] Morocco-UK solar/wind/battery project: [VIDEO] Germany is aiming to get 100% of it's electricity from green sources by 2035, so expect a lot more projects in this space in future: [LINK]"},{"id":29230784,"author":"thrawn0r","month":"2021-11","story":"Chile wants to export solar energy to Asia via 15,000km submarine cable","stance":"reference","why":"Notes global submarine cable capacity is a bottleneck.","quote":"global supply capacity of these cables is estimated at 4000 KM/p.a","text":"\"global supply capacity of these cables is estimated at 4000 KM/p.a\" [VIDEO] seems like a bottleneck."},{"id":28896821,"author":"ximeng","month":"2021-10","story":"The booming business of knitting together the world’s electricity grids","stance":"reference","why":"UK-Morocco solar project: cable needs and cost versus nuclear.","quote":"This video also suggests that this sort of renewable project will have an average cost of 48 GBP/MWh versus 92.50 GBP/MWh for new nuclear.","text":"[VIDEO] more on the UK to Morocco solar / wind farm plan. 3,800km times four HVDC cables for redundancy. Global capacity around 4,000km of cable per year. So to make this project happen, need to open a new cable manufacturing facility. This video also suggests that this sort of renewable project will have an average cost of 48 GBP/MWh versus 92.50 GBP/MWh for new nuclear."},{"id":28902398,"author":"tuatoru","month":"2021-10","story":"Is nuclear power our best bet against climate change?","stance":"reference","why":"Overview of the Morocco-UK power project.","quote":"Morocco-UK Power Project","text":"Morocco-UK Power Project: [VIDEO] Australian - ASEAN Power Link: [LINK] Edit: oops, now it's called Australia - Asia Power Link."}]},"jnZ2NNYySuE":{"wy":"Used as the canonical example of arbitrary code execution through nothing but controller input, often in talks about speedrunning and exploits.","q":{"x":"(this second video is really good, he goes into detail of the process)","i":32000282,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":43233795,"author":"pdpi","month":"2025-03","story":"Speedrunners are vulnerability researchers, they just don't know it yet","stance":"reference","why":"Arbitrary code execution on Super Mario World as extreme example","quote":"arbitrary code execution on Super Mario World","text":"At its most extreme, this crossover gets you things like arbitrary code execution on Super Mario World. EDIT: There was supposed to be a link here. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32000282,"author":"SkeuomorphicBee","month":"2022-07","story":"Arbitrary Code Execution in Super Mario All-Stars [video]","stance":"reference","why":"TASBot injecting pong and snake into Super Mario World at AGDQ 2014","quote":"they inject a pong and a snake game using a TASBot","text":"Just to add some context, arbitrary code execution in Super Mario World is nothing new, it has been known since 2014. There was a tool assisted demonstration at Awesome Games Done Quick 2014 [1] where they inject a pong and a snake game using a TASBot (a robot controller that replays input scripts to the console). And in 2016 a streamer performed it manually to inject a flappy birds game [2] (this second video is really good, he goes into detail of the process). So, what this newer demonstration brings is the use of the known arbitrary code execution capabilities of \"Super Mario World\" to help finish all 5 games in \"Super Mario All-Stars + Super Mario World\" as quick as possible. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":20865349,"author":"eatitraw","month":"2019-09","story":"NLP's Clever Hans Moment Has Arrived","stance":"reference","why":"Injecting runnable code into Super Mario World","quote":"They also used the same technique to inject runnable code in the game","text":"> Evolved player makes invalid moves far away in the board, causing opponent players to run out of memory and crash Well, this sounds like speedrunning. People have found arbitrary code execution vulnerabilities in SNES and used them to jump to the credits (which counts as completed the game) in less than a minute: [LINK] They also used the same technique to inject runnable code in the game: [VIDEO]"}]},"oI2aMKwXXnE":{"wy":"Used as a reference when discussing inverted-steering analogies and how hard it is to rewire a learned skill.","q":{"x":"this guy took a more serious attempt at learning to ride the backwards","i":17692895,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":35806874,"author":"aurizon","month":"2023-05","story":"Poisoning Language Models During Instruction Tuning","stance":"reference","why":"Inverted-steering bike analogy.","quote":"geared stem that inverts steering","text":"This reminds me of the 'poisoned' clown bike, which has a geared stem that inverts steering, move bars in an arc to the right = bike goes left. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23018332,"author":"hamaluik","month":"2020-04","story":"Right-to-left-language-induced GUI bugs","stance":"reference","why":"Analogy for how hard RTL is to grasp.","quote":"It's like riding a bike with the handlebars backwards","text":"I had never really considered RTL design until I decided on a whim to support Arabic in an app [1], and before doing so I would have easily laughed at this. After doing so, I can completely understand it. It seems so simple—text just goes from right to left instead of left to right, but truly grokking that seems impossible for me. It's like riding a bike with the handlebars backwards [2]—it just doesn't work in your brain, as much as your higher-level thoughts seem to understand it. I was lucky that Flutter did almost all of the heavy lifting for me, but I spent more time than I'd like to admit ironing things out and I still don't think it's quite right. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17692895,"author":"jesusth1","month":"2018-08","story":"4D toys","stance":"reference","why":"Serious attempt at learning the backwards bike.","quote":"this guy took a more serious attempt at learning to ride the backwards","text":"Yeap, [VIDEO] this guy took a more serious attempt at learning to ride the backwards and it seems it didn't take him that much time."}]}});
